(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2a.9ae16c00da.js"]={"13":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cluster of findings is most characteristic of lower motor neuron weakness rather than upper motor neuron weakness?","choices":{"A":"Flaccid tone, atrophy, fasciculations, and hyporeflexia","B":"Spastic tone, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign","C":"Clasp-knife rigidity with clonus at the ankle joint","D":"Normal tone with weakness confined above the neck","E":"Increased tone with brisk, pathologically spread reflexes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lower motor neuron lesions destroy the final common pathway to muscle, producing flaccid tone, denervation atrophy, fasciculations from spontaneous denervated fiber firing, and hyporeflexia or areflexia. Spastic tone with hyperreflexia and a Babinski sign instead reflects loss of descending inhibition from an upper motor neuron lesion, clasp-knife rigidity with clonus is likewise a classic upper motor neuron finding, weakness confined above the neck with normal tone does not describe a limb LMN pattern at all, and increased tone with pathologically spread reflexes again describes upper motor neuron rather than lower motor neuron disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Flaccid, atrophic, fasciculating, and hyporeflexic muscle is the signature LMN combination, the direct mirror image of spastic UMN weakness. COMLEX: Fasciculations specifically point to active denervation of motor units and are a key bedside clue that favors an anterior horn cell or peripheral nerve process.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, the same UMN/LMN distinction extended here to sensory level, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"A sharp, horizontal boundary below which all motor, sensory, and autonomic function is lost is best described using which localizing term?","choices":{"A":"A dermatomal radicular pattern","B":"A stocking-glove polyneuropathy","C":"A single peripheral mononeuropathy","D":"A patchy plexopathy distribution","E":"A spinal cord sensory level"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A discrete horizontal band below which all sensory, motor, and autonomic function abruptly stops reflects transverse involvement of the entire cord cross-section at one segment, termed a sensory level, and is a hallmark of cord rather than root or nerve pathology. A dermatomal radicular pattern instead follows a single oblique band corresponding to one nerve root rather than a sharp transverse cutoff, a stocking-glove polyneuropathy produces a gradual distal-to-proximal gradient rather than one abrupt boundary, a single mononeuropathy affects only the territory of one named nerve, and a plexopathy produces an irregular, multi-root distribution rather than a clean horizontal level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Identifying a sharp sensory level is the fastest way to confirm a lesion sits within the spinal cord itself rather than at a root, plexus, or peripheral nerve. COMLEX: Documenting the exact dermatome where sensation changes lets the examiner estimate the injured vertebral level at the bedside before imaging is obtained.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, the same UMN/LMN distinction extended here to sensory level, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"Numbness confined strictly to the little finger and the ulnar half of the ring finger, sparing the rest of the hand, is most consistent with which pattern?","choices":{"A":"A C8 dermatomal radiculopathy pattern","B":"A mononeuropathy of the ulnar nerve","C":"A diffuse length-dependent polyneuropathy","D":"A central cord syndrome sensory pattern","E":"A dorsal column tract sensory pattern"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sensory loss restricted to the little finger and the ulnar half of the ring finger, without involvement of the rest of the hand or forearm, follows the specific cutaneous territory of the ulnar nerve, marking this a peripheral mononeuropathy rather than a root or central process. A C8 radiculopathy would typically also involve the medial forearm rather than stopping cleanly at the wrist, a length-dependent polyneuropathy would present symmetrically in both hands and feet rather than in one nerve's isolated territory, central cord syndrome produces a bilateral, arm-predominant pattern rather than a single-nerve distribution, and a dorsal column lesion produces loss of vibration and proprioception rather than this discrete cutaneous sensory band.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve mononeuropathy, classically from compression at the elbow, is a frequently tested contrast to a C8 or T1 radiculopathy that instead extends into the medial forearm. COMLEX: A positive Tinel sign over the cubital tunnel at the elbow supports ulnar entrapment over a more proximal root lesion.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, the same UMN/LMN distinction extended here to sensory level, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"The paired posterior spinal arteries, unlike the single anterior spinal artery, form which distinctive vascular arrangement along the dorsal cord surface?","choices":{"A":"A single midline trunk with almost no collateral supply","B":"An arrangement fed only by the artery of Adamkiewicz","C":"A closed system with no communication across the midline","D":"A richly anastomotic plexus with extensive collateral flow","E":"A vessel pair confined only to the lumbosacral segments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The paired posterior spinal arteries run along the dorsal cord as a richly interconnected anastomotic plexus with abundant collateral channels, which is why isolated posterior spinal artery infarction is far less common and often less devastating than anterior spinal artery occlusion. Describing them as a single midline trunk with minimal collateral flow instead describes the anterior spinal artery's more precarious watershed-dependent supply, the artery of Adamkiewicz reinforces the anterior rather than posterior circulation, a closed system without midline communication contradicts their extensively anastomotic nature, and confining them to only the lumbosacral segments ignores their full rostrocaudal extent along the entire cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The posterior spinal arteries' rich anastomotic network explains why posterior cord infarction is rare, while the anterior spinal artery's more precarious single-trunk supply explains why anterior spinal artery syndrome is the classic vascular cord emergency. COMLEX: This anatomic contrast is the basis for why vibration and proprioception are so often spared even in severe cord ischemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing ischemic, lacunar, and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism and deficit pattern, the same territory-to-deficit logic applied here to spinal cord vasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which thoracic cord region is classically considered a vascular watershed zone that is most vulnerable to ischemia during systemic hypotension?","choices":{"A":"The conus medullaris at the L1-L2 level","B":"The cervical enlargement around C5 to C8","C":"The lumbosacral enlargement around L2 to S1","D":"The mid-thoracic cord around T4 to T8","E":"The upper cervical cord immediately below the medulla"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mid-thoracic cord, roughly T4 to T8, receives comparatively sparse segmental medullary artery reinforcement between the richer cervical and lumbar feeders, making it a relative vascular watershed zone that is disproportionately vulnerable to infarction during systemic hypotension or aortic cross-clamping. The cervical enlargement instead receives generous reinforcement from multiple cervical segmental arteries, the lumbosacral enlargement is dominantly supplied by the large artery of Adamkiewicz, the conus medullaris receives supply from anastomoses of the terminal anterior spinal artery and lumbar feeders rather than being the classic watershed segment, and the upper cervical cord is well perfused directly from the vertebral arteries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing the mid-thoracic cord as a watershed zone explains why thoracic-level infarcts are disproportionately common during profound or prolonged hypotension. COMLEX: This watershed vulnerability is a key reason perioperative blood pressure is monitored closely during any procedure near the descending thoracic aorta.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing ischemic, lacunar, and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism and deficit pattern, the same territory-to-deficit logic applied here to spinal cord vasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Segmental medullary arteries arising from radicular branches at multiple spinal levels serve which primary function for the anterior spinal artery?","choices":{"A":"Draining venous blood away from the anterior cord surface","B":"Reinforcing anterior spinal artery flow along its full length","C":"Supplying only the dorsal root ganglia at each level","D":"Forming the dural venous sinuses at each vertebral level","E":"Carrying CSF between adjacent spinal segments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the anterior spinal artery is a single, relatively thin longitudinal trunk, it depends on periodic reinforcement from segmental medullary arteries entering at multiple levels, the largest being the artery of Adamkiewicz, to maintain adequate perfusion pressure along its entire rostrocaudal length. These vessels are arterial feeders rather than venous drainage channels, they primarily reinforce the anterior spinal artery rather than isolated dorsal root ganglia, they are unrelated to dural venous sinus formation, which is a cranial structure, and they carry blood rather than cerebrospinal fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmental reinforcement of a single thin anterior spinal artery trunk explains why interruption of a major feeder, especially the artery of Adamkiewicz, can produce infarction over several cord segments rather than just one. COMLEX: This segmental dependency is why preoperative vascular mapping before aortic surgery specifically searches for the dominant feeding levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing ischemic, lacunar, and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism and deficit pattern, the same territory-to-deficit logic applied here to spinal cord vasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Testing the biceps reflex by tapping the biceps tendon at the elbow primarily assesses the integrity of which spinal segments?","choices":{"A":"The C7 and C8 segments","B":"The L3 and L4 segments","C":"The T1 and T2 segments","D":"The S1 and S2 segments","E":"The C5 and C6 segments"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The biceps reflex tests the musculocutaneous nerve and the C5-C6 spinal segments, making its absence a useful clue for cervical radiculopathy or plexus injury at that level. C7-C8 instead corresponds to the triceps and finger flexor reflexes rather than the biceps, L3-L4 corresponds to the patellar reflex, T1-T2 has no standard clinically tested deep tendon reflex, and S1-S2 corresponds to the Achilles reflex rather than an upper extremity reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Biceps at C5-C6 is the first rung of the upper extremity reflex ladder, biceps, brachioradialis, and triceps, tested sequentially from rostral to caudal cervical segments. COMLEX: A diminished biceps reflex paired with deltoid or shoulder-external-rotator weakness points toward a C5 rather than a C6 radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 Using reflex findings and long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here with named dermatomes, myotomes, and deep tendon reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"The brachioradialis reflex, elicited by tapping the distal radius just proximal to the wrist, localizes primarily to which spinal segment?","choices":{"A":"The C7 segment","B":"The C6 segment","C":"The C8 segment","D":"The T1 segment","E":"The L4 segment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The brachioradialis reflex tests the radial nerve and the C5-C6 segments, with C6 the root it most often localizes clinically, and it is classically paired with wrist extension and thumb and index finger sensation when localizing a C6 radiculopathy. C7 instead corresponds to the triceps reflex and elbow extension, C8 corresponds to finger flexion without a standard reflex of its own, T1 governs intrinsic hand muscle function rather than a reflex arc tested here, and L4 is a lower extremity segment corresponding to the patellar reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The brachioradialis reflex at C6, paired with thumb and index finger sensory changes, is a classic localizing triad for C6 radiculopathy from a C5-C6 disc herniation. COMLEX: An inverted radial reflex, finger flexion instead of the expected brachioradialis contraction, suggests a cervical myelopathy above the tested segment rather than an isolated root lesion.","crossRef":"📎 Using reflex findings and long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here with named dermatomes, myotomes, and deep tendon reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Absence of the Achilles tendon reflex on physical examination most directly localizes a lesion to which spinal segments?","choices":{"A":"The L3 and L4 segments","B":"The L1 and L2 segments","C":"The S1 and S2 segments","D":"The C7 and C8 segments","E":"The T11 and T12 segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Achilles, or ankle-jerk, reflex tests the tibial nerve and the S1-S2 spinal segments, so its absence localizes a lesion to that reflex arc, classically from an S1 radiculopathy. L3-L4 instead corresponds to the patellar reflex rather than the ankle jerk, L1-L2 has no standard tested deep tendon reflex, C7-C8 corresponds to the triceps and finger flexor reflexes in the upper extremity, and T11-T12 likewise has no routinely tested reflex arc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achilles at S1-S2 completes the standard lower extremity reflex sequence alongside the patellar reflex at L3-L4, and both are essential for rapid bedside root localization. COMLEX: A diminished Achilles reflex paired with weak plantar flexion strongly favors an S1 rather than an L5 radiculopathy, since L5 spares this reflex arc.","crossRef":"📎 Using reflex findings and long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here with named dermatomes, myotomes, and deep tendon reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":1,"stem":"A bilateral, cape-like loss of pain and temperature sensation over the shoulders and arms, with preserved vibration and proprioception, is the classic sensory pattern of which named cord condition?","choices":{"A":"Brown-Sequard syndrome","B":"Anterior cord syndrome","C":"Posterior cord syndrome","D":"Cervical syringomyelia","E":"Conus medullaris syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An expanding fluid-filled cavity within the central cord, syringomyelia, destroys the crossing anterolateral fibers near the central gray matter first, producing the classic bilateral cape distribution of pain and temperature loss while sparing the more peripherally located dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts until later. Brown-Sequard syndrome instead produces a lateralized, crossed deficit pattern rather than a bilateral cape distribution, anterior cord syndrome produces bilateral motor loss together with the sensory loss rather than a purely sensory cape pattern, posterior cord syndrome produces predominant proprioceptive loss rather than pain and temperature loss, and conus medullaris syndrome produces saddle anesthesia and early sphincter dysfunction rather than an upper-body cape pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cape distribution of dissociated sensory loss is the single most recognizable clue to syringomyelia and is frequently paired with Chiari malformation in vignette form. COMLEX: Patients with syringomyelia often report painless burns on the hands, since pain and temperature sensation is lost well before touch or proprioception.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways, the same crossing and tract logic that explains why each named cord syndrome produces its characteristic deficit pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":1,"stem":"Bilateral loss of motor function and pain and temperature sensation below the lesion, with preservation of vibration and proprioception, defines which named spinal cord syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Central cord syndrome","B":"Brown-Sequard syndrome","C":"Anterior cord syndrome","D":"Posterior cord syndrome","E":"Cauda equina syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anterior cord syndrome results from compromise of the anterior two-thirds of the cord, producing bilateral loss of motor function through the corticospinal tracts and bilateral loss of pain and temperature through the anterolateral system, while sparing the dorsal columns and their vibration and proprioceptive function since those are supplied by the separate posterior spinal arteries. Central cord syndrome instead produces arm-predominant rather than uniformly bilateral leg and arm findings, Brown-Sequard syndrome produces a lateralized, crossed pattern rather than symmetric bilateral loss, posterior cord syndrome produces predominant proprioceptive rather than motor and pain-temperature loss, and cauda equina syndrome involves lumbosacral roots below the cord rather than the cord itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bilateral motor and pain-temperature loss with sparing of vibration and proprioception defines anterior cord syndrome, the clinical manifestation of anterior spinal artery territory compromise. COMLEX: This same dissociated pattern is what makes anterior cord syndrome a frequently tested counterpart to Brown-Sequard and central cord syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways, the same crossing and tract logic that explains why each named cord syndrome produces its characteristic deficit pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with cauda equina syndrome, conus medullaris syndrome is more likely to present with which characteristic feature?","choices":{"A":"Symmetric findings with early, prominent bladder dysfunction","B":"Markedly asymmetric leg weakness with severe radicular pain","C":"Slowly progressive symptoms evolving over many months","D":"Complete sparing of bowel and bladder function throughout","E":"Prominent upper extremity weakness out of proportion to the legs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Conus medullaris syndrome, from a lesion at the cord's terminal segments around L1-L2, tends to produce more symmetric leg and perineal findings with early and prominent bladder and bowel dysfunction, since the compact sacral segments and their autonomic fibers are affected together. Cauda equina syndrome, by contrast, damages individual descending nerve roots, producing more asymmetric weakness and more severe radicular pain since only the involved roots are affected, a gradual course over many months is atypical for either acute syndrome, complete sparing of bowel and bladder function would be unusual for either presentation given their sacral involvement, and prominent upper extremity weakness is not a feature of either syndrome, since both are confined to lumbosacral structures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conus medullaris syndrome, more symmetric with early sphincter involvement, versus cauda equina syndrome, more asymmetric and radicular, is a classic paired board comparison. COMLEX: Both are surgical emergencies requiring urgent imaging and decompression, but recognizing which pattern is present helps predict the level of the causative lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways, the same crossing and tract logic that explains why each named cord syndrome produces its characteristic deficit pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man presents with progressive hand weakness and visible fasciculations of the forearm muscles, along with brisk reflexes and a positive Babinski sign in the same limb. He has no sensory complaints anywhere. Which combination of findings best explains this mixed presentation?","choices":{"A":"Simultaneous upper and lower motor neuron involvement without sensory loss","B":"Pure lower motor neuron disease with a coincidental UMN finding","C":"A single peripheral mononeuropathy mimicking a central process","D":"Combined dorsal column and anterolateral tract dysfunction","E":"An isolated neuromuscular junction disorder with variable fatigue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fasciculations and atrophy reflect lower motor neuron degeneration while hyperreflexia and a Babinski sign in the same limb reflect concurrent upper motor neuron involvement, and the combination of both patterns without any sensory loss is the classic hallmark of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, which selectively spares sensory pathways. Calling this pure lower motor neuron disease with a coincidental, unrelated UMN finding ignores that both patterns arise from the same underlying disease process, a single mononeuropathy could not produce both LMN fasciculations and UMN hyperreflexia together, dorsal column and anterolateral dysfunction would produce sensory rather than motor findings, and a neuromuscular junction disorder produces fatigable weakness without fasciculations, hyperreflexia, or a Babinski sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined UMN and LMN signs without any sensory involvement is the classic diagnostic clue for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, distinguishing it from purely motor neuropathies or purely upper motor neuron processes. First Aid mnemonic for the disease-modifying drug: riLouzole, a nod to Lou Gehrig disease. COMLEX: The absence of sensory findings despite significant motor disability is what separates ALS from a mixed sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy on exam.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating UMN, LMN, NMJ, and myopathic weakness patterns, extended here to a disease that produces both UMN and LMN findings simultaneously in the same patient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man reports two weeks of sharp pain radiating from his neck down the lateral forearm into his thumb and index finger, worsened by turning his head toward the painful side. He has mild weakness of wrist extension and a diminished brachioradialis reflex. Which pattern and root level best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A C5 radiculopathy from foraminal compression","B":"A radial nerve mononeuropathy at the spiral groove","C":"A brachial plexus lower trunk traction injury","D":"A C6 radiculopathy from foraminal nerve root compression","E":"A C8 radiculopathy from a paracentral disc herniation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radicular pain down the lateral forearm into the thumb and index finger, worsened by neck rotation toward the painful side, a positive Spurling sign, together with weak wrist extension and a diminished brachioradialis reflex, localizes precisely to a C6 radiculopathy, most often from foraminal stenosis or a C5-C6 disc herniation compressing the exiting root. A C5 radiculopathy would instead primarily affect shoulder abduction and the biceps reflex rather than the brachioradialis reflex and thumb sensation, a radial mononeuropathy at the spiral groove would not produce neck-triggered radicular pain or a dermatomal sensory pattern, a lower trunk plexus injury would affect the medial forearm and hand rather than the thumb and index finger, and a C8 radiculopathy would affect finger flexion and the medial hand rather than this lateral, thumb-predominant pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C6 radiculopathy combining lateral forearm and thumb pain, weak wrist extension, and a diminished brachioradialis reflex is one of the most heavily tested cervical root patterns. COMLEX: A positive Spurling maneuver, neck extension and rotation toward the symptomatic side reproducing radicular pain, supports a foraminal root compression diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 Matching sensory deficits to radicular, peripheral nerve, and polyneuropathy patterns, applied here to localize cervical radicular pain to a specific nerve root","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man who frequently leans on his elbows at his desk develops numbness of the ring and little fingers along with weakness of finger abduction, but normal wrist extension and normal sensation over the thenar eminence. Which diagnosis and mechanism best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A C8 radiculopathy from a cervical disc herniation","B":"A lower brachial plexus trunk compression injury","C":"Ulnar neuropathy from chronic compression at the elbow","D":"Median neuropathy from carpal tunnel compression","E":"A T1 radiculopathy from thoracic outlet compression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Numbness of the ring and little fingers with weak finger abduction, normal wrist extension, and normal thenar sensation localizes to an isolated ulnar mononeuropathy, classically from chronic compression of the nerve at the cubital tunnel behind the medial epicondyle, exactly the mechanism suggested by habitual elbow leaning. A C8 radiculopathy would typically also involve the medial forearm rather than sparing it, a lower brachial plexus trunk injury would affect a broader combination of ulnar and medial antebrachial cutaneous territory rather than an isolated ulnar nerve pattern, median neuropathy at the wrist would instead cause thenar sensory loss and weakness rather than sparing it, and a T1 radiculopathy would present with more diffuse intrinsic hand weakness and possible Horner syndrome rather than this isolated ulnar pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar neuropathy at the elbow from repetitive leaning or prolonged flexion is a classic occupational and postural mononeuropathy tested against C8 radiculopathy, which extends further into the forearm. COMLEX: A positive Tinel sign over the cubital tunnel and reproducible symptoms with sustained elbow flexion support this diagnosis at the bedside.","crossRef":"📖 Matching sensory deficits to peripheral nerve versus radicular patterns, applied here to isolate an ulnar mononeuropathy from a cervical root lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man undergoes prolonged cardiac bypass surgery complicated by a period of severe intraoperative hypotension. He awakens with bilateral leg weakness and loss of pain and temperature sensation below approximately T6, but intact vibration and joint position sense. Spinal imaging shows no compressive lesion. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the artery of Adamkiewicz from a dislodged cardiac thrombus","B":"Acute spinal epidural hematoma from anticoagulation used during bypass surgery","C":"A compressive disc herniation that was simply missed on the initial imaging study","D":"Watershed ischemia of the mid-thoracic anterior spinal artery from hypotension","E":"Bilateral posterior spinal artery infarction that spares all descending motor pathways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mid-thoracic cord is a relative vascular watershed zone with sparser segmental arterial reinforcement, so a prolonged period of systemic hypotension during cardiac surgery, without any embolic or compressive event, can produce watershed infarction in the anterior spinal artery territory, causing the classic dissociated pattern of bilateral motor and pain-temperature loss with sparing of vibration and proprioception. An embolic Adamkiewicz occlusion would be a discrete arterial event rather than a global hypotensive one and is less consistent with the described hemodynamic mechanism, an epidural hematoma would appear as a compressive lesion on imaging, which is explicitly absent here, a missed disc herniation would likewise be expected to show a compressive abnormality on imaging, and posterior spinal artery infarction would spare pain and temperature while affecting vibration, the opposite of the pattern described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Watershed spinal cord ischemia from systemic hypotension, without any structural or embolic cause, is a distinct mechanism from the more commonly tested artery of Adamkiewicz sacrifice during aortic surgery. COMLEX: Maintaining adequate perfusion pressure throughout any prolonged cardiac or vascular procedure is a key preventive strategy for this watershed complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing ischemic stroke mechanisms and deficit patterns, applied here to a watershed rather than single-vessel spinal cord infarct","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman develops gradually progressive gait unsteadiness and loss of vibration sense in both legs over several days, with normal strength and fully intact pain and temperature sensation throughout. MRI shows a small area of restricted diffusion confined to the dorsal cord at the thoracic level. Which vascular territory is most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"The posterior spinal artery territory","B":"The anterior spinal artery territory","C":"The artery of Adamkiewicz territory","D":"The vertebral artery territory in the neck","E":"The anterior choroidal artery territory"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated infarction of the posterior spinal artery territory selectively damages the dorsal columns, producing loss of vibration and proprioception with a resulting sensory ataxic gait while sparing motor strength and pain-temperature sensation, which are supplied by the anterior two-thirds of the cord; this pattern is rare given the rich anastomotic supply to the posterior columns but can occur. Anterior spinal artery territory infarction would instead produce bilateral weakness and loss of pain and temperature while sparing vibration, the opposite pattern described here, the artery of Adamkiewicz is a feeder to the anterior spinal artery and its occlusion would produce the same anterior pattern, the vertebral artery in the neck supplies the cervical cord and posterior fossa rather than an isolated thoracic dorsal lesion, and the anterior choroidal artery is an intracranial vessel entirely unrelated to spinal cord perfusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated posterior spinal artery infarction is a rare but testable mirror image of anterior spinal artery syndrome, producing pure sensory ataxia rather than the far more common motor and pain-temperature pattern. COMLEX: The rarity of this presentation reflects the posterior spinal arteries' extensive collateral anastomotic network compared with the more precarious single anterior trunk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing vascular territories and their characteristic deficit patterns, contrasted here with the far more commonly tested anterior spinal artery syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man with a history of intravenous cocaine use presents with abrupt onset of bilateral leg paralysis, loss of pain and temperature sensation below T8, and urinary retention, occurring within minutes of using the drug. Vibration and proprioception remain intact. Which mechanism most likely produced this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A fibrocartilaginous embolism from disc material entering the venous system","B":"Cocaine-induced vasospasm producing anterior spinal artery ischemia","C":"Direct compressive epidural abscess formation from injection site infection","D":"An acute demyelinating transverse myelitis triggered by viral infection","E":"Mechanical trauma to the cauda equina from a fall during intoxication"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cocaine is a potent vasoconstrictor, and intravenous use has been reported to trigger abrupt vasospasm of the anterior spinal artery, producing sudden bilateral flaccid paralysis with loss of pain and temperature sensation but sparing of vibration and proprioception, the classic anterior spinal artery syndrome pattern, occurring within minutes of drug exposure. Fibrocartilaginous embolism is a distinct, unrelated mechanism typically following axial trauma or heavy lifting rather than drug use, an epidural abscess would develop over days with fever and would appear as a compressive lesion on imaging rather than presenting abruptly within minutes, transverse myelitis typically evolves over hours to days following a viral prodrome rather than instantaneously, and mechanical cauda equina trauma would produce asymmetric radicular findings rather than this symmetric, cord-level dissociated pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cocaine-induced vasospasm as an uncommon but recognized cause of acute anterior spinal artery syndrome broadens the classic aortic-surgery teaching point to a toxicologic trigger. COMLEX: Obtaining a thorough substance use history is essential whenever a young patient presents with an abrupt, non-traumatic dissociated cord syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing vascular occlusion mechanisms, extended here to a toxin-triggered vasospastic cause of anterior spinal artery syndrome rather than a surgical or embolic one","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man sustains a stab wound to the lower abdomen and reports a band of numbness across the inguinal crease bilaterally, along with new difficulty initiating urination. Using dermatomal landmarks, at approximately which spinal level should this sensory finding be localized?","choices":{"A":"The T10 level","B":"The T12 level","C":"The S3 level","D":"The L4 level","E":"The L1 level"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The inguinal crease and groin region is the classic surface landmark for the L1 dermatome, so a sensory band at this location, together with new bladder dysfunction, localizes the injury to approximately the L1 spinal level, near the transition between the thoracolumbar cord and the conus medullaris. T10 instead corresponds to the umbilicus, a more rostral landmark, T12 corresponds to a level just above the pubic region rather than the inguinal crease itself, S3 corresponds to perianal and saddle sensation rather than the groin, and L4 corresponds to the anterior knee and medial leg rather than the inguinal crease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The inguinal ligament as the L1 dermatomal landmark completes the classic sequence of nipple at T4, umbilicus at T10, and groin at L1 used for rapid sensory level estimation. COMLEX: A sensory level near L1 with new bladder dysfunction should raise concern for conus medullaris involvement given the close anatomic proximity of this landmark to the cord's termination.","crossRef":"📎 Using reflex findings and long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here with named dermatomes, myotomes, and deep tendon reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man reports weakness raising his right arm overhead along with a diminished biceps reflex and numbness over the lateral shoulder after a fall onto his shoulder. Which cervical nerve root is most likely compressed?","choices":{"A":"The C6 nerve root","B":"The C5 nerve root","C":"The C7 nerve root","D":"The C8 nerve root","E":"The T1 nerve root"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Weakness of shoulder abduction, sensory loss over the lateral shoulder, and a diminished biceps reflex together localize to the C5 nerve root, which supplies the deltoid and biceps muscles and the biceps reflex arc, most often compressed at the C4-C5 level. C6 would instead primarily affect wrist extension, the brachioradialis reflex, and thumb sensation rather than shoulder abduction, C7 governs elbow extension and the triceps reflex, C8 governs finger flexion, and T1 governs intrinsic hand muscle function, none of which match this shoulder-predominant pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C5 radiculopathy producing deltoid weakness, lateral shoulder numbness, and a diminished biceps reflex is a classic upper cervical root pattern often confused with a primary shoulder injury after trauma. COMLEX: Distinguishing a C5 radiculopathy from an axillary nerve injury, which also weakens the deltoid, requires checking the biceps reflex and forearm sensation, both spared in an isolated axillary nerve lesion.","crossRef":"📎 Using reflex findings and long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here with named dermatomes, myotomes, and deep tendon reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman reports difficulty pushing off with her right foot while walking, along with numbness over the lateral foot and heel and an absent ankle reflex on that side. Which nerve root deficit best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"An S1 radiculopathy affecting plantar flexion and ankle reflex","B":"An L5 radiculopathy affecting dorsiflexion and the medial foot","C":"An L4 radiculopathy affecting the patellar reflex primarily","D":"An L3 radiculopathy affecting knee extension and anterior thigh","E":"A common fibular mononeuropathy at the head of the fibula"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Difficulty pushing off reflects weak ankle plantar flexion, and numbness over the lateral foot and heel together with an absent ankle, or Achilles, reflex localizes precisely to an S1 radiculopathy, most often from an L5-S1 disc herniation. An L5 radiculopathy would instead impair dorsiflexion and cause numbness over the dorsum and medial foot while sparing the ankle reflex, an L4 radiculopathy would reduce the patellar rather than the Achilles reflex, an L3 radiculopathy would affect the anterior thigh and knee extension rather than the foot, and a common fibular mononeuropathy at the head of the fibula would weaken dorsiflexion and eversion rather than plantar flexion and would leave the ankle reflex intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S1 radiculopathy reducing the ankle reflex is the classic counterpart to L5 radiculopathy, which spares that reflex, and this pairing is one of the most frequently tested lower extremity root distinctions. COMLEX: Testing the ability to walk on the toes, which requires plantar flexion, versus walking on the heels, which requires dorsiflexion, quickly separates S1 from L5 involvement at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 Using reflex findings and long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here with named dermatomes, myotomes, and deep tendon reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with a known Chiari I malformation reports several months of painless burns on both hands, which he only noticed after the injuries had already occurred, along with mild weakness of intrinsic hand muscles. His vibration sense and gait remain entirely normal. Which cord process best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Anterior cord syndrome from vascular compromise","B":"Brown-Sequard syndrome from lateral cord compression","C":"Central cord syndrome from cervical hyperextension","D":"Posterior cord syndrome from dorsal column ischemia","E":"Syringomyelia from an expanding central cervical cavity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A slowly expanding central cervical syrinx, strongly associated with Chiari I malformation, destroys the crossing anterolateral fibers near the central canal first, producing painless injuries from unnoticed thermal and pain insensitivity in a cape distribution over the hands and arms, while sparing vibration sense and gait until the cavity later encroaches on the more peripheral corticospinal tracts and dorsal columns. Anterior cord syndrome would instead produce more acute bilateral motor loss alongside the sensory findings rather than this indolent, motor-sparing course, Brown-Sequard syndrome produces a lateralized rather than bilateral symmetric pattern, central cord syndrome from trauma has an acute post-injury onset rather than a gradually progressive course tied to a structural malformation, and posterior cord syndrome would instead impair vibration and gait rather than sparing them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Painless hand burns from unnoticed thermal injury is the classic real-world presenting clue for syringomyelia, reflecting selective early loss of pain and temperature sensation. COMLEX: Screening for an underlying Chiari I malformation with MRI is an essential next step whenever a cervical syrinx is identified or suspected.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus and anterolateral pathways, the same selective crossing-fiber vulnerability responsible for syringomyelia's dissociated sensory pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman sustains a burst fracture of a thoracic vertebra in a fall, with bone fragments retropulsed into the anterior spinal canal. She develops bilateral leg paralysis and loss of pain and temperature sensation below the injury, but preserved vibration and joint position sense. Which mechanism and named syndrome best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Central cord syndrome from hyperextension compressing the central gray","B":"Anterior cord syndrome from compressed anterior spinal artery flow","C":"Brown-Sequard syndrome from asymmetric lateral cord compression","D":"Posterior cord syndrome from compression of the dorsal columns","E":"Cauda equina syndrome from compression of lumbosacral nerve roots"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retropulsed bone fragments compressing the anterior portion of the canal compromise the anterior spinal artery territory, producing bilateral motor loss through the corticospinal tracts and bilateral pain and temperature loss through the anterolateral system, while sparing the dorsal columns supplied by the separate posterior spinal arteries, the defining pattern of anterior cord syndrome. Central cord syndrome instead follows a hyperextension mechanism and produces arm-predominant rather than bilateral leg-predominant paralysis, Brown-Sequard syndrome requires an asymmetric, lateralized injury rather than this symmetric bilateral pattern, posterior cord syndrome would impair vibration and proprioception rather than sparing them, and cauda equina syndrome involves lumbosacral roots below the cord's termination rather than a thoracic-level cord compression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior cord syndrome from a burst fracture with retropulsed bone fragments is a classic traumatic mechanism distinct from the aortic-surgery vascular cause of anterior spinal artery syndrome. COMLEX: Urgent surgical decompression is often indicated when retropulsed fragments are actively compressing the anterior spinal cord and its vascular supply.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — comparing the corticospinal and anterolateral pathways, the same anterior two-thirds territory whose bilateral compromise defines anterior cord syndrome here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man develops sudden low back pain followed by symmetric mild weakness of both legs, saddle anesthesia, and early urinary retention after a large central disc herniation at L1. Straight leg raise testing produces minimal radicular pain. Which syndrome best fits this presentation, and how does it differ from a more caudal lumbar disc herniation?","choices":{"A":"Cauda equina syndrome, with the same symmetric pattern expected from any level of lumbar disc herniation","B":"Brown-Sequard syndrome, since central disc material classically compresses only one lateral half of the canal","C":"Conus medullaris syndrome, with symmetric findings and earlier sphincter loss than a root-level herniation","D":"Central cord syndrome, since central compression always implies central gray matter cervical-type injury","E":"A simple L5 radiculopathy, since straight leg raise testing is the definitive test for excluding cord-level pathology"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A central disc herniation at L1 compresses the compact terminal segments of the cord itself, the conus medullaris, producing more symmetric leg weakness, saddle anesthesia, and early bladder dysfunction with relatively little radicular pain, in contrast to a more caudal lumbar herniation compressing individual cauda equina roots, which instead produces asymmetric weakness and prominent radicular pain. Calling this cauda equina syndrome ignores that root-level compression at a lower lumbar level would produce a more asymmetric, root-based pattern rather than the symmetric conus pattern described, Brown-Sequard syndrome requires a laterally asymmetric injury rather than a midline central compression, central cord syndrome is a cervical-specific entity from hyperextension trauma rather than a lumbar disc process, and a straight leg raise test screens for lumbosacral root tension rather than definitively excluding cord-level pathology, which this vignette's minimal radicular pain actually argues against.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conus medullaris syndrome from a high lumbar central disc herniation, with symmetric findings and early sphincter involvement, is a classic contrast to the more asymmetric, radicular cauda equina syndrome from a lower-level herniation. COMLEX: Emergent MRI and surgical evaluation are warranted for either syndrome given the shared risk of permanent bowel, bladder, and sexual dysfunction if decompression is delayed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — comparing the major spinal cord pathways, the compact bundling of these tracts at the conus explaining why its lesions produce more symmetric findings than root-level cauda equina compression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q01","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man undergoes emergency repair of a thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm complicated by a 25-minute period of severe intraoperative hypotension. In the recovery unit he is noted to have flaccid paralysis of both legs and loss of pain and temperature sensation below T10, with preserved vibration and joint position sense in both legs. This patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___","choices":{"A":"posterior spinal artery","B":"vertebral artery","C":"artery of Adamkiewicz","D":"posterior inferior cerebellar artery","E":"basilar artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thoracoabdominal aortic surgery places the great radicular artery of Adamkiewicz, the dominant segmental feeder to the lower anterior spinal artery, at risk during aortic cross-clamping and hypotension. Its territory supplies the anterior two-thirds of the cord, so occlusion spares the dorsal columns, which the posterior spinal arteries supply, but sacrifices the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts. The posterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, PICA, and basilar artery are all real cerebrospinal vessels but do not perfuse the thoracolumbar anterior cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: preserved vibration/proprioception with bilateral flaccid leg weakness and a pain/temperature level after aortic surgery is the classic anterior spinal artery syndrome vignette; the artery of Adamkiewicz is the vessel name boards expect. COMLEX: counsel patients before elective aortic repair that this is a recognized, feared complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — spinal cord blood supply and anterior spinal artery syndrome, tested here as a board-style vessel-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q02","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman reports three weeks of difficulty pushing off with her right foot while walking. On exam she has weak plantarflexion and weak hip extension on the right, an absent right ankle jerk, and diminished sensation over the lateral foot and heel. Straight leg raise reproduces posterior thigh and calf pain. This patient's findings localize to which nerve root?","choices":{"A":"S1","B":"L3","C":"L4","D":"L5","E":"S2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Weak plantarflexion and hip extension, an absent ankle reflex, and lateral foot/heel sensory loss form the S1 radiculopathy pattern, since the Achilles reflex arc runs through S1-S2 and the S1 dermatome covers the lateral foot and sole. L5 radiculopathy instead causes weak dorsiflexion and great toe extension with dorsal foot numbness and a normal ankle reflex. L3 and L4 localize to the knee jerk and anterior thigh/medial leg, and S2 alone would not typically abolish the ankle reflex or impair plantarflexion this prominently.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair each reflex with its root level (biceps C5-C6, brachioradialis C6, triceps C7, patellar L3-L4, Achilles S1-S2) and use it to separate L5 from S1 disc disease. COMLEX: this distinction guides which myotomes to test and which levels to consider for a lumbar HVLA or muscle energy technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 7 — dermatome, myotome, and reflex localization applied to a single-root vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q03","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Chiari I malformation presents with several months of painless burns on both hands that he noticed only after the injury had occurred. Exam shows bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation over the shoulders and upper arms in a shawl-like distribution, with normal vibration, proprioception, and light touch throughout, and mild intrinsic hand weakness. This patient's condition is most likely caused by an injury to the ___","choices":{"A":"dorsal column pathway","B":"lateral corticospinal tract","C":"dorsal root ganglion","D":"posterior horn, gray matter","E":"anterior white commissure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A syrinx expands the central canal and first interrupts decussating spinothalamic fibers as they cross in the anterior white commissure, producing bilateral, cape-like loss of pain and temperature with preserved touch and proprioception, since the dorsal columns run uncrossed and lateral to the expanding cavity. Further expansion later reaches anterior horn cells, explaining the mild intrinsic hand weakness. The dorsal column, lateral corticospinal tract, dorsal root ganglion, and posterior horn are plausible cord structures but none explains a bilateral, suspended pain/temperature deficit with sparing of touch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 'painless burn' plus cape-like bilateral pain/temperature loss with preserved touch is the syringomyelia trigger phrase; anchor it to the anterior white commissure crossing point, not the central canal itself. COMLEX: Chiari I is a common comorbid finding and a frequent distractor toward a craniocervical rather than cord-level explanation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 8 — major spinal cord syndromes applied to a board-style vignette requiring derivation, not recall, of the diagnosis","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man develops foot drop after habitually crossing his legs. Exam shows weak ankle dorsiflexion and eversion with numbness over the dorsum of the foot; reflexes are normal. Which additional finding would favor an L5 radiculopathy over an isolated fibular (peroneal) mononeuropathy?","choices":{"A":"Weak ankle dorsiflexion","B":"Weak foot inversion","C":"Weak great toe extension","D":"Numbness over the dorsal foot","E":"Tenderness over the fibular head"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Foot inversion is performed by tibialis posterior, a tibial-nerve-innervated but L5-rooted muscle, so it remains strong in an isolated fibular neuropathy, which spares the tibial nerve entirely, but becomes weak in an L5 radiculopathy, which affects every muscle supplied by that root regardless of which peripheral nerve carries it distally. Weak dorsiflexion, weak toe extension, and dorsal foot numbness are shared by both an L5 radiculopathy and a fibular neuropathy, since the fibular nerve carries the L5 fibers governing those functions, and tenderness or a Tinel sign at the fibular head would instead support the mononeuropathy over the root lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testing an L5-innervated but non-fibular muscle, such as tibialis posterior for inversion or gluteus medius for hip abduction, is the fastest bedside way to separate an L5 radiculopathy from an isolated fibular neuropathy. COMLEX: this same logic applies whenever a single myotome is shared across two different peripheral nerves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — UMN, LMN, sensory level, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns forced into direct discrimination within a single board-style vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man sustains a deep laceration behind the knee. Afterward he cannot flex his toes or plantarflex the ankle well, and sensation over the sole of the foot is reduced. Sensation and strength elsewhere in the leg, back, and perineum are normal, and he has no back pain. This deficit pattern is best described as a ___","choices":{"A":"S1 radiculopathy","B":"L5 radiculopathy","C":"deep fibular mononeuropathy","D":"tibial nerve mononeuropathy","E":"length-dependent polyneuropathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A mononeuropathy produces deficits in the distribution of a single anatomically defined peripheral nerve, and trauma is its most common cause; loss of flexion of the toes is the course's example of tibial nerve injury, which also explains the weak plantarflexion and reduced sensation on the sole after a wound behind the knee. An S1 radiculopathy usually causes shooting pain from the back along the posterior leg and a reduced ankle reflex. An L5 radiculopathy weakens dorsiflexion of the ankle and great toe. Deep fibular injury causes loss of dorsiflexion of the foot and toes, the opposite movement. A length-dependent polyneuropathy is bilateral and symmetric, not confined to one nerve after a local injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Course mononeuropathy examples: ulnar loses finger flexion-adduction, deep peroneal loses foot and toe dorsiflexion, median loses forearm pronation and finger movements, tibial loses toe flexion. COMLEX: A single-nerve deficit after local trauma needs prompt evaluation for nerve repair.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — UMN, LMN, sensory level, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns forced into direct discrimination within a single board-style vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman develops acute right leg weakness. Exam shows increased tone, brisk patellar and Achilles reflexes, sustained clonus at the ankle, and an extensor plantar response, but no visible atrophy or fasciculations despite the significant weakness. This combination of findings, despite marked weakness, most reliably indicates a lesion of the ___","choices":{"A":"upper motor neuron","B":"lower motor neuron","C":"neuromuscular junction","D":"muscle fiber itself","E":"peripheral sensory nerve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased tone, hyperreflexia, clonus, and an extensor plantar response reflect loss of descending inhibitory control over spinal reflex arcs, the signature of an upper motor neuron lesion, and the absence of atrophy or fasciculations argues against any process that directly denervates muscle, since those signs require destruction of the lower motor neuron itself. A lower motor neuron lesion would instead produce flaccid tone, hyporeflexia, and eventual atrophy with fasciculations, a neuromuscular junction disorder would produce fatigable weakness without any reflex or tone change, primary muscle disease would produce proximal weakness with normal or reduced reflexes rather than clonus and an extensor plantar response, and a peripheral sensory nerve lesion would not produce motor findings at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clonus and an extensor plantar (Babinski) response in the setting of significant weakness without atrophy is the most reliable bedside combination for localizing to the upper motor neuron and corticospinal tract. COMLEX: absence of fasciculations on a thorough exam is a key negative finding that helps exclude an anterior horn cell or peripheral process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — UMN, LMN, sensory level, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns forced into direct discrimination within a single board-style vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q07","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man develops sudden weakness of the left arm and leg with loss of pain and temperature sensation on the right side of the body below the neck. MRI shows a small infarct confined to the gray matter and adjacent white matter of the left half of the cord at C6, with the right half and the posterior columns spared. The vessel most likely occluded is a single ___","choices":{"A":"posterior spinal artery on the left","B":"artery of Adamkiewicz at T10","C":"left vertebral artery at its origin","D":"pial branch of the arterial vasocorona","E":"sulcal branch of the anterior spinal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most of the gray matter and adjacent white matter is served by the central, or sulcal, branches of the anterior spinal artery, and these branches alternate, one serving the left side of the cord and the next the right, so occlusion of a single central branch can infarct one half of the central cord. The posterior spinal arteries supply the posterior columns, which are spared. The artery of Adamkiewicz reinforces the lower thoracic and upper lumbar cord, not C6. Occlusion of a vertebral artery would compromise the whole anterior spinal artery origin or the medulla rather than a single hemicord segment. A pial vasocorona branch supplies the peripheral rim of the lateral and anterior funiculi rather than central gray matter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior spinal artery central (sulcal) branches alternate left and right and supply the gray matter; posterior spinal arteries and the vasocorona supply the posterior columns and outer rim. COMLEX: A unilateral cord infarct can mimic a Brown-Sequard pattern, so distinguish vascular from compressive causes with MRI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — spinal cord blood supply and anterior spinal artery syndrome, tested here as a board-style vessel-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q08","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old competitive gymnast develops sudden severe back pain while landing a dismount, followed within minutes by bilateral leg paralysis and loss of pain and temperature sensation below T10, with sparing of vibration and proprioception. MRI shows no compressive lesion, vascular malformation, or fracture. This presentation is most likely caused by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"epidural hematoma formation","B":"acute transverse myelitis","C":"fibrocartilaginous embolism","D":"spinal cord contusion injury","E":"cocaine-induced vasospasm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Axial trauma can force nucleus pulposus material from a disc into adjacent vertebral venous or arterial channels, producing a fibrocartilaginous embolus that lodges in the anterior spinal artery territory and causes sudden, non-compressive anterior cord infarction, exactly the abrupt-onset dissociated pattern described here with no structural lesion on imaging. Epidural hematoma would appear as a compressive mass on imaging, which is explicitly absent, transverse myelitis typically evolves over hours to days rather than minutes and often follows a viral prodrome, spinal cord contusion requires a demonstrable structural injury or fracture on imaging, and cocaine-induced vasospasm requires a toxicologic exposure rather than an axial-loading athletic mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibrocartilaginous embolism after axial trauma or heavy lifting, with a normal MRI aside from the cord infarct itself, is a classic non-compressive cause of sudden anterior spinal artery syndrome in young, otherwise healthy patients. COMLEX: absence of a structural or compressive lesion on imaging should prompt consideration of an embolic or vasospastic mechanism rather than assuming imaging was simply insensitive.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — spinal cord blood supply and anterior spinal artery syndrome, tested here as a board-style vessel-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man reports difficulty extending his right knee, an absent right patellar reflex, and numbness over the medial leg and ankle. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"L2","B":"L4","C":"L3","D":"L5","E":"S1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Weak knee extension, an absent patellar reflex, and sensory loss over the medial leg and ankle together localize precisely to the L4 nerve root, since the quadriceps and patellar reflex arc run through L3-L4 and the L4 dermatome specifically covers the medial leg and ankle rather than the anterior thigh. L2 and L3 would instead affect the anterior and medial thigh without reaching the ankle, L5 governs dorsiflexion and the dorsal foot with a normal patellar reflex, and S1 governs plantarflexion and the ankle jerk rather than the knee jerk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L4 pairing knee extension, the patellar reflex, and medial ankle sensation is a frequently tested lower extremity root level, distinguished from L3 by the addition of ankle-level sensory loss. COMLEX: testing sensation at the medial malleolus specifically, not just the anterior thigh, is what separates an L3 from an L4 lesion at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 7 — dermatome, myotome, and reflex localization applied to a single-root vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q10","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman reports weakness of finger flexion and intrinsic hand muscle weakness with numbness over the medial hand and forearm, along with a mild ipsilateral ptosis and miosis. This combination of findings localizes most precisely to which level?","choices":{"A":"T1","B":"C6","C":"C7","D":"C8","E":"C5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrinsic hand and finger flexor weakness with medial hand and forearm numbness reflects a lower brachial plexus or root pattern, and the accompanying ptosis and miosis occur because the T1 root carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers destined for the superior cervical ganglion, so their interruption at the root produces an ipsilateral partial Horner sign alongside the sensory and motor findings, localizing the lesion specifically to T1 rather than the adjacent C8 level. C8 alone would produce the finger flexion and medial hand findings without the sympathetic disruption, and C5, C6, and C7 govern more proximal, lateral upper extremity function unrelated to either the medial hand pattern or the sympathetic fibers described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a T1 radiculopathy combining intrinsic hand weakness with an ipsilateral Horner sign is a classic localizing pair, since T1 is the specific root carrying the sympathetic outflow to the eye. COMLEX: this same T1-Horner association is why a Pancoast tumor at the lung apex can produce combined hand weakness and Horner syndrome by compressing the lower brachial plexus and sympathetic chain together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 7 — dermatome, myotome, and reflex localization applied to a single-root vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 25-year-old man sustains a stab wound to the right side of the thoracic spinal cord. He develops right-sided weakness and loss of vibration and proprioception below the lesion, along with loss of pain and temperature sensation on the left side beginning a couple of segments below the lesion. This presentation is most likely caused by a lesion confined to the ___","choices":{"A":"central gray matter, bilaterally","B":"anterior two-thirds of the cord","C":"posterior columns, bilaterally","D":"right half of the spinal cord","E":"cauda equina roots, bilaterally"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A penetrating injury confined to one lateral half of the cord interrupts the ipsilateral corticospinal tract and dorsal columns, which have not yet crossed, producing ipsilateral weakness and vibration/proprioceptive loss, while also interrupting ascending spinothalamic fibers that already crossed a segment or two below, producing contralateral pain and temperature loss beginning slightly caudal to the lesion, together forming this crossed, lateralized pattern. Bilateral central gray matter injury would instead produce a bilateral cape-like pattern rather than a lateralized crossed one, bilateral anterior two-thirds injury would produce bilateral rather than lateralized motor and sensory loss, bilateral posterior column injury would spare pain/temperature entirely rather than producing crossed loss, and bilateral cauda equina injury would produce an asymmetric root-level pattern below the conus rather than this cord-level crossed syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a lateralized, crossed pattern, ipsilateral motor/vibration loss with contralateral pain/temperature loss a segment or two lower, is the signature of a hemicord lesion and should never be confused with the bilateral patterns of central, anterior, or posterior cord syndromes. COMLEX: penetrating trauma is the classic real-world mechanism for producing an isolated lateral hemicord injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 8 — major spinal cord syndromes applied to a board-style vignette requiring derivation, not recall, of the diagnosis","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl13_bloom_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with pernicious anemia develops progressive gait unsteadiness and a positive Romberg sign over several months. Strength and pain/temperature sensation are fully preserved throughout, but vibration and proprioception are lost in both legs. This pattern is most likely caused by involvement of the ___","choices":{"A":"anterolateral system, bilaterally","B":"dorsal columns, bilaterally","C":"lateral corticospinal tracts, bilaterally","D":"central gray matter, bilaterally","E":"anterior horn cells, bilaterally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin B12 deficiency classically produces subacute combined degeneration, damaging the dorsal columns first or most prominently, which explains isolated bilateral loss of vibration and proprioception with a positive Romberg sign and sensory ataxic gait, while strength and pain/temperature sensation, carried by separate pathways, remain intact early in the course. Bilateral anterolateral system involvement would instead impair pain and temperature sensation, which is preserved here, bilateral lateral corticospinal tract involvement would produce weakness and spasticity, which is also absent, bilateral central gray matter involvement would produce a cape-like dissociated sensory pattern rather than isolated dorsal column loss, and bilateral anterior horn cell involvement would produce lower motor neuron weakness rather than a purely sensory presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency selectively targeting the dorsal columns, and later the corticospinal tracts, is a classic combination of a metabolic cause with an anatomically predictable sensory pattern. COMLEX: checking a B12 level and methylmalonic acid is a key next step whenever isolated proprioceptive loss and a positive Romberg sign are found without other tract involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 8 — major spinal cord syndromes applied to a board-style vignette requiring derivation, not recall, of the diagnosis","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"14":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":1,"stem":"The medulla oblongata occupies which position within the overall organization of the brainstem?","choices":{"A":"The most rostral segment, just below the thalamus","B":"The middle segment, sandwiched between midbrain and pons","C":"A cerebellar structure lying dorsal to the fourth ventricle","D":"A diencephalic structure continuous with the hypothalamus","E":"The most caudal segment, between the spinal cord and the pons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The medulla oblongata, or myelencephalon, is the most caudal segment of the brainstem, extending from the foramen magnum and its continuity with the cervical spinal cord rostrally to the pons-medulla junction. Placing it as the most rostral brainstem segment beneath the thalamus instead describes the midbrain's relationship to the diencephalon, describing it as the middle segment between midbrain and pons instead describes the pons itself, calling it a cerebellar structure ignores that the cerebellum is a separate structure dorsal to the brainstem rather than part of it, and calling it diencephalic confuses it with the thalamus and hypothalamus, which lie far more rostrally.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medulla's caudal position, continuous with the cervical cord below and the pons above, is the anchor point for every brainstem cross-section question that follows. COMLEX: Recognizing the medulla as the most caudal brainstem segment is essential for localizing lower cranial nerve deficits, since only CN VI through XII arise at or below this level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — distinguishing central and peripheral nervous system organization, the same craniocaudal brainstem framework extended here to the medulla's position between the spinal cord and pons","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":1,"stem":"Despite making up only about 0.5 percent of total brain weight, the medulla is functionally critical for which reason?","choices":{"A":"Essentially all ascending sensory and descending motor pathways traverse it","B":"It is the sole site of primary motor cortex output onto skeletal muscle fibers","C":"It generates the entire cerebrospinal fluid supply for the whole brain and cord","D":"It is the only brainstem level that contains any cranial nerve nuclei whatsoever","E":"It houses the primary auditory and visual sensory cortices bilaterally in full"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Although modest in size, the medulla is a critical conduit because virtually every ascending sensory tract from the spinal cord and every descending motor tract to the spinal cord must pass through it, and it also houses the nuclei for six of the twelve cranial nerves along with autonomic centers governing heart rate and respiration. It is not itself the site of primary motor cortex output, which arises in the cerebral cortex, cerebrospinal fluid is produced by choroid plexus rather than the medulla generally, cranial nerve nuclei are also found in the midbrain and pons rather than exclusively in the medulla, and primary auditory and visual cortex are cortical structures unrelated to the medulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medulla's outsized clinical importance relative to its small size is a recurring board theme, since a small lesion here can interrupt nearly every long tract connecting brain to body. COMLEX: Medullary reticular formation cell groups regulating heart rate and respiration explain why medullary compression from herniation is rapidly fatal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — distinguishing central and peripheral nervous system organization, the same craniocaudal brainstem framework extended here to the medulla's position between the spinal cord and pons","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerves are associated with the medulla or the immediately adjacent pons-medulla junction?","choices":{"A":"Cranial nerves I through V exclusively","B":"Cranial nerves VI through X and XII","C":"Cranial nerves III and IV exclusively","D":"Cranial nerves II, III, and IV exclusively","E":"Cranial nerves XI exclusively, arising wholly within the medulla"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Six of the twelve cranial nerves, VI through X and XII, are associated with the medulla or the pons-medulla junction, reflecting the dense concentration of cranial nerve nuclei and rootlets at this level. Cranial nerves I through V instead correspond mostly to more rostral structures, the olfactory bulb, optic pathways, and midbrain and pontine trigeminal and oculomotor nuclei, cranial nerves III and IV arise from the midbrain rather than the medulla, cranial nerve II is the optic nerve and has no brainstem nucleus at all, and cranial nerve XI actually arises primarily from upper cervical spinal cord segments rather than from the medulla itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorizing VI through X and XII as medullary or pontomedullary cranial nerves is essential for predicting which nerve deficits accompany a given brainstem stroke. COMLEX: The true origin of the accessory nerve from upper cervical cord rather than the medulla is a frequently tested exception worth flagging early.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — distinguishing central and peripheral nervous system organization, the same craniocaudal brainstem framework extended here to the medulla's position between the spinal cord and pons","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":1,"stem":"The paired longitudinal ridges on the anterior surface of the medulla, flanking the anterior median fissure, are formed by which underlying structure?","choices":{"A":"The gracile and cuneate fasciculi of the posterior columns","B":"The restiform body carrying cerebellar afferents","C":"The inferior olivary nuclear complex","D":"The corticospinal fibers of the pyramids","E":"The spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pyramids are the paired anterior ridges formed by descending corticospinal fibers en route to the pyramidal decussation, and they flank the anterior median fissure on the ventral medullary surface. The gracile and cuneate fasciculi instead form posterior, not anterior, surface landmarks, the restiform body forms a posterolateral rather than anterior elevation, the inferior olivary complex underlies the olive lateral to the pyramid rather than the pyramid itself, and the spinal trigeminal tract lies in the lateral rather than anterior medulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pyramids as the anterior surface marker for the corticospinal tract are the anchor landmark for orienting any anterior medullary cross-section. COMLEX: Bilateral pyramid infarction from anterior spinal artery occlusion produces the striking picture of quadriparesis with preserved consciousness, since the reticular formation is spared.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — identifying the major components of the ventricular system and their surrounding neuroanatomic relationships, the same landmark-based approach applied here to medullary surface anatomy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":1,"stem":"The shallow groove between the pyramid and the olive, from which rootlets of the hypoglossal nerve emerge, is called which structure?","choices":{"A":"The preolivary sulcus","B":"The postolivary sulcus","C":"The anterior median fissure","D":"The sulcus limitans","E":"The posterior intermediate sulcus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The preolivary sulcus is the shallow anterior groove separating the pyramid medially from the olive laterally, and it is the exit point for hypoglossal nerve rootlets on their way to the hypoglossal canal. The postolivary sulcus instead lies posterior to the olive and is the exit point for cranial nerves IX and X rather than XII, the anterior median fissure lies at the very midline between the two pyramids rather than lateral to them, the sulcus limitans is an internal ventricular floor landmark separating motor from sensory nuclei rather than a surface groove, and the posterior intermediate sulcus is a posterior column landmark unrelated to the hypoglossal nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preolivary equals hypoglossal exit, postolivary equals glossopharyngeal and vagus exit, a paired mnemonic distinction that is heavily tested when predicting cranial nerve involvement in medial versus lateral medullary lesions. COMLEX: A tongue deviating toward the side of a lesion on protrusion localizes damage to the hypoglossal root as it exits through the preolivary sulcus or to its nucleus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — identifying the major components of the ventricular system and their surrounding neuroanatomic relationships, the same landmark-based approach applied here to medullary surface anatomy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":1,"stem":"The midline landmark marking the point where the central canal of the spinal cord opens into the fourth ventricle is known by which name?","choices":{"A":"The area postrema","B":"The medullary obex","C":"The rhomboid fossa","D":"The sulcus limitans","E":"The tuberculum cinereum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The obex is the midline point on the posterior medullary surface where the narrow central canal flares open into the wide fourth ventricle, and it serves as the anatomic dividing line between the caudal closed medulla and the rostral open medulla. The area postrema is a nearby chemoreceptive emetic center in the ventricular wall rather than the opening point itself, the rhomboid fossa is the broader diamond-shaped floor of the fourth ventricle rather than a single point landmark, the sulcus limitans is the internal groove separating motor and sensory nuclei rather than the caudal-rostral transition point, and the tuberculum cinereum is the lateral surface ridge overlying the spinal trigeminal tract, unrelated to the ventricular opening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The obex is the single most important reference point for describing whether a given medullary cross-section is caudal, closed, or rostral, open, to the fourth ventricle. COMLEX: Structures such as the area postrema sit just rostral to the obex and are clinically important because they lack a blood-brain barrier, permitting direct detection of circulating toxins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — identifying the major components of the ventricular system and their surrounding neuroanatomic relationships, the same landmark-based approach applied here to medullary surface anatomy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":1,"stem":"The caudal medulla is described as anatomically closed for which reason?","choices":{"A":"Its ventricular cavity is completely obliterated by ependymal scarring","B":"Its lateral apertures remain surgically sealed until the level of the pons","C":"Its cavity is a narrow continuation of the spinal cord's canal","D":"Its choroid plexus has not yet begun secreting any cerebrospinal fluid","E":"Its central canal is compressed fully shut by the overlying cerebellum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The caudal medulla is termed closed because its internal cavity is simply a narrow continuation of the spinal cord's central canal, retaining the tubular configuration seen throughout the cord rather than the flared, open ventricular shape seen more rostrally. The cavity is not obliterated by scarring, since it remains a patent, fluid-filled canal, the lateral apertures of Luschka are a fourth ventricle feature at the pons level rather than a caudal medullary one, choroid plexus secretion is unrelated to why the caudal canal is described as closed, and no cerebellar compression is responsible for the tubular caudal shape, which instead simply reflects direct continuity with the cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Closed caudal medulla equals central canal, open rostral medulla equals fourth ventricle floor, this single distinction is the fastest way to identify roughly where a given medullary cross-section sits. COMLEX: Recognizing whether an imaging slice shows a closed or open medullary configuration helps localize a lesion along the rostrocaudal axis before considering specific nuclei.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity and brain vesicles to the adult ventricular system, the same closed-to-open transition logic applied here to the medulla's central canal versus fourth ventricle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":1,"stem":"As the medullary cavity transitions from closed to open at the level of the obex, the central canal undergoes which structural change?","choices":{"A":"It narrows progressively further and terminates blindly at the obex","B":"It flares open to form the wide, V-shaped fourth ventricle floor","C":"It bifurcates abruptly into two separate parallel canals side by side","D":"It rotates a full ninety degrees to lie flat within the coronal plane","E":"It is replaced entirely by newly formed secretory choroid plexus tissue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At the obex, the previously narrow central canal flares laterally and posteriorly, opening into the broad, V-shaped rhomboid fossa that forms the floor of the fourth ventricle in the rostral, open medulla. The canal does not simply narrow and terminate, since it continues rostrally as the expanded ventricular space rather than closing off, it does not bifurcate into two parallel canals, it does not rotate into the coronal plane, and while choroid plexus is present within the fourth ventricle, the ventricular floor itself is neural tissue rather than being replaced by choroid plexus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Visualizing the central canal flaring open at the obex into the rhomboid fossa is the key mental image linking spinal cord central canal anatomy to fourth ventricle anatomy. COMLEX: This flaring accounts for why motor and sensory cranial nerve nuclei come to lie side by side along the ventricular floor rather than in a tubular arrangement as in the cord.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity and brain vesicles to the adult ventricular system, the same closed-to-open transition logic applied here to the medulla's central canal versus fourth ventricle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":1,"stem":"In the developing medulla, alar plate neuroblasts caudal to the obex form the gracile and cuneate nuclei. Rostral to the obex, some alar plate cells migrate ventromedially to form which structure?","choices":{"A":"The hypoglossal nucleus beside the midline","B":"The nucleus ambiguus in the lateral tegmentum","C":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus","D":"The medial and inferior vestibular nuclei","E":"The inferior olivary nuclear complex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Alar plate neuroblasts give rise to the sensory nuclei of the medulla: the vestibular and cochlear nuclei, the solitary nucleus, and the spinal trigeminal nucleus, plus the gracile and cuneate nuclei caudal to the obex. Rostral to the obex, some alar plate cells migrate ventromedially to form the inferior olivary complex, which produces the olive on the anterolateral surface. The hypoglossal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, and dorsal motor vagal nucleus come from the basal plate, not the alar plate. The vestibular nuclei are alar derivatives but stay in the dorsolateral floor of the fourth ventricle, lateral to the sulcus limitans, rather than migrating ventrally.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alar plate gives sensory nuclei (and, by migration, the inferior olive); basal plate gives motor nuclei (XII, dorsal motor X, ambiguus, inferior salivatory). COMLEX: The inferior olive's climbing fibers to the contralateral cerebellum explain why olivary lesions cause ataxia and palatal tremor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity and brain vesicles to the adult ventricular system, the same caudal-versus-rostral-to-obex logic applied here to alar plate derivatives","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the medulla, the medial lemniscus is characteristically positioned in which location relative to the pyramid?","choices":{"A":"In the posterolateral tegmentum near the restiform body","B":"Immediately anterior to the pyramid at the ventral surface","C":"As a paramedian vertical band directly posterior to the pyramid","D":"In the lateral tegmentum beside the spinal trigeminal tract","E":"Scattered diffusely throughout the entire medullary tegmentum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After crossing as internal arcuate fibers at the sensory decussation, the medial lemniscus reforms on the contralateral side as a paramedian, vertically oriented band lying directly posterior to the pyramid, a position it retains through most medullary levels. It does not lie in the posterolateral tegmentum near the restiform body, which is instead where cerebellar afferent and spinal trigeminal structures are found, it does not lie anterior to the pyramid at the ventral surface, since the pyramid itself occupies that position, it is not located in the lateral tegmentum near the spinal trigeminal tract, which is a separate and more laterally placed structure, and it does not scatter diffusely, since it maintains a discrete, compact bundle throughout its course.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial lemniscus directly behind the pyramid is a key paramedian pairing that explains why medial medullary syndrome damages both structures together, producing combined motor and dorsal column sensory loss. COMLEX: Testing vibration and proprioception alongside strength at the bedside reflects this shared paramedian vascular territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, and corticospinal pathways, the same tract organization now localized within cross-sections of the medulla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":1,"stem":"Throughout its course in the brainstem, the spinothalamic tract, part of the anterolateral system, occupies which characteristic location?","choices":{"A":"A peripheral, anterolateral position throughout the brainstem","B":"A deep paramedian position immediately adjacent to the medial lemniscus","C":"A superficial dorsal position directly beneath the fourth ventricle floor","D":"A position confined strictly to the region internal to the pyramid itself","E":"A position that migrates all the way to the midline at every medullary level"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The spinothalamic tract, as part of the anterolateral system, remains in a peripheral, anterolateral location throughout its course in the medulla and the rest of the brainstem, in contrast to the medial lemniscus, which occupies a deep paramedian position after crossing. It does not lie deep and paramedian next to the medial lemniscus, since that description instead fits the medial lemniscus itself, it is not superficial and dorsal beneath the ventricular floor, which instead houses cranial nerve nuclei, it is not confined internal to the pyramid, which houses only corticospinal fibers, and it does not migrate to the midline, since its peripheral anterolateral position is maintained consistently.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The spinothalamic tract's persistently peripheral anterolateral position, versus the medial lemniscus's deep paramedian position, is the anatomic basis for why lateral medullary strokes affect pain and temperature while sparing vibration and proprioception, and medial strokes show the opposite pattern. COMLEX: This dissociation between lateral and medial deficit patterns is a frequently tested contrast pair on brainstem localization questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, and corticospinal pathways, the same tract organization now localized within cross-sections of the medulla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":1,"stem":"Descending sympathetic fibers traveling toward the intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord pass through the medulla in which location?","choices":{"A":"The medial floor of the fourth ventricle near the hypoglossal nucleus","B":"Within the pyramid directly alongside the descending corticospinal fibers","C":"Within the restiform body alongside ascending cerebellar afferent fibers","D":"The lateral tegmentum, near the anterolateral system","E":"Within the medial lemniscus alongside crossed dorsal column fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The descending hypothalamospinal sympathetic fibers course through the lateral tegmentum of the medulla in close association with the anterolateral system, which is why lesions in this lateral territory, most classically from posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion, can produce an ipsilateral Horner syndrome alongside contralateral body pain and temperature loss. These fibers do not travel in the medial ventricular floor near the hypoglossal nucleus, which houses a motor cranial nerve nucleus rather than descending autonomic fibers, they are not within the pyramid, which carries only corticospinal fibers, they are not within the restiform body, which carries cerebellar afferents, and they are not within the medial lemniscus, which carries dorsal column sensory fibers rather than autonomic fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Descending sympathetic fibers riding alongside the anterolateral system in the lateral tegmentum is the anatomic link explaining why lateral medullary lesions can produce Horner syndrome together with dissociated pain and temperature loss. COMLEX: Recognizing ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis as a triad prompts a search for an accompanying lateral brainstem lesion rather than a purely peripheral cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, and corticospinal pathways, the same tract organization now localized within cross-sections of the medulla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman is found to have a small infarct on MRI at the level of the foramen magnum, at the junction between the spinal cord and the brainstem. A radiology resident asks which brainstem segment this level corresponds to. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The caudal medulla, since it begins directly at the foramen magnum","B":"The rostral pons, since brainstem levels begin above the foramen magnum","C":"The caudal midbrain, since the foramen magnum marks the tectal plate","D":"The cerebellar vermis, since the fourth ventricle opens at this level","E":"The upper cervical cord exclusively, with no brainstem involvement at all"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medulla begins precisely at the level of the foramen magnum, where it is directly continuous with the cervical spinal cord, making a lesion at this junction a caudal medullary lesion rather than a lesion of any other brainstem segment. Calling this the rostral pons is incorrect, since the pons lies well rostral to the medulla, calling it the caudal midbrain is incorrect for the same reason, and to a greater degree, calling it cerebellar vermis confuses a dorsal accessory structure with the medulla itself, and calling it purely cervical cord with no brainstem involvement ignores that the foramen magnum is specifically defined as the caudal boundary of the medulla, not a point entirely below it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The foramen magnum as the exact caudal boundary of the medulla is a precise anatomic fact frequently tested when interpreting imaging near the craniocervical junction. COMLEX: Structural abnormalities at the craniocervical junction, such as Chiari malformation, are clinically significant precisely because they compress the medulla at this transition point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — distinguishing central and peripheral nervous system organization, the same craniocaudal framework used here to pinpoint the medulla's exact caudal boundary at the foramen magnum","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man sustains a small ischemic stroke localized to the medulla and subsequently develops hoarseness, dysphagia, and mild ipsilateral ataxia, without any facial or oculomotor abnormality. A colleague argues the lesion must instead be in the pons because of the ataxia. Which response best explains why the medulla remains the most likely location?","choices":{"A":"Ataxia can never occur from any lesion located below the level of the pons","B":"Hoarseness and dysphagia are pontine findings requiring facial nerve injury","C":"The absence of facial and oculomotor findings proves the lesion must sit in the cord","D":"The medulla contains its own cerebellar peduncle fibers and lower cranial nerve nuclei","E":"Cerebellar peduncle fibers are found only within the pons and never the medulla"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medulla contains the nucleus ambiguus, whose damage produces hoarseness and dysphagia, and the restiform body carrying spinocerebellar and other cerebellar afferents, whose involvement produces ipsilateral ataxia, so this full combination is well explained by a purely medullary lesion without requiring pontine involvement, and the absence of facial or oculomotor findings is expected since those nuclei belong to the pons and midbrain respectively. Claiming ataxia never occurs below the pons ignores that medullary cerebellar peduncle fibers can produce it, claiming hoarseness and dysphagia require facial nerve injury is incorrect since they instead reflect nucleus ambiguus and cranial nerves IX and X, claiming the findings prove a spinal cord lesion ignores that spinal cord lesions do not produce cranial nerve deficits like hoarseness at all, and claiming cerebellar peduncle fibers exist only in the pons ignores that the restiform body, a major cerebellar peduncle component, is a defining medullary landmark.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that the medulla itself carries cerebellar afferent fibers through the restiform body, part of the eventual inferior cerebellar peduncle, prevents the common error of assuming any ataxia must localize to the pons or cerebellum alone. COMLEX: Nucleus ambiguus dysfunction producing hoarseness and dysphagia should immediately prompt consideration of a medullary rather than pontine process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — identifying major ventricular system components and their neuroanatomic relationships, the same brainstem-level reasoning extended here to differentiate medullary from pontine localization","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension develops sudden vertigo, difficulty swallowing, and an unsteady gait. Vascular imaging reveals occlusion of a vessel branch supplying the medulla. His physician correctly identifies the medulla, rather than the pons or midbrain, as the affected segment primarily because of which distinguishing feature?","choices":{"A":"The lesion sits above the pons-medulla junction, within basilar artery territory","B":"The lesion sits below the pons-medulla junction, in vertebral artery territory","C":"The lesion sits within the tectum, within posterior cerebral artery territory","D":"The lesion sits within the cerebral peduncles, within perforating branch territory","E":"The lesion sits within the internal capsule, within lenticulostriate territory"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The medulla is supplied by branches of the vertebral arteries and lies at or below the pons-medulla junction, so a vascular lesion affecting vertigo, dysphagia, and gait centers within this caudal, vertebral-artery-supplied territory correctly localizes to the medulla rather than more rostral brainstem segments. Placing the lesion at or above the pons-medulla junction in basilar territory instead describes pontine or higher structures, the tectum and posterior cerebral artery territory describe midbrain and posterior cortical structures unrelated to this presentation, the cerebral peduncles describe midbrain structures supplied by different perforating vessels, and the internal capsule and lenticulostriate territory describe a supratentorial structure entirely outside the brainstem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vertebral artery territory as the defining vascular supply of the medulla is a key fact for separating medullary strokes from basilar-territory pontine strokes on imaging and clinical grounds. COMLEX: Vertigo, dysphagia, and gait instability together should prompt evaluation of the posterior circulation, specifically the vertebral and posterior inferior cerebellar arteries feeding the medulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — CNS organizational principles applied here to vascular territory-based brainstem level localization","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":2,"stem":"During a brainstem dissection, a student identifies a prominent oval elevation lateral to the pyramid on the anterior medullary surface and correctly names it the olive. Which underlying structure produces this surface bulge?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus ambiguus and adjacent reticular formation","B":"The gracile and cuneate nuclei of the posterior columns","C":"The restiform body carrying cerebellar afferent fibers","D":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus and its overlying tract","E":"The inferior olivary nuclear complex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The olive, or inferior olivary eminence, is produced by the underlying inferior olivary nuclear complex, a large cell group that becomes progressively more prominent at rostral medullary levels and projects primarily to the contralateral cerebellum. The nucleus ambiguus lies deep within the reticular formation and does not produce a visible anterior surface bulge, the gracile and cuneate nuclei instead underlie posterior surface tubercles rather than the anterior olive, the restiform body forms a posterolateral rather than anterior surface elevation, and the spinal trigeminal nucleus with its overlying tract forms a lateral surface ridge, the tuberculum cinereum, rather than the anterior olive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The olive as the surface marker for the inferior olivary complex, itself a major source of climbing fiber input to the cerebellum, is a foundational landmark for orienting any anterior or anterolateral medullary cross-section. COMLEX: Identifying the olive on gross specimen or imaging quickly orients the examiner to the midolivary level, the richest cross-section for testing multiple tract and nucleus locations simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — identifying the major components of the ventricular system and their neuroanatomic relationships, the same external-to-internal landmark reasoning extended here to the olive and inferior olivary complex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man undergoes posterior fossa surgery, and the surgeon carefully identifies a thick posterolateral bundle on the medullary surface that will later join a smaller bundle to help form the inferior cerebellar peduncle. Which structure has the surgeon identified?","choices":{"A":"The restiform body","B":"The juxtarestiform body alone","C":"The gracile tubercle","D":"The trigeminal tubercle","E":"The postolivary sulcus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The restiform body is the thick posterolateral elevation on the medullary surface carrying posterior spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, and olivocerebellar afferents, and it enlarges as it ascends toward the pons, where it joins the smaller juxtarestiform body to collectively form the inferior cerebellar peduncle. The juxtarestiform body alone is the smaller of the two contributing bundles rather than the larger, more prominent surface elevation described, the gracile tubercle is a posterior midline landmark overlying the gracile nucleus rather than a lateral cerebellar peduncle component, the trigeminal tubercle overlies the spinal trigeminal tract rather than cerebellar afferent fibers, and the postolivary sulcus is a groove for cranial nerve IX and X exit rather than a fiber bundle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Restiform body plus juxtarestiform body equals inferior cerebellar peduncle is a key compound-structure fact frequently tested when tracing cerebellar afferent pathways from the medulla. COMLEX: Surgical or vascular injury to the restiform body produces ipsilateral limb and gait ataxia by disrupting spinocerebellar input before it ever reaches the cerebellum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — ventricular system component identification, the same structural-landmark approach extended here to cerebellar peduncle formation from surface bundles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":2,"stem":"A neurology resident is asked to trace the exit points of the lower cranial nerves on a brainstem specimen. She correctly identifies rootlets of the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves emerging from which surface landmark?","choices":{"A":"The preolivary sulcus","B":"The anterior median fissure","C":"The postolivary sulcus","D":"The medullary obex","E":"The cuneate tubercle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The postolivary, or retroolivary, sulcus is the shallow trough located between the restiform body and the olive from which the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerve rootlets emerge on the lateral medullary surface. The preolivary sulcus instead is the exit point for the hypoglossal nerve rather than IX and X, the anterior median fissure is the midline groove between the two pyramids and is not a cranial nerve exit point, the obex is the ventricular opening landmark rather than a nerve exit site, and the cuneate tubercle is a posterior surface landmark overlying the cuneate nucleus, unrelated to cranial nerve rootlet emergence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postolivary sulcus as the exit site for cranial nerves IX and X, paired with the preolivary sulcus for cranial nerve XII, is a classic anterior-versus-lateral surface landmark distinction tested on brainstem anatomy questions. COMLEX: Jugular foramen syndromes reflect combined injury to cranial nerves IX, X, and XI as they exit the skull together, distinct from the medullary rootlet origin itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — surrounding neuroanatomic relationships of major landmarks, the same approach extended here to lateral surface cranial nerve exit points","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":2,"stem":"A radiology fellow reviews two axial MRI slices through the medulla from the same patient. One slice shows a narrow, slit-like central cavity; the other shows a broad, diamond-shaped cavity forming a floor beneath the cerebellum. Which conclusion about slice location is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The narrow slit-like slice is rostral to the obex; the broad diamond-shaped slice is caudal to it","B":"The narrow slit-like slice is caudal to the obex; the broad diamond-shaped slice is rostral to it","C":"Both slices necessarily represent the exact same rostrocaudal level imaged twice over","D":"The narrow slice must instead represent a pathologic stenosis rather than normal anatomy","E":"The broad slice must instead represent frank hydrocephalus rather than normal anatomy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A narrow, slit-like central cavity is the expected appearance of the closed caudal medulla, where the cavity is simply a continuation of the spinal cord central canal, while a broad, diamond-shaped cavity forming a floor beneath the cerebellum is the expected appearance of the open rostral medulla, where the canal has flared into the fourth ventricle's rhomboid fossa; therefore the narrow slice is caudal and the broad slice is rostral to the obex. Reversing this assignment is incorrect, assuming both slices represent the same level ignores the clear morphologic difference described, assuming pathologic stenosis is unwarranted since a narrow canal is the normal caudal appearance, and assuming hydrocephalus is likewise unwarranted since a diamond-shaped fourth ventricle floor is the normal rostral appearance rather than pathologic dilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing a narrow slit-shaped canal from a broad diamond-shaped ventricular floor on imaging is a practical application of the closed-versus-open medulla concept that appears on both anatomy and radiology-integrated board questions. COMLEX: Correctly identifying rostrocaudal level from ventricular shape alone helps predict which cranial nerve nuclei should be visible on a given slice before formal landmark identification.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity and brain vesicles to the adult ventricular system, applied here to reading medullary imaging by cavity shape","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":2,"stem":"A first-year student is confused about why the hypoglossal nucleus appears to occupy different positions on cross-sections taken caudal versus rostral to the obex. Which explanation best resolves this apparent discrepancy?","choices":{"A":"One nucleus sits in the central gray caudally, then lies beneath the open ventricular floor rostrally","B":"There are two entirely separate hypoglossal nuclei, one caudal and one rostral to the obex","C":"The hypoglossal nucleus physically migrates outward through the tegmentum after birth","D":"Imaging artifact accounts for the difference, since the nucleus never changes position","E":"The nucleus is completely absent caudal to the obex and only appears rostral to it"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Caudal to the obex, the hypoglossal nucleus is embedded in the central gray matter surrounding the still-narrow central canal, while rostral to the obex, once the canal has flared into the fourth ventricle, this same continuous nucleus comes to lie in the medial floor of the ventricle, immediately beneath the ependymal surface, a positional shift that reflects the closed-to-open transition rather than any change in the nucleus itself. There are not two separate nuclei, since it is one continuous column of neurons, there is no postnatal migration, since this positional relationship is a normal feature of the mature closed-to-open transition rather than an ongoing developmental process, imaging artifact does not explain a real and consistent anatomic feature, and the nucleus is present, not absent, caudal to the obex, simply in a different position relative to the smaller canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Understanding that a single continuous nucleus can appear to change position purely because of the surrounding cavity's closed-to-open transition prevents a common misreading of serial brainstem cross-sections. COMLEX: This concept underlies why the hypoglossal trigone, a visible bulge on the fourth ventricle floor, is only apparent rostral to the obex, not caudal to it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — embryonic neural tube cavity to adult ventricular system relationships, extended here to explain apparent nuclear repositioning across the closed-to-open transition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathology specimen from a patient with a Chiari I malformation shows compression at the craniocervical junction. The pathologist notes distortion specifically at the transition where the medullary cavity changes configuration. Which structure is most directly affected at this precise transition point?","choices":{"A":"The pons-medulla junction, where the medulla ends and the pons then begins","B":"The motor decussation, where the corticospinal fibers cross the midline","C":"The sensory decussation, where the internal arcuate fibers cross the midline","D":"The obex, where the narrow central canal flares into the fourth ventricle","E":"The cerebellopontine angle, where cranial nerves VII and VIII both emerge"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The obex is specifically the point at which the medullary cavity changes configuration from the narrow, closed central canal to the broad, open fourth ventricular floor, making it the precise anatomic transition point referenced here, and it is a clinically relevant landmark in Chiari malformation given its proximity to the herniating cerebellar tonsils at the craniocervical junction. The pons-medulla junction is a separate, more rostral boundary marking transition between two different brainstem segments rather than a cavity-shape transition, the motor decussation is a fiber-crossing landmark for the corticospinal tract rather than a ventricular cavity landmark, the sensory decussation is likewise a fiber-crossing landmark for internal arcuate fibers rather than a cavity-shape transition, and the cerebellopontine angle is a lateral pons-level landmark for CN VII and VIII exit, unrelated to the closed-to-open medullary cavity transition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The obex's proximity to the craniocervical junction is precisely why Chiari malformation and tonsillar herniation exert their earliest and most direct mechanical effects right at this closed-to-open transition point. COMLEX: Syrinx formation in Chiari malformation classically begins near the obex and central canal, directly reflecting this anatomic vulnerability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — neural tube cavity to adult ventricular system relationships, applied here to a clinical craniocervical junction pathology centered on the obex transition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man develops sudden right-sided arm and leg weakness with reduced vibration and proprioception on the same side of the body, plus tongue deviation toward the left side upon protrusion. Imaging shows a small paramedian infarct in the left medulla. Which set of tracts and nuclei, all sharing a common paramedian vascular territory, best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and nucleus ambiguus, all supplied by the PICA","B":"The restiform body, vestibular nuclei, and solitary nucleus, all supplied by the PICA","C":"The pyramid and spinothalamic tract only, both supplied by the posterior spinal artery","D":"The medial lemniscus and vestibular nuclei only, both supplied by the vertebral artery","E":"The pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal root, supplied by the anterior spinal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus and root all lie in the paramedian medulla and share a common blood supply from penetrating branches of the anterior spinal artery, so their combined injury explains contralateral hemiparesis from pyramid damage, contralateral loss of vibration and proprioception from medial lemniscus damage, and ipsilateral tongue deviation from hypoglossal nerve damage. The spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and nucleus ambiguus instead occupy the lateral rather than paramedian territory supplied by the PICA, the restiform body, vestibular nuclei, and solitary nucleus are likewise lateral PICA-territory structures, describing only the pyramid and spinothalamic tract together as posterior spinal artery territory incorrectly groups a paramedian and a lateral structure under the wrong vessel, and describing only the medial lemniscus and vestibular nuclei together incorrectly pairs a paramedian structure with a lateral one under a single vessel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The paramedian trio of pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus sharing anterior spinal artery supply is the anatomic basis for medial medullary syndrome, a frequently tested vascular localization pattern. First Aid mnemonic for this territory: Ants love M&M's, anterior spinal artery affecting the Medial lemniscus and Motor pathways. COMLEX: Contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral dorsal column loss, and ipsilateral tongue deviation together should immediately suggest a paramedian rather than lateral medullary process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — comparing the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, and corticospinal pathways, the same tract organization now applied to a shared paramedian vascular territory within the medulla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman develops sudden vertigo, hoarseness, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature over her body while sparing pain and temperature on her ipsilateral face. Imaging localizes the infarct to the lateral medulla. Which structures, sharing this lateral territory, best account for the described deficits?","choices":{"A":"The medial lemniscus, hypoglossal nucleus, and pyramid, all in the paramedian tegmentum","B":"The vestibular nuclei, nucleus ambiguus, and anterolateral system in the lateral tegmentum","C":"The gracile nucleus, cuneate nucleus, and posterior columns, all in the dorsal midline area","D":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus and tectobulbospinal system, both located near the midline","E":"The inferior olivary nucleus by itself, without any involvement of an adjacent structure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vestibular nuclei, nucleus ambiguus, and anterolateral system all occupy the lateral medullary tegmentum, so their combined involvement explains vertigo from vestibular nuclei damage, hoarseness from nucleus ambiguus damage, and contralateral body pain and temperature loss from anterolateral system damage, while the spared ipsilateral facial pain and temperature sensation indicates the adjacent spinal trigeminal nucleus was not significantly involved in this case. The medial lemniscus, hypoglossal nucleus, and pyramid instead occupy the paramedian rather than lateral territory and would produce a very different, medial medullary pattern, the gracile and cuneate nuclei occupy the dorsal midline rather than the lateral tegmentum, the medial longitudinal fasciculus and tectobulbospinal system are likewise near the midline rather than laterally placed, and the inferior olivary nucleus alone would not explain vertigo, hoarseness, or contralateral sensory loss, since it primarily projects to the cerebellum rather than mediating these functions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lateral trio of vestibular nuclei, nucleus ambiguus, and anterolateral system sharing PICA territory is the anatomic foundation for lateral medullary, or Wallenberg, syndrome, one of the highest-yield brainstem vascular syndromes tested on boards. First Aid mnemonic: don't pick a PICA lame horse that can't eat, tying PICA to the hoarseness and dysphagia of lateral medullary syndrome. COMLEX: Contralateral body pain-temperature loss combined with ipsilateral bulbar symptoms like hoarseness should immediately prompt consideration of a lateral rather than paramedian medullary process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral, and corticospinal pathway comparison, extended here to a shared lateral vascular territory distinct from the paramedian pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with a lateral medullary infarct has loss of pain and temperature sensation over the left face. He also has reduced pinprick sensation in the skin of the left external ear and over the left posterior pharynx, regions supplied by cranial nerves other than the trigeminal. An examiner reasons that a single lesion explains all of these facial-region findings. Which anatomic fact supports this reasoning?","choices":{"A":"The solitary tract receives all somatic pain fibers from cranial nerves VII, IX, and X","B":"The anterolateral system carries pain from the face as well as from the trunk","C":"The spinal trigeminal tract also receives pain fibers from nerves VII, IX, and X","D":"The medial lemniscus carries both facial and body pain at the midmedullary level","E":"The nucleus ambiguus relays pain from the ear and pharynx to the sensory thalamus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The spinal trigeminal tract is made of central processes of primary sensory fibers that enter mainly in the trigeminal nerve, but it also receives somatic afferent fibers from cranial nerves VII, IX, and X; these terminate in the spinal trigeminal nucleus, the center for all somatic pain and temperature information carried on cranial nerves, which projects to the contralateral thalamus. A lateral medullary lesion of this tract therefore reduces ipsilateral pain and temperature from the face, ear, and pharynx together. The solitary tract carries visceral afferent input, including taste, not somatic pain. The anterolateral system carries pain from the contralateral body. The medial lemniscus carries discriminative touch and vibration, and the nucleus ambiguus is a motor nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The spinal trigeminal nucleus is the brainstem relay for pain and temperature from V, VII, IX, and X; its tract runs uncrossed, so medullary lesions cause ipsilateral facial loss. COMLEX: Ipsilateral facial pain loss plus contralateral body pain loss is the crossed sensory signature of the lateral medulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — anterolateral pathway comparison, extended here to its neighbor in the lateral medulla, the spinal trigeminal tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":3,"stem":"On a brainstem specimen, a student finds a slight longitudinal elevation on the lateral surface of the caudal medulla, below the level of the obex. It is formed by superficially placed fibers that carry pain and temperature from the ipsilateral face. This elevation is the ___","choices":{"A":"cuneate tubercle","B":"trigeminal tubercle","C":"gracile tubercle","D":"restiform body","E":"inferior olive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"On the lateral medullary surface caudal to the obex, fibers of the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus lie superficially and form the trigeminal tubercle, or tuberculum cinereum; rostral to the obex these fibers come to lie internal to the enlarging restiform body. The cuneate and gracile tubercles are posterior elevations over the cuneate and gracile nuclei, which relay discriminative touch and proprioception, not facial pain. The restiform body is a posterolateral cerebellar-bound bundle that enlarges rostral to the obex. The inferior olive is an anterolateral bulge over the inferior olivary nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surface landmarks predict the structure beneath: pyramid over corticospinal fibers, olive over the inferior olivary nucleus, trigeminal tubercle over the spinal trigeminal tract, gracile and cuneate tubercles over their nuclei. COMLEX: The superficial position of the spinal trigeminal tract makes it vulnerable in lateral medullary and posterior spinal artery lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — external medullary landmarks applied here to specimen-based structure identification","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q02","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman develops sudden right-sided ptosis, miosis, and facial anhidrosis, along with decreased pinprick sensation over the left arm and leg but normal pinprick sensation over the right face. MRI shows a small infarct in the right dorsolateral medulla. The ptosis and miosis are best explained by involvement of the ___","choices":{"A":"spinal trigeminal tract","B":"spinothalamic tract","C":"inferior cerebellar peduncle","D":"descending sympathetic tract","E":"dorsal motor nucleus of vagus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Descending hypothalamospinal sympathetic fibers travel uncrossed through the dorsolateral tegmentum of the medulla, so a lateral medullary lesion interrupts them before they reach the intermediolateral cell column, producing an ipsilateral central (first-order) Horner syndrome, which includes facial anhidrosis because the lesion lies proximal to where the facial sudomotor fibers leave with the external carotid plexus. The spinal trigeminal tract explains the preserved ipsilateral face finding if intact, and would instead produce ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss if involved. The spinothalamic tract explains the contralateral body sensory loss, not the pupil/lid findings. The inferior cerebellar peduncle, in the same lateral territory, carries cerebellar afferents, and the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, which lies dorsomedially in the ventricular floor, carries parasympathetic outflow; neither carries sympathetic fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sympathetic chain from hypothalamus to eye is a three-neuron pathway, and the first-order neuron's brainstem course through the dorsolateral medulla is the classic explanation for Horner syndrome in lateral medullary infarction. COMLEX: differentiate this central Horner pattern from a third-order (postganglionic, internal carotid) lesion such as carotid dissection, in which facial anhidrosis is usually absent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — internal tract and fiber localization within the medulla applied to a lesion-based vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":3,"stem":"A radiology fellow reviews an axial brainstem MRI showing a broad, diamond-shaped cerebrospinal fluid space forming the floor of an overlying structure, with cranial nerve nuclei arranged side by side across this floor rather than stacked around a narrow central canal. This image most likely represents a slice through the ___","choices":{"A":"open, rostral medulla","B":"closed, caudal medulla","C":"cervicomedullary junction","D":"obex","E":"pontomedullary junction just caudal to the pons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As the medulla opens rostral to the obex, the central canal splays open into the fourth ventricle, and nuclei that were stacked dorsoventrally around the canal spread out mediolaterally along the ventricular floor, producing the diamond-shaped CSF space described. The closed caudal medulla instead shows a narrow, slit-like central canal with nuclei stacked around it. The cervicomedullary junction and obex mark the transition itself rather than the open rostral segment, and a pontomedullary-junction slice would already show pontine structures such as the trapezoid body, not a diamond fourth ventricle floor alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognize 'diamond-shaped fourth ventricle floor' as shorthand for the open, rostral medulla, a common imaging-orientation distractor. COMLEX: this level-identification skill underlies correctly reading any brainstem cross-section on a practical or board image.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — closed versus open medulla transition applied here to level localization from an axial image","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":3,"stem":"An axial MRI slice shows a small brainstem structure directly continuous with the cervical spinal cord at its caudal end and directly continuous with a larger, more voluminous structure superiorly that contains the trigeminal motor and principal sensory nuclei. The structure shown on this slice is best classified as the ___","choices":{"A":"pons","B":"midbrain","C":"medulla oblongata","D":"cervical spinal cord","E":"cerebellar vermis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The medulla is the most caudal brainstem segment, directly continuous with the cervical spinal cord below and with the pons, which houses the trigeminal motor and principal sensory nuclei, above, so a structure positioned between these two landmarks is the medulla. The pons itself contains the trigeminal nuclei rather than lying caudal to them, the midbrain lies rostral to the pons and does not directly abut the spinal cord, the cervical spinal cord lies caudal to the foramen magnum rather than continuous with the pons, and the cerebellar vermis is a dorsal accessory structure rather than part of this rostrocaudal brainstem chain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: using two flanking landmarks, the spinal cord below and the trigeminal nuclei-bearing pons above, to triangulate the medulla's position is a reliable strategy on any brainstem-level imaging question. COMLEX: this same caudal-to-rostral chain, cord to medulla to pons to midbrain, is the basis for predicting which cranial nerves can be affected at a given lesion level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — the medulla's caudal brainstem position applied here to level-of-injury and cranial-nerve-based localization reasoning","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man sustains a small ischemic stroke restricted to a single brainstem segment and develops combined vagus, glossopharyngeal, and hypoglossal nerve deficits, without any oculomotor, trochlear, abducens, or facial nerve involvement. This pattern of combined deficits, and specifically this pattern of spared nerves, localizes the lesion most precisely to which brainstem segment?","choices":{"A":"the midbrain","B":"the pons","C":"the pontomedullary junction","D":"the cervicomedullary junction","E":"the medulla oblongata"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cranial nerves IX, X, and XII arise exclusively from the medulla, while the oculomotor and trochlear nerves arise from the midbrain and the abducens and facial nerves arise from the pons, so a lesion producing only IX, X, and XII deficits while sparing all four of these other nerves localizes cleanly to the medulla rather than any adjacent or transitional segment. The midbrain and pons are excluded by the sparing of their respective nerves, the pontomedullary junction would be expected to also affect abducens or facial function given its proximity to pontine nuclei, and the cervicomedullary junction lies caudal to all cranial nerve nuclei and would not produce isolated cranial neuropathies at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cranial nerves IX, X, and XII as purely medullary nuclei, in contrast to III and IV in the midbrain and VI and VII in the pons, is the anchor fact for localizing a lesion by which nerves are affected versus spared. COMLEX: a combined IX, X, XII deficit without any pontine or midbrain nerve involvement should specifically point toward the medulla rather than a broader brainstem process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — the medulla's caudal brainstem position applied here to level-of-injury and cranial-nerve-based localization reasoning","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the medulla as the caudal part of the brainstem between the spinal cord and pons.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with vertigo, dysphagia, and gait ataxia. Catheter angiography shows an occlusion isolated to a vessel that also supplies the upper cervical spinal cord and arises directly from the subclavian artery before joining its paired counterpart to form a single midline trunk. This occlusion localizes to which vessel territory?","choices":{"A":"the vertebral artery","B":"the basilar artery","C":"the internal carotid artery","D":"the posterior cerebral artery","E":"the anterior cerebral artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medulla and upper cervical cord are perfused by the paired vertebral arteries, which arise from the subclavian arteries and join at the pontomedullary junction to form the single midline basilar artery, so a vessel matching this exact description, subclavian origin, cervical cord supply, and paired-to-single confluence, is the vertebral artery. The basilar artery is the already-joined single trunk rather than a paired vessel arising from the subclavian, the internal carotid artery supplies the anterior circulation and cerebral hemispheres rather than the medulla, and the posterior and anterior cerebral arteries are terminal branches supplying cortex far rostral to the brainstem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vertebral artery's subclavian origin, cervical cord branches, and confluence into the basilar artery is the anatomic basis for why medullary strokes are grouped under vertebrobasilar, or posterior circulation, disease. COMLEX: vertigo, dysphagia, and ataxia together should always prompt evaluation of the posterior circulation rather than the carotid territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — the medulla's caudal brainstem position applied here to level-of-injury and cranial-nerve-based localization reasoning","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has loss of vibration and position sense in the left leg, with the left arm spared. MRI shows a tiny infarct on the posterior surface of the caudal medulla, beneath the medial of the two paired elevations that flank the posterior midline. The surface landmark overlying the damaged nucleus is the ___","choices":{"A":"cuneate tubercle","B":"trigeminal tubercle","C":"restiform body","D":"gracile tubercle","E":"facial colliculus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The posterior column nuclei first appear at the level of the motor decussation and form elevations on the posterior medulla: the gracile tubercle medially, over the gracile nucleus, which relays proprioception and vibration from the lower limb, and the cuneate tubercle laterally, over the cuneate nucleus for the upper limb. A lesion beneath the medial elevation therefore causes ipsilateral leg loss with the arm spared, because the fibers have not yet crossed. The cuneate tubercle lies lateral and serves the arm. The trigeminal tubercle carries facial pain fibers. The restiform body is a cerebellar peduncle bundle, and the facial colliculus is in the floor of the fourth ventricle at the caudal pons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gracile is medial and carries the leg; cuneate is lateral and carries the arm; lesions of these nuclei cause ipsilateral loss because crossing occurs in the internal arcuate fibers above them. COMLEX: An isolated ipsilateral posterior column deficit at the caudal medulla fits the posterior spinal artery territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — external medullary landmarks applied here to imaging-based structure identification","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major external landmarks: pyramids, olives, gracile and cuneate tubercles, inferior cerebellar peduncle, pre-olivary sulcus, postolivary sulcus, and obex.","batch":3,"stem":"On the posterior surface of the medulla, a small rounded elevation lies just lateral to the gracile tubercle and overlies a nucleus that relays proprioceptive and vibratory input from the upper limb. This elevation is best identified as the ___","choices":{"A":"gracile tubercle","B":"cuneate tubercle","C":"restiform body","D":"tuberculum cinereum","E":"medullary obex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cuneate tubercle is the posterior surface elevation lying just lateral to the gracile tubercle, and it overlies the cuneate nucleus, which relays proprioceptive and vibratory input specifically from the upper limb before these fibers cross as internal arcuate fibers. The gracile tubercle instead overlies the gracile nucleus, relaying lower limb rather than upper limb input, the restiform body is a more lateral fiber bundle carrying cerebellar afferents rather than a nucleus-overlying tubercle, the tuberculum cinereum is a lateral ridge overlying the spinal trigeminal tract, and the obex is the midline ventricular opening point rather than a lateral posterior elevation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cuneate tubercle for the upper limb and gracile tubercle for the lower limb is a paired posterior landmark distinction that mirrors the somatotopic organization of the dorsal columns themselves. COMLEX: this same medial-to-lateral, leg-to-arm somatotopy is preserved throughout the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway up to the thalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — external medullary landmarks applied here to imaging-based structure identification","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":3,"stem":"A brainstem cross-section shows cranial nerve nuclei stacked dorsoventrally in a compact column surrounding a narrow central lumen, with no visible ventricular floor. This section was most likely taken from a level ___","choices":{"A":"caudal to the obex","B":"rostral to the obex","C":"at the obex itself","D":"within the open fourth ventricle","E":"at the level of the facial colliculus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Caudal to the obex, the medulla is closed, meaning its cavity is simply a narrow continuation of the spinal cord's central canal, so nuclei remain stacked dorsoventrally around this compact lumen rather than spreading laterally along a ventricular floor. Rostral to the obex the canal has already flared open into the fourth ventricle, producing a visible floor with nuclei arranged side by side rather than stacked, at the obex itself the canal is just beginning to open into the ventricle, and the open fourth ventricle and a facial-colliculus (pontine) level both show a broad ventricular floor with nuclei arranged side by side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nuclei stacked around a narrow canal, rather than spread along a ventricular floor, is the single fastest visual cue for identifying a caudal, closed medullary section. COMLEX: recognizing this configuration helps predict that hypoglossal and vagal nuclei will appear embedded in central gray matter rather than beneath a thin ependymal floor at this level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — closed versus open medulla transition applied here to level localization from an axial image","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the medulla changes from a closed caudal medulla with central canal to an open rostral medulla forming the floor of the fourth ventricle.","batch":3,"stem":"A first-year student compares two axial sections from the same specimen and notes that the hypoglossal nucleus appears embedded deep within central gray matter on one slice, but lies directly beneath a thin ependymal surface on another slice. This apparent difference in position is best explained by which factor?","choices":{"A":"two entirely separate hypoglossal nuclei","B":"postmortem tissue shrinkage artifact","C":"the closed-to-open transition at the obex","D":"decussation of hypoglossal fibers","E":"compression from an adjacent syrinx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hypoglossal nucleus is a single continuous column of neurons whose apparent position shifts purely because of the surrounding cavity, embedded in central gray matter caudal to the obex where the canal is narrow, and lying just beneath the ependymal floor rostral to the obex once the canal has flared into the fourth ventricle, so the difference reflects the closed-to-open transition rather than any change in the nucleus itself. There are not two separate nuclei, since it remains one continuous structure, postmortem shrinkage would not produce this specific, consistent, and anatomically predictable positional pattern, the hypoglossal nucleus does not decussate, and no clinical history of a syrinx is provided to explain the finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that a single continuous nucleus can appear to shift position purely from the surrounding cavity's closed-to-open transition prevents a common misreading of serial brainstem sections. COMLEX: this concept explains why the hypoglossal trigone is visible on the ventricular floor only rostral to the obex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — closed versus open medulla transition applied here to level localization from an axial image","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man develops sudden left arm and leg weakness and diminished vibration sense on the same left limbs, without facial weakness or numbness. Tongue movement and pinprick sensation are entirely normal. MRI shows a small right paramedian medullary infarct. Which structure's relative sparing best explains the preserved pinprick sensation?","choices":{"A":"medial lemniscus","B":"pyramidal tract","C":"hypoglossal nucleus","D":"corticospinal tract","E":"spinothalamic tract"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A paramedian medullary infarct damages the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal fascicles, all clustered together in the anterior spinal artery territory, but spares the spinothalamic tract, which runs in the separate, more lateral anterolateral system, so pinprick sensation remains intact even though weakness and vibratory loss occur. The medial lemniscus and pyramidal/corticospinal tract are the very structures damaged, accounting for the vibratory loss and weakness, and the hypoglossal nucleus, while paramedian, governs tongue movement rather than pinprick sensation and is not the structure responsible for the sparing of pain sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinprick sensation surviving a paramedian medullary infarct is explained by the spinothalamic tract's lateral position, outside anterior spinal artery territory, the same logic that explains why medial and lateral medullary syndromes produce opposite sensory dissociation patterns. COMLEX: testing both vibration and pinprick separately at the bedside localizes a lesion to medial versus lateral medulla before imaging is available.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — internal tract and fiber localization within the medulla applied to a lesion-based vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl14_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Localize corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and sympathetic fibers.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man develops sudden left-sided limb ataxia after a small dorsolateral medullary infarct, with normal strength throughout and no tongue weakness. Which structure's involvement best explains the ataxia?","choices":{"A":"the inferior olive","B":"the restiform body","C":"the nucleus ambiguus","D":"the vestibular nuclei","E":"the spinal trigeminal tract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The restiform body carries posterior spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, and olivocerebellar afferents in the posterolateral medulla, and its disruption interrupts cerebellar input before it ever reaches the cerebellum, producing ipsilateral limb ataxia without any accompanying weakness, since the pyramid lies in the separate paramedian territory. The inferior olive lies ventrolaterally, outside a dorsolateral infarct, and its climbing fibers cross to enter the contralateral restiform body, so an olivary lesion would give ataxia contralateral to the lesion, the nucleus ambiguus would instead cause hoarseness and dysphagia, the vestibular nuclei would cause vertigo and nystagmus rather than limb ataxia specifically, and the spinal trigeminal tract would cause facial sensory loss rather than ataxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the restiform body as the medulla's own cerebellar afferent conduit explains why a purely medullary lesion can still produce prominent limb ataxia without any cerebellar parenchymal damage. COMLEX: ipsilateral ataxia without weakness after a brainstem stroke should prompt consideration of a lateral medullary, restiform body-involving lesion rather than an isolated cerebellar infarct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — internal tract and fiber localization within the medulla applied to a lesion-based vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":1,"stem":"The hypoglossal nucleus is classified as which functional cell column, and what does it innervate?","choices":{"A":"A somatic efferent nucleus supplying the ipsilateral intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles","B":"A visceral efferent nucleus supplying preganglionic fibers to thoracic and abdominal viscera","C":"A branchial efferent nucleus supplying pharyngeal and laryngeal skeletal muscle bilaterally","D":"A visceral afferent nucleus relaying taste sensation from the posterior third of the tongue","E":"A general sensory nucleus relaying touch and pressure sensation from the tongue surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hypoglossal nucleus is a somatic efferent nucleus whose axons exit through the preolivary sulcus and innervate the ipsilateral intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue, making it the final common pathway for tongue movement. It is not visceral efferent, since that description instead fits the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, it is not branchial efferent, since that fits the nucleus ambiguus, it does not carry taste, which is a solitary nucleus function, and it is not a general sensory relay, since tongue touch and pressure travel instead via the lingual nerve to the trigeminal system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoglossal equals somatic efferent, ipsilateral tongue, is the anchor fact for predicting tongue deviation direction after a lesion. First Aid mnemonic for the LMN pattern: the tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion as if it were licking its own wounds. COMLEX: A lower motor neuron hypoglossal lesion causes the protruded tongue to deviate toward the side of the lesion, the opposite of a supranuclear corticobulbar lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — identifying external medullary landmarks such as the preolivary and postolivary sulci, the surface exit points for the cranial nerve roots arising from the nuclei covered here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary function of the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus within the medulla?","choices":{"A":"Supplying somatic efferent fibers to the ipsilateral extrinsic tongue musculature","B":"Supplying branchial efferent fibers to pharyngeal and laryngeal skeletal muscle","C":"Relaying visceral sensory input from the carotid sinus and aortic arch centrally","D":"Relaying general somatic sensation from the external auditory canal peripherally","E":"Supplying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to thoracic and abdominal viscera"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is a visceral efferent nucleus lying just lateral to the hypoglossal nucleus, and it supplies preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in autonomic ganglia innervating thoracic and abdominal viscera, including the heart, lungs, and gut. It does not innervate the tongue, which is a hypoglossal function, it does not supply pharyngeal or laryngeal skeletal muscle, which is a nucleus ambiguus function, it does not relay carotid sinus or aortic arch sensation, which terminates in the solitary nucleus, and it plays no role in external ear sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dorsal motor vagal nucleus as the source of preganglionic parasympathetic outflow to the gut and heart explains why vagal nerve stimulation slows heart rate and increases gut motility. COMLEX: Damage here disrupts parasympathetic balance to the viscera, a mechanism relevant to autonomic dysregulation in medullary lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — identifying external medullary landmarks such as the preolivary and postolivary sulci, the surface exit points for the cranial nerve roots arising from the nuclei covered here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":1,"stem":"The nucleus ambiguus is best described by which functional classification and distribution?","choices":{"A":"A somatic efferent nucleus whose axons travel exclusively in cranial nerve XII","B":"A branchial efferent nucleus whose axons travel in cranial nerves IX and X","C":"A visceral efferent nucleus whose axons travel in cranial nerves III, VII, and IX","D":"A visceral afferent nucleus whose axons travel centrally within cranial nerve X","E":"A general sensory nucleus whose axons travel centrally within cranial nerve V"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus is a branchial, special visceral, efferent nucleus lying medial to the spinal trigeminal nucleus, and its axons travel in the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves to innervate pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles governing swallowing and phonation. It is not somatic efferent confined to cranial nerve XII, which instead describes the hypoglossal nucleus, it is not the visceral efferent parasympathetic outflow of cranial nerves III, VII, and IX, which describes scattered autonomic nuclei, it does not carry visceral afferent information, which is a solitary nucleus function, and it does not relay general sensory input via cranial nerve V.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nucleus ambiguus damage produces hoarseness and dysphagia from ipsilateral palatal, pharyngeal, and laryngeal weakness, a hallmark finding in lateral medullary lesions. COMLEX: Testing palatal elevation and gag reflex at the bedside directly screens nucleus ambiguus and cranial nerve IX and X function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — identifying external medullary landmarks such as the preolivary and postolivary sulci, the surface exit points for the cranial nerve roots arising from the nuclei covered here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which triad of deficits is classically produced by medial medullary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral hemiparesis, ipsilateral proprioceptive loss, contralateral tongue deviation","B":"Contralateral facial pain loss, ipsilateral limb ataxia, ipsilateral Horner syndrome","C":"Contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral proprioceptive loss, ipsilateral tongue deviation","D":"Contralateral body pain loss, ipsilateral facial pain loss, ipsilateral hoarseness","E":"Bilateral vertigo, bilateral nystagmus, bilateral loss of taste on the tongue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Medial medullary syndrome, from occlusion of anterior spinal artery penetrating branches, damages the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal root together, producing contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral loss of proprioception and vibration, and ipsilateral tongue deviation on protrusion. Reversing the laterality of the hemiparesis and sensory loss is incorrect, since these deficits are contralateral to the lesion while tongue deviation is ipsilateral, facial pain loss with ataxia and Horner syndrome instead describes the lateral, not medial, syndrome, body and facial pain loss with hoarseness likewise describes the lateral syndrome, and bilateral vertigo and taste loss describe neither syndrome accurately.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial medullary syndrome equals contralateral body weakness plus contralateral dorsal column loss plus ipsilateral tongue deviation, the classic paramedian triad. COMLEX: This crossed pattern, weakness and sensory loss on one side, tongue deviation on the other, is a frequently tested localizing sign for anterior spinal artery territory stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus within medullary cross-sections, the same paramedian-versus-lateral tract organization that underlies the two syndromes distinguished here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination of findings is most characteristic of lateral medullary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral proprioceptive loss, and tongue deviation","B":"Quadriparesis, bilateral proprioceptive loss, and bilateral tongue weakness","C":"Ipsilateral hemiparesis with preserved pain, temperature, and vibratory sensation","D":"Contralateral body pain loss, ipsilateral facial pain loss, hoarseness, and vertigo","E":"Contralateral facial weakness with sparing of pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral medullary, or Wallenberg, syndrome results from PICA territory injury to the anterolateral system, spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus, vestibular nuclei, nucleus ambiguus, and solitary nucleus, producing contralateral body pain and temperature loss, ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss, hoarseness and dysphagia, and vertigo with nystagmus, often with ipsilateral Horner syndrome. Contralateral hemiparesis with proprioceptive loss and tongue deviation instead describes medial, not lateral, medullary syndrome, bilateral deficits describe severe anterior spinal artery occlusion rather than a unilateral lateral lesion, hemiparesis with preserved sensation does not occur in either syndrome as classically described, and facial weakness is not a feature of either medullary syndrome, since the facial motor nucleus lies in the pons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral medullary syndrome equals crossed sensory loss, contralateral body, ipsilateral face, plus hoarseness, dysphagia, vertigo, and Horner syndrome, the single highest-yield brainstem vascular syndrome. COMLEX: No limb weakness accompanies classic Wallenberg syndrome, since the pyramid is a paramedian, not lateral, structure and is spared.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus within medullary cross-sections, the same paramedian-versus-lateral tract organization that underlies the two syndromes distinguished here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Medial medullary syndrome and lateral medullary syndrome are each attributable to occlusion of which respective vessels?","choices":{"A":"The anterior spinal artery for medial syndrome and the PICA for lateral syndrome","B":"The PICA for medial syndrome and the anterior spinal artery for lateral syndrome","C":"The posterior spinal artery for medial syndrome and the basilar artery for lateral syndrome","D":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery for both medial and lateral syndrome equally","E":"The superior cerebellar artery for medial syndrome and the vertebral artery for lateral syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Medial structures of the medulla, the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus and roots, are supplied by penetrating branches of the anterior spinal artery, while the posterolateral medulla rostral to the obex is supplied by the posterior inferior cerebellar artery, so medial medullary syndrome follows anterior spinal artery occlusion and lateral medullary syndrome follows PICA occlusion or vertebral artery occlusion proximal to the PICA origin. Reversing these vessel assignments is incorrect, the posterior spinal artery instead supplies the posterior columns caudal to the obex rather than the medial syndrome territory, the anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the cochlear nuclei at the pons-medulla junction rather than either syndrome, and the superior cerebellar artery supplies midbrain and cerebellar territory unrelated to either medullary syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior spinal artery for medial, PICA for lateral, is the single vessel-to-syndrome pairing most frequently tested for medullary stroke localization. First Aid mnemonics: Ants love M&M's for anterior spinal artery, Medial lemniscus and Motor loss, and don't pick a PICA lame horse that can't eat for PICA, hoarseness and dysphagia. COMLEX: Since PICA usually arises from the vertebral artery, vertebral artery occlusion itself can also produce the lateral medullary syndrome picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus within medullary cross-sections, the same paramedian-versus-lateral tract organization that underlies the two syndromes distinguished here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man presents with acute vertigo, nausea, and imbalance after a posterior circulation stroke. Examination reveals horizontal nystagmus and past-pointing on finger-to-nose testing ipsilateral to the lesion. Which medullary nuclear group best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"The vestibular nuclei, which receive input directly from cranial nerve VIII","B":"The solitary nucleus, which receives visceral afferent input from cranial nerve X","C":"The nucleus ambiguus, which supplies branchial efferent fibers via cranial nerve IX","D":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, which governs thoracic parasympathetic tone","E":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus, which relays facial pain and temperature sensation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medial and inferior vestibular nuclei occupy the lateral floor of the fourth ventricle and receive direct input from the vestibular division of cranial nerve VIII, so their involvement produces vertigo, nystagmus, and imbalance, matching this presentation. The solitary nucleus instead mediates taste and visceral sensation rather than balance, the nucleus ambiguus governs pharyngeal and laryngeal motor function rather than balance, the dorsal motor vagal nucleus governs visceral autonomic outflow, and the spinal trigeminal nucleus relays facial pain and temperature rather than vestibular input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vestibular nuclei as the medullary and pontine relay for cranial nerve VIII balance input explains why lateral brainstem lesions so often present with prominent vertigo and nystagmus. COMLEX: Central vertigo from a brainstem lesion typically presents with direction-changing nystagmus and accompanying long-tract or cranial nerve signs, unlike isolated peripheral vertigo.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus within medullary cross-sections, extended here to the neighboring vestibular nuclei sharing the same lateral tegmental territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman recovering from a lateral medullary infarct reports that food now tastes bland only on one side of her tongue, while she still perceives touch and pressure normally on both sides. Which structure best accounts for this isolated finding?","choices":{"A":"The hypoglossal nucleus, damaged along its somatic efferent motor column","B":"The nucleus ambiguus, damaged along its branchial efferent motor column","C":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, damaged along its parasympathetic column","D":"The solitary nucleus, damaged along its rostral gustatory portion","E":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus, damaged along its pars caudalis pain division"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rostral solitary nucleus receives special visceral afferent taste fibers from cranial nerves VII and IX and constitutes the gustatory nucleus, so its damage produces isolated ipsilateral taste loss while general touch and pressure sensation, carried separately by the lingual nerve, remain intact. The hypoglossal nucleus governs tongue movement rather than taste, so its damage would cause weakness rather than ageusia, the nucleus ambiguus governs pharyngeal and laryngeal motor function, the dorsal motor vagal nucleus governs visceral autonomic outflow rather than taste, and the spinal trigeminal pars caudalis mediates facial pain and temperature rather than taste.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The solitary nucleus's gustatory portion as the sole relay for taste from cranial nerves VII, IX, and X is a frequently tested dissociation between taste and general tongue sensation. COMLEX: Isolated ageusia without tongue weakness or numbness should prompt consideration of a solitary nucleus or tract lesion rather than a hypoglossal or trigeminal process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — tract and nucleus localization within medullary cross-sections, applied here to the solitary nucleus's position along the ventricular floor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man develops a lateral medullary infarct and, on examination, has diminished pinprick sensation over the ipsilateral cheek and forehead but normal pinprick sensation over the contralateral limbs and trunk at this early stage. Which structure's involvement best explains the ipsilateral facial finding?","choices":{"A":"The medial lemniscus, carrying crossed dorsal column sensory fibers","B":"The spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus, pars caudalis","C":"The hypoglossal nucleus, governing ipsilateral tongue musculature","D":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, governing visceral outflow","E":"The nucleus ambiguus, governing pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus carry pain and temperature information from the ipsilateral face and are located in the lateral medulla within PICA territory, so their involvement in a lateral medullary infarct produces ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss, distinct from the contralateral body pain and temperature loss caused by anterolateral system involvement. The medial lemniscus carries crossed proprioceptive and vibratory information from the body rather than facial pain, the hypoglossal nucleus governs tongue movement rather than facial sensation, the dorsal motor vagal nucleus governs visceral autonomic function, and the nucleus ambiguus governs pharyngeal and laryngeal muscle rather than facial sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The spinal trigeminal nucleus as the source of ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss, paired with contralateral body pain and temperature loss from the anterolateral system, defines the crossed sensory pattern that is the hallmark of lateral medullary syndrome. COMLEX: This crossed facial-versus-body sensory pattern is one of the most specific bedside findings for localizing a lesion to the lateral medulla rather than elsewhere along the neuraxis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus within medullary cross-sections, the same structure whose function is applied here to a clinical vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with atrial fibrillation develops sudden right arm and leg weakness. Examination shows reduced vibration and proprioception on the same right limbs, and his tongue deviates to the left on protrusion. Imaging confirms a small paramedian infarct in the left medulla. Which vessel territory is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"A branch of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying the left lateral medulla","B":"A branch of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying the pontomedullary junction","C":"A branch of the superior cerebellar artery supplying the rostral midbrain tegmentum","D":"A penetrating branch of the posterior spinal artery supplying the dorsal columns","E":"A penetrating branch of the anterior spinal artery supplying the left medial medulla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This combination of contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral proprioceptive and vibratory loss, and tongue deviation toward the side opposite the weakness represents medial medullary syndrome, caused by occlusion of anterior spinal artery penetrating branches supplying the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus or root on the left, producing right-sided body findings and left-sided tongue deviation. A posterior inferior cerebellar artery lesion instead produces the lateral, not medial, syndrome with crossed facial and body sensory loss rather than hemiparesis, an anterior inferior cerebellar artery lesion affects the pontomedullary junction and cochlear structures, a superior cerebellar artery lesion affects the midbrain rather than the medulla, and the posterior spinal artery supplies only the posterior columns caudal to the obex without producing hemiparesis or tongue deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A paramedian medullary infarct producing crossed hemiparesis, dorsal column loss, and tongue deviation should immediately be attributed to anterior spinal artery territory rather than PICA territory. COMLEX: Medial medullary syndrome is an infarct of paramedian branches of the anterior spinal and/or vertebral arteries; small penetrating-artery (lacunar) infarcts are classically due to lipohyalinosis from unmanaged hypertension rather than to cardiac emboli.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal root within medullary cross-sections, the same paramedian trio whose combined vascular injury defines medial medullary syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with hypertension develops sudden vertigo, hoarseness, and difficulty swallowing. Examination shows loss of pain and temperature sensation over the left face and the contralateral right trunk and limbs, along with left ptosis and miosis. Which diagnosis and vascular territory best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Lateral medullary syndrome from posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory infarction","B":"Medial medullary syndrome from anterior spinal artery territory infarction","C":"Acute lateral pontine syndrome from anterior inferior cerebellar artery territory infarction","D":"Midbrain Weber syndrome from posterior cerebral artery perforator infarction","E":"Locked-in syndrome from bilateral basilar artery territory infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The combination of vertigo, hoarseness and dysphagia from nucleus ambiguus involvement, crossed facial-versus-body pain and temperature loss from spinal trigeminal and anterolateral system involvement, and ipsilateral Horner syndrome from descending sympathetic fiber injury is the classic presentation of lateral medullary, or Wallenberg, syndrome from PICA territory infarction. Medial medullary syndrome instead produces contralateral hemiparesis and dorsal column loss with tongue deviation rather than this crossed sensory and bulbar pattern, lateral pontine syndrome from anterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion classically adds ipsilateral facial weakness and deafness not described here, Weber syndrome involves the cerebral peduncle and oculomotor nerve rather than crossed sensory loss, and locked-in syndrome produces quadriplegia with preserved consciousness rather than this sensory pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This exact constellation, vertigo, dysphagia, crossed facial-and-body sensory loss, and ipsilateral Horner syndrome, is the single most heavily tested brainstem vascular syndrome on Step 1 and should be recognized instantly. COMLEX: Recognizing PICA-territory infarction as the cause guides urgent posterior circulation vascular imaging and stroke workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — predicting major deficits caused by PICA and other posterior circulation vessel occlusions, applied here to lateral medullary, Wallenberg, syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student examines a patient with a confirmed lateral medullary infarct and is surprised to find normal strength in all four limbs and a symmetric face. An attending explains that limb and facial weakness are not expected in this syndrome for which reason?","choices":{"A":"The pyramid and facial nucleus are supplied redundantly by both PICA and ASA","B":"Weakness only appears once a lateral lesion has extended into the pons bilaterally","C":"The pyramid and facial nucleus lie outside the lateral PICA territory affected here","D":"The corticospinal tract decussates rostral to the medulla and is therefore unreachable","E":"The facial nucleus lies within the lateral medulla but is spared by chance alone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pyramid, carrying corticospinal fibers, occupies the paramedian anterior spinal artery territory, and the facial motor nucleus lies in the pons rather than the medulla at all, so neither structure falls within the lateral PICA territory damaged in lateral medullary syndrome, explaining the preserved strength and symmetric face. Claiming redundant dual supply is incorrect, since the pyramid is supplied by the anterior spinal artery only, not PICA, claiming weakness requires bilateral pontine extension mischaracterizes the actual anatomic reason, claiming the corticospinal tract decussates rostral to the medulla is inaccurate since the pyramidal decussation occurs at the caudal medulla itself, and claiming the facial nucleus lies within the lateral medulla is anatomically incorrect, since it is a pontine structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No limb weakness and no facial weakness in classic Wallenberg syndrome is explained purely by territory, the pyramid is paramedian and the facial nucleus is pontine, neither reachable by a lateral PICA-territory lesion. COMLEX: This absence of weakness is a key discriminating feature that separates lateral medullary syndrome from medial medullary syndrome and from pontine syndromes on board vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinal trigeminal tract/nucleus within medullary cross-sections, the same paramedian-versus-lateral organization that explains why medial and lateral medullary syndromes produce distinct, non-overlapping deficit patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_bloom_q01","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with atrial fibrillation and poorly controlled hypertension develops sudden right arm and leg weakness. On exam, the tongue deviates toward the left on protrusion, and vibration and joint position sense are reduced over the right limbs while pain and temperature sensation remain intact throughout. This patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___","choices":{"A":"posterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"superior cerebellar artery","C":"posterior spinal artery","D":"basilar artery, paramedian pontine branch","E":"anterior spinal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Contralateral limb weakness and dorsal column loss with an ipsilateral, LMN-pattern tongue deviation localizes to the paramedian medulla, where the anterior spinal artery supplies the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal fascicles together. Spinothalamic fibers run laterally and are spared, which is why pain and temperature are intact. The posterior inferior cerebellar and superior cerebellar arteries supply lateral medullary and cerebellar/midbrain territory respectively, the posterior spinal artery supplies only the dorsal columns without motor or hypoglossal involvement, and a paramedian pontine branch would produce cranial nerve VI or VII findings instead of tongue deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tongue deviating toward the lesion and away from the weak limbs, plus contralateral weakness and proprioceptive loss, with pain/temperature spared, is the medial medullary syndrome fingerprint; anchor it to the anterior spinal artery, not PICA. COMLEX: atrial fibrillation is the classic embolic risk factor woven into this vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — medial versus lateral medullary syndrome derived here from crossed/alternating findings rather than named directly","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl15_bloom_q02","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman develops sudden vertigo, hoarseness, and dysphagia. Examination shows loss of pinprick sensation over the left face and the contralateral right trunk and limbs, along with left-sided ptosis, miosis, and left-sided limb ataxia on finger-to-nose testing. Strength is normal in all four limbs. The most likely additional finding in this patient is ___","choices":{"A":"contralateral tongue deviation","B":"ipsilateral facial weakness","C":"absent ipsilateral gag reflex","D":"contralateral limb ataxia","E":"bilateral limb weakness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crossed facial and body pain/temperature loss, ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and ipsilateral limb ataxia localize to the dorsolateral medulla, the same territory containing the nucleus ambiguus, which produces ipsilateral palatal and pharyngeal weakness and an absent gag reflex alongside the hoarseness and dysphagia already described. Corticospinal fibers run in the medial medulla and are spared here, which is why strength is preserved and why tongue deviation and bilateral limb weakness are not expected findings. Facial weakness would require CN VII nucleus or fascicle involvement, which lies in the pons, not the medulla, and the ataxia described is already ipsilateral, not contralateral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the highest-yield teaching point in lateral medullary syndrome is normal strength, since spinal trigeminal, spinothalamic, vestibular, nucleus ambiguus, and sympathetic fibers are all lateral while the pyramid is medial and spared. COMLEX: differentiate this from lateral pontine syndrome, which does add ipsilateral facial weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — medial versus lateral medullary syndrome derived here from crossed/alternating findings rather than named directly","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl15_bloom_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient recovering from a lateral medullary infarct is found on exam to have diminished palatal elevation and an absent gag reflex on the ipsilateral side. Nursing notes separately report that food now tastes bland only over the ipsilateral posterior third of the tongue, while touch and pressure sensation on the tongue remain normal and symmetric. The nucleus most likely responsible for the diminished gag reflex is the ___","choices":{"A":"solitary nucleus","B":"nucleus ambiguus","C":"dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","D":"hypoglossal nucleus","E":"vestibular nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus provides branchial motor fibers to the pharynx, larynx, and soft palate via CN IX and X, so its involvement produces the diminished gag reflex and palatal weakness. The solitary nucleus instead carries visceral and special sensory input, including the taste loss described separately in the same patient, explaining why both findings can coexist from two adjacent but functionally distinct nuclei. The dorsal motor nucleus of vagus supplies parasympathetic output to thoracoabdominal viscera rather than pharyngeal muscle, the hypoglossal nucleus governs tongue movement, not palatal elevation, and the vestibular nuclei govern balance and eye movement, not the gag reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know that nucleus ambiguus is motor to the gag/swallow/phonation and solitary nucleus is sensory for taste and viscera, since boards often test both from the same lateral medullary vignette to see if students conflate them. COMLEX: this distinction is directly relevant to dysphagia and aspiration risk assessment after brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — medullary cranial nerve nuclei applied here to differentiate two nuclei with overlapping deficits in a single vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl15_bloom_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient recovering from a small dorsal medullary infarct develops early satiety, abdominal bloating, and delayed gastric emptying, with a normal voice, normal swallowing, and a normal gag reflex on exam. Which nucleus's involvement best explains the visceral symptoms?","choices":{"A":"nucleus ambiguus","B":"solitary nucleus","C":"hypoglossal nucleus","D":"dorsal vagal nucleus","E":"vestibular nuclei"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus supplies preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to thoracic and abdominal viscera, including the stomach, so its involvement slows gastric motility and produces early satiety and bloating, while sparing voice, swallowing, and the gag reflex, which instead depend on the separate nucleus ambiguus. The nucleus ambiguus governs pharyngeal and laryngeal muscle rather than visceral motility, so its damage would instead produce hoarseness and dysphagia, not gastroparesis, the solitary nucleus mediates taste and visceral sensory input rather than motor outflow to the gut, the hypoglossal nucleus governs tongue movement, and the vestibular nuclei govern balance rather than autonomic gut function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsal motor vagal nucleus as the source of preganglionic parasympathetic outflow to thoracoabdominal viscera explains why its damage produces autonomic gut dysfunction distinct from the pharyngeal and laryngeal deficits of a nucleus ambiguus lesion. COMLEX: recognizing this dissociation, autonomic symptoms without bulbar weakness, helps localize a lesion to this specific medial visceral efferent column.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — medullary cranial nerve nuclei applied here to differentiate two nuclei with overlapping deficits in a single vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl15_bloom_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major medullary cranial nerve nuclei and their functions: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and spinal trigeminal nucleus.","batch":3,"stem":"After a small dorsal medullary lesion, a patient notices a dry mouth on one side, and sialometry shows reduced parotid secretion on that side. Taste, voice, swallowing, gag reflex, and facial sensation are all normal. The nucleus most likely damaged is the ___","choices":{"A":"inferior salivatory nucleus","B":"superior salivatory nucleus","C":"dorsal motor vagal nucleus","D":"nucleus ambiguus","E":"solitary nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The inferior salivatory nucleus is a basal plate visceral efferent nucleus of the medulla whose preganglionic axons exit with the glossopharyngeal nerve and synapse in the otic ganglion, whose postganglionic cells supply secretomotor input to the parotid gland. The superior salivatory nucleus belongs to the facial nerve and supplies the submandibular, sublingual, and lacrimal glands. The dorsal motor vagal nucleus supplies thoracic and abdominal viscera. Damage to the nucleus ambiguus would cause hoarseness, dysphagia, and gag loss, and damage to the solitary nucleus would affect taste and visceral reflexes, all normal here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inferior salivatory (IX, otic ganglion) supplies the parotid; superior salivatory (VII) supplies the submandibular, sublingual, and lacrimal glands. COMLEX: Know the parasympathetic route to the parotid when reasoning about salivary loss after skull base or middle ear injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — medullary cranial nerve nuclei applied here to separate a visceral efferent nucleus from adjacent nuclei with overlapping territory","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl15_bloom_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Distinguish medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"Which single finding, if present, would most strongly argue against a diagnosis of lateral medullary syndrome and instead favor medial medullary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"ipsilateral Horner syndrome","B":"ipsilateral facial numbness","C":"contralateral body pain loss","D":"ipsilateral limb ataxia","E":"contralateral hemiparesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Contralateral hemiparesis requires pyramid involvement, and the pyramid lies in the paramedian anterior spinal artery territory rather than the lateral PICA territory, so its presence is inconsistent with a lesion confined to the lateral medulla and instead points to medial medullary syndrome. Ipsilateral Horner syndrome, ipsilateral facial numbness, contralateral body pain loss, and ipsilateral limb ataxia are all expected findings in lateral medullary syndrome, arising respectively from descending sympathetic fibers, the spinal trigeminal tract, the anterolateral system, and the restiform body, all of which occupy the lateral tegmentum spared in the medial syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: limb weakness is the single most reliable discriminator between the two classic medullary syndromes, since the pyramid is paramedian and essentially never involved in a pure lateral medullary lesion. COMLEX: whenever a vignette combines crossed sensory findings with true limb weakness, consider a larger infarct spanning both paramedian and lateral territory rather than a pure lateral medullary syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — medial versus lateral medullary syndrome derived here from crossed/alternating findings rather than named directly","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"16":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structures fall within the vascular territory of the anterior spinal artery in the medulla?","choices":{"A":"The anterolateral system, spinal trigeminal tract, and vestibular nuclei","B":"The nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, and inferior cerebellar peduncle","C":"The gracile nucleus, cuneate nucleus, and spinal trigeminal nucleus caudally","D":"The facial motor nucleus, trapezoid body, and superior olivary nucleus","E":"The pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus and roots"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medial structures of the medulla at all levels, the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus and roots, are served by penetrating branches of the anterior spinal artery. The anterolateral system, spinal trigeminal tract, and vestibular nuclei instead belong to PICA territory, the nucleus ambiguus and solitary nucleus are also PICA structures rather than anterior spinal artery structures, the gracile and cuneate nuclei with the caudal spinal trigeminal nucleus belong to the posterior spinal artery territory caudal to the obex, and the facial nucleus, trapezoid body, and superior olivary nucleus are pontine structures outside the medulla entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior spinal artery equals pyramid, medial lemniscus, hypoglossal, the paramedian trio whose shared blood supply explains why medial medullary syndrome damages all three together. First Aid mnemonic: Ants love M&M's, anterior spinal artery affecting the Medial lemniscus and Motor pathways. COMLEX: Recognizing this shared territory lets a clinician predict the full triad of deficits from a single vascular event rather than three separate lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome by their deficit patterns, the same two syndromes whose vascular basis in the anterior spinal artery and PICA is explained here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"The entire posterolateral medulla rostral to the obex, including the anterolateral system, spinal trigeminal tract, vestibular nuclei, solitary tract, and nucleus ambiguus, is supplied by which artery?","choices":{"A":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery, a branch of the vertebral artery","B":"The anterior spinal artery, a small unpaired midline branch of the vertebral artery","C":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery, a branch of the basilar artery","D":"The posterior spinal artery, typically arising as a branch of the PICA itself","E":"The superior cerebellar artery, a branch arising from the basilar artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rostral to the obex, the entire posterolateral medulla is served by branches of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery, itself a branch of the vertebral artery, which explains why occlusion of either vessel produces the same lateral medullary deficit pattern. The anterior spinal artery instead serves the medial, paramedian medulla, the anterior inferior cerebellar artery serves only the cochlear nuclei and a small adjacent restiform body region at the pontomedullary junction, the posterior spinal artery serves the posterior medulla caudal to the obex, and the superior cerebellar artery supplies midbrain and cerebellar territory unrelated to the medulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PICA territory equals the broad posterolateral medulla, the single vessel most often tested for lateral medullary syndrome localization. First Aid mnemonic: don't pick a PICA lame horse that can't eat, tying PICA to the hoarseness and dysphagia of lateral medullary syndrome. COMLEX: Since PICA usually branches from the vertebral artery, a proximal vertebral artery occlusion can mimic isolated PICA occlusion clinically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome by their deficit patterns, the same two syndromes whose vascular basis in the anterior spinal artery and PICA is explained here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"Aside from the anterior spinal artery and PICA, small direct branches supplying the anterolateral medulla arise from which vessel?","choices":{"A":"The basilar artery, via small direct penetrating pontine branches","B":"The vertebral artery itself, via small direct penetrating branches","C":"The posterior cerebral artery, via small thalamoperforating branches","D":"The anterior communicating artery, via small septal perforating branches","E":"The middle cerebral artery, via small lenticulostriate perforating branches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In addition to giving rise to the anterior spinal artery, posterior spinal artery, and PICA, the vertebral artery itself gives off small direct penetrating branches that supply the anterolateral medulla, so the medulla receives contributions from the vertebral artery both directly and through its named branches. The basilar, posterior cerebral, anterior communicating, and middle cerebral arteries all supply structures rostral to the medulla, the pons, midbrain, thalamus, or cerebral hemispheres, and play no direct role in medullary perfusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medulla's blood supply is entirely vertebrobasilar in origin, with the vertebral artery contributing both named branches and small direct penetrators to the anterolateral medulla. COMLEX: This direct vertebral contribution explains why some anterolateral medullary deficits occur without a discrete PICA or anterior spinal artery occlusion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome by their deficit patterns, the same two syndromes whose vascular basis in the anterior spinal artery and PICA is explained here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion damaging the nucleus ambiguus produces which combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral tongue paralysis with deviation toward the side of the lesion","B":"Ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss with preserved touch sensation","C":"Bilateral loss of taste sensation over the posterior third of the tongue","D":"Ipsilateral vertigo, nystagmus, and past-pointing on finger-to-nose testing","E":"Ipsilateral vocal cord paralysis with hoarseness, dysphagia, and gag loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the nucleus ambiguus provides branchial efferent innervation to the pharynx and larynx via cranial nerves IX and X, a lesion here causes ipsilateral vocal cord and soft palate paralysis, producing hoarseness, dysphagia, and a diminished or absent gag reflex. Ipsilateral tongue deviation instead reflects hypoglossal nucleus damage, ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss reflects spinal trigeminal tract damage, vertigo and past-pointing reflect vestibular nuclei damage, and bilateral taste loss does not match the unilateral, ipsilateral pattern produced by a solitary nucleus lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nucleus ambiguus damage equals hoarseness, dysphagia, and a diminished gag reflex from ipsilateral pharyngeal and laryngeal weakness, a core finding in lateral medullary syndrome. COMLEX: Bedside gag reflex and palatal elevation testing directly screens nucleus ambiguus and cranial nerve IX and X integrity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying nucleus ambiguus and hypoglossal nucleus as functional cell columns, applied here to predict the specific clinical findings produced when these and other medullary structures are damaged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":1,"stem":"A lower motor neuron lesion of the hypoglossal nucleus or its exiting fascicles produces which finding on tongue protrusion?","choices":{"A":"Flaccid contralateral tongue weakness with deviation away from the side of the lesion","B":"Spastic ipsilateral tongue weakness with deviation away from the side of the lesion","C":"Bilateral tongue fasciculations without any deviation on protrusion at all","D":"Flaccid ipsilateral tongue weakness with deviation toward the side of the lesion","E":"Preserved tongue strength with isolated loss of taste sensation only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hypoglossal nucleus and its exiting fascicles supply the ipsilateral genioglossus and other tongue muscles, so a lower motor neuron lesion produces flaccid ipsilateral weakness, and because the unopposed contralateral genioglossus pushes the tongue tip toward the weak side, the protruded tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion. Contralateral weakness with deviation away from the lesion, spastic weakness, and deviation away from the lesion all describe an upper motor neuron corticobulbar pattern rather than this nuclear or fascicular lesion, bilateral fasciculations without deviation would suggest a diffuse process rather than a focal lesion, and taste is a solitary nucleus function unrelated to hypoglossal fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A nuclear or fascicular hypoglossal lesion causes the tongue to deviate toward the side of the lesion, the opposite of a supranuclear corticobulbar lesion, a frequently tested localizing distinction. First Aid mnemonic: the tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion as if it were licking its own wounds. COMLEX: Flaccid tongue weakness with fasciculations points to a lower motor neuron process, while spastic weakness without fasciculations points to an upper motor neuron process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying nucleus ambiguus and hypoglossal nucleus as functional cell columns, applied here to predict the specific clinical findings produced when these and other medullary structures are damaged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":1,"stem":"The area postrema is functionally unique among medullary structures because it serves as an emetic center that lacks which protective feature?","choices":{"A":"An intact blood-brain barrier, which normally keeps circulating toxins out of the brain","B":"A functioning glymphatic drainage system, allowing rapid clearance of metabolites","C":"A myelinated efferent pathway, allowing rapid transmission of vomiting signals","D":"A dedicated arterial supply, allowing it to remain resistant to ischemic injury","E":"A GABAergic inhibitory circuit, allowing it to suppress the vomiting reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The area postrema, located in the wall of the fourth ventricle at the obex, is one of the circumventricular organs that lacks an intact blood-brain barrier, allowing it to sample circulating blood-borne toxins and emetogenic substances directly and trigger vomiting, which is why a focal lesion or irritation here commonly presents as intractable vomiting. It is not distinguished by glymphatic drainage, efferent myelination, a dedicated resistant arterial supply, or an inhibitory GABAergic circuit, none of which describes the actual anatomic basis for its chemosensory role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The area postrema's absent blood-brain barrier is the classic explanation for why it functions as the chemoreceptor trigger zone for vomiting and why chemotherapy agents can act there directly. COMLEX: Intractable vomiting out of proportion to other neurologic findings should raise suspicion for a dorsal medullary lesion near the obex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying nucleus ambiguus and hypoglossal nucleus as functional cell columns, applied here to predict the specific clinical findings produced when these and other medullary structures are damaged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man sustains a vertebral artery dissection proximal to the origin of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. Which set of medullary structures is most likely to become ischemic as a result?","choices":{"A":"The gracile nucleus, cuneate nucleus, and posterior columns caudal to the obex","B":"The pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus and roots bilaterally","C":"The anterolateral system, spinal trigeminal tract, and vestibular nuclei","D":"The facial motor nucleus, trapezoid body, and superior olivary nucleus","E":"The cochlear nuclei and the adjacent restiform body at the pontomedullary junction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because PICA typically arises directly from the vertebral artery, a dissection or occlusion of the vertebral artery proximal to the PICA origin cuts off flow to the same posterolateral territory that PICA itself supplies, namely the anterolateral system, spinal trigeminal tract, vestibular nuclei, solitary tract, and nucleus ambiguus, reproducing lateral medullary syndrome. The pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus belong to anterior spinal artery territory rather than PICA territory, the gracile and cuneate nuclei belong to the posterior spinal artery territory caudal to the obex, the facial nucleus and auditory relay nuclei are pontine structures, and the cochlear nuclei belong to the anterior inferior cerebellar artery rather than PICA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proximal vertebral artery occlusion can clinically mimic isolated PICA occlusion because PICA is a direct vertebral artery branch, a commonly tested vascular anatomy pearl. COMLEX: Vertebral artery dissection in a younger patient should prompt consideration of trauma or connective tissue disease as an underlying cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — predicting major deficits caused by PICA and vertebral artery lesions, applied here to a proximal vertebral dissection that cuts off flow to PICA territory before it branches","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman develops acute bilateral tongue weakness, bilateral limb weakness, and bilateral loss of proprioception and vibration after a posterior circulation stroke. Which vascular explanation best accounts for this bilateral pattern?","choices":{"A":"Occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery on both sides","B":"Occlusion of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery near the cochlear nuclei","C":"Occlusion of both posterior spinal arteries, affecting the dorsal columns","D":"Occlusion of both vertebral arteries sparing the medial penetrating branches","E":"Occlusion of the unpaired anterior spinal artery trunk itself, not a single branch"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The paired penetrating branches of the anterior spinal artery that supply the medial medulla alternate to the right and left, so occlusion of one penetrating branch typically produces unilateral medial medullary syndrome, but occlusion of the unpaired anterior spinal artery trunk itself cuts off both sets of penetrators simultaneously, producing bilateral damage to both pyramids, both medial lemnisci, and both hypoglossal nuclei or roots. Bilateral PICA occlusion is exceedingly rare and would produce crossed sensory rather than motor and proprioceptive deficits, anterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion affects hearing rather than limb function, bilateral posterior spinal artery occlusion would spare the pyramids and hypoglossal fibers, and bilateral vertebral occlusion sparing the medial penetrators contradicts the bilateral medial findings described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bilateral tongue and limb weakness with bilateral dorsal column loss after a posterior circulation stroke should point to anterior spinal artery trunk occlusion rather than a unilateral penetrating branch. COMLEX: This bilateral paramedian pattern is a high-yield exception to the usual unilateral medial medullary syndrome presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal root within medullary cross-sections, the same paramedian trio whose bilateral vascular compromise produces the bilateral deficits described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient develops a rare infarct caudal to the obex involving the posterior spinal artery territory. Examination reveals ipsilateral loss of proprioception and vibration in the limbs together with ipsilateral loss of facial pain and temperature sensation. Which structures best explain this combined deficit?","choices":{"A":"The medial lemniscus together with the hypoglossal nucleus and exiting roots","B":"The gracile and cuneate nuclei with the spinal trigeminal tract","C":"The anterolateral system together with the nucleus ambiguus and its roots","D":"The vestibular nuclei together with the solitary tract and nucleus","E":"The inferior olivary complex together with the medullary reticular formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Caudal to the obex, the posterior spinal artery supplies the posterior column, gracile and cuneate, nuclei along with the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus, so its rare occlusion produces an ipsilateral loss of proprioception and vibration from damage to the posterior column nuclei together with an ipsilateral loss of facial pain and temperature from spinal trigeminal tract damage, both ipsilateral because these caudal structures lie above the level of decussation for the relevant crossed pathways. The medial lemniscus and hypoglossal nucleus belong to anterior spinal artery territory, the anterolateral system and nucleus ambiguus belong to PICA territory rostral to the obex, the vestibular nuclei and solitary tract also belong to rostral PICA territory, and the inferior olivary complex and reticular formation are not part of the posterior spinal artery's supply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The posterior spinal artery's rare syndrome produces ipsilateral, not crossed, deficits because it affects the posterior column nuclei and spinal trigeminal tract before their fibers decussate or ascend contralaterally. COMLEX: This all-ipsilateral pattern distinguishes posterior spinal artery infarction from the crossed pattern of lateral medullary syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus within medullary cross-sections, extended here to the posterior spinal artery's rare, entirely ipsilateral syndrome caudal to the obex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a rare isolated medullary lesion develops severe limb ataxia contralateral to the lesion and impaired motor learning without significant weakness or sensory loss. Which structure's disruption best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus ambiguus, disrupting branchial efferent output to the pharynx and larynx","B":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, disrupting parasympathetic outflow to viscera","C":"The inferior olivary complex, disrupting olivocerebellar climbing fiber input","D":"The medullary reticular formation, disrupting autonomic control of heart and lungs","E":"The area postrema, disrupting chemoreceptor sensing of circulating toxins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The inferior olivary complex is a massive precerebellar relay nucleus whose climbing fibers project to the contralateral cerebellum, and disruption of this olivocerebellar pathway impairs motor learning and produces severe ataxia and incoordination without primary weakness or sensory loss, since the olive is not itself a motor or sensory relay for the body. Nucleus ambiguus damage instead causes hoarseness and dysphagia, dorsal motor vagal damage causes autonomic visceral dysfunction, reticular formation damage causes cardiorespiratory instability, and area postrema damage causes intractable vomiting, none of which reproduces this ataxia-predominant picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The inferior olive as the source of climbing fibers essential for cerebellar motor learning explains why isolated olivary damage produces ataxia resembling a cerebellar lesion despite being a brainstem structure. COMLEX: Ataxia without weakness or sensory loss should prompt consideration of cerebellar or olivocerebellar pathway involvement rather than a corticospinal or lemniscal lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — identifying the olives as an external landmark overlying the inferior olivary complex, whose internal disruption and consequence for cerebellar motor learning is explored here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with a rapidly expanding posterior fossa mass develops sudden central apnea and cardiovascular collapse. Which mechanism and medullary structure best explain this catastrophic finding?","choices":{"A":"Tonsillar herniation compressing the nucleus ambiguus controlling pharyngeal muscles","B":"Tonsillar herniation compressing the area postrema controlling the vomiting reflex","C":"Tonsillar herniation compressing the inferior olivary complex controlling motor learning","D":"Tonsillar herniation compressing the ventrolateral reticular formation","E":"Tonsillar herniation compressing the hypoglossal nucleus controlling tongue movement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ventrolateral reticular area of the medulla contains the vital cell groups that control heart rate and respiration, so sudden tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum from a rapidly expanding posterior fossa mass can mechanically crush this region, producing immediate central apnea, respiratory arrest, and cardiovascular collapse, a well-recognized neurosurgical emergency. Compression of the nucleus ambiguus, area postrema, inferior olivary complex, or hypoglossal nucleus would instead produce dysphagia, vomiting, ataxia, or tongue weakness respectively, none of which explains sudden cardiorespiratory arrest.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sudden central apnea as an early sign of medullary compression from tonsillar herniation reflects damage to the ventrolateral reticular formation's vital cardiorespiratory centers, a classic neurosurgical emergency vignette. COMLEX: Recognizing early central apnea as a herniation warning sign is essential for triggering emergent decompression before irreversible brainstem injury occurs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — identifying medullary cranial nerve nuclei as discrete functional cell columns, contrasted here with the diffuse, non-nuclear reticular formation whose vital autonomic role is exposed by herniation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with a small dorsal medullary lesion at the level of the obex develops intractable vomiting that is disproportionate to her mild gait unsteadiness. Which structure's involvement best explains the prominent vomiting?","choices":{"A":"The area postrema, a chemoreceptor trigger zone lacking a blood-brain barrier","B":"The inferior olivary complex, a precerebellar relay nucleus lacking cerebellar input","C":"The nucleus ambiguus, a branchial efferent nucleus lacking parasympathetic fibers","D":"The medullary reticular formation, an autonomic center lacking respiratory neurons","E":"The hypoglossal nucleus, a somatic efferent nucleus lacking sensory afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The area postrema sits in the wall of the fourth ventricle at the obex and, lacking an intact blood-brain barrier, functions as a chemoreceptor trigger zone that can generate intractable vomiting when focally irritated or damaged, a presentation disproportionate to the mild ataxia from incidental nearby structures. The inferior olivary complex does receive extensive cerebellar-directed input rather than lacking it, the nucleus ambiguus does contribute to autonomic reflex arcs via its role in swallowing rather than lacking parasympathetic connections, the reticular formation does contain respiratory neurons rather than lacking them, and the hypoglossal nucleus is a pure motor column whose absence of sensory afferents is unrelated to vomiting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vomiting out of proportion to other neurologic findings at the level of the obex is a classic clue pointing to area postrema involvement rather than a larger structural lesion. COMLEX: The area postrema's role as a circumventricular organ without a blood-brain barrier is the same mechanism exploited by antiemetic and chemotherapy pharmacology discussions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — identifying the solitary nucleus's location along the floor of the fourth ventricle near the obex, the same region housing the area postrema implicated in this vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_bloom_q01","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man develops right hemiparesis, loss of vibration and position sense on the right, and deviation of the tongue to the left, with the right side of the medulla spared. Although the anterior spinal artery runs in the midline, its occlusion usually produces this one-sided pattern rather than bilateral deficits. The one-sided pattern is best explained by occlusion of ___","choices":{"A":"the left vertebral artery proximal to its PICA branch","B":"the left PICA near its origin","C":"the left posterior spinal artery","D":"the left anterior inferior cerebellar artery","E":"left anterior spinal artery penetrating branches"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medial medullary structures, including the pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nucleus and roots, are served by penetrating branches of the anterior spinal artery, and these branches tend to alternate to the right and left. Occlusion of the penetrating branches to one side produces the unilateral medial medullary, or Dejerine, syndrome: contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral loss of proprioception and vibration, and ipsilateral tongue deviation; occlusion of the anterior spinal artery trunk itself can cause bilateral deficits. Vertebral and PICA occlusion produce the lateral medullary syndrome. The posterior spinal artery supplies the posterior medulla caudal to the obex, and the AICA supplies the caudal pons and cochlear nuclei.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial medullary syndrome follows unilateral anterior spinal artery penetrating branch occlusion: contralateral hemiparesis and lemniscal loss, ipsilateral tongue deviation. COMLEX: Bilateral tongue, limb, and lemniscal deficits point to the anterior spinal artery trunk instead.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7 — anterior spinal artery versus vertebral and PICA territory distinction applied to a paramedian medullary vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl16_bloom_q02","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man receiving cisplatin develops severe nausea and vomiting. Neurologic examination and brain MRI are normal. The circulating drug is detected by a structure on the caudal floor of the fourth ventricle, near the obex, whose capillaries lack a blood-brain barrier. That structure is the ___","choices":{"A":"area postrema","B":"nucleus ambiguus","C":"solitary nucleus","D":"inferior olive","E":"hypoglossal nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The area postrema is a circumventricular organ on the caudal floor of the fourth ventricle near the obex; its fenestrated capillaries let it sample blood-borne emetics such as chemotherapy drugs, so it acts as the chemoreceptor trigger zone and signals the adjacent solitary nucleus and medullary vomiting circuitry. The nucleus ambiguus is a branchial motor nucleus for the pharynx and larynx, the solitary nucleus receives taste and visceral afferents behind an intact blood-brain barrier and relays rather than detects circulating toxins, the inferior olive is a precerebellar relay in the ventral medulla, and the hypoglossal nucleus is a somatic motor nucleus for the tongue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the area postrema's fenestrated capillaries let it sample circulating toxins directly, which is why it triggers vomiting with chemotherapy, uremia, and small dorsal medullary lesions alike. COMLEX: distinguish this central vomiting mechanism from peripheral vestibular or vagal causes of nausea.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — structure-specific medullary lesion findings applied to area postrema and inferior olive vignettes","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl16_bloom_q03","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":3,"stem":"Several months after a brainstem stroke, a 61-year-old man develops a new, rhythmic, involuntary movement of the soft palate, noted incidentally on exam, without any new weakness or sensory change. MRI shows T2 hyperintensity and enlargement of a structure in the ventral medulla, just lateral to the pyramid. This patient's palatal tremor is most likely caused by hypertrophic degeneration of the ___","choices":{"A":"dentate nucleus","B":"red nucleus","C":"inferior olive","D":"nucleus ambiguus","E":"vestibular nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Delayed-onset palatal tremor after a brainstem stroke is caused by hypertrophic olivary degeneration, in which disruption of the dentato-rubro-olivary pathway, the Guillain-Mollaret triangle, leads to transsynaptic hypertrophy and enlargement of the inferior olive, which sits ventrolaterally in the medulla just lateral to the pyramid and correlates with the described imaging. The dentate nucleus and red nucleus are the other two corners of this triangle but lie in the cerebellum and midbrain, not the ventral medulla, and neither develops the characteristic delayed hypertrophy itself. The nucleus ambiguus and vestibular nuclei are anatomically unrelated to this circuit and do not produce palatal tremor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic olivary degeneration with delayed palatal tremor after a brainstem or cerebellar lesion is a classic, testable Guillain-Mollaret triangle vignette. COMLEX: recognize that the tremor can persist for years and is often mistaken for a new stroke on repeat imaging if the enlarging olive is misread.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — structure-specific medullary lesion findings applied to area postrema and inferior olive vignettes","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl16_bloom_q04","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has ipsilateral facial weakness, ipsilateral hearing loss, vertigo, and ipsilateral limb ataxia, but no hoarseness, dysphagia, or palatal weakness. Which vessel territory is most likely involved, and why does this pattern argue against PICA?","choices":{"A":"posterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"anterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"superior cerebellar artery","D":"anterior spinal artery","E":"basilar artery, mid-basilar perforators"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Facial weakness and hearing loss require involvement of the facial motor nucleus and cochlear nuclei, both pontine rather than medullary structures supplied by the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, so their presence, combined with the absence of nucleus ambiguus findings like hoarseness and dysphagia, points away from PICA and toward AICA territory instead. PICA supplies the medullary nucleus ambiguus and would be expected to produce hoarseness and dysphagia, which are absent here, the superior cerebellar artery supplies rostral cerebellum and midbrain without producing facial weakness or hearing loss, the anterior spinal artery supplies the paramedian medulla and would not explain facial or cochlear findings, and diffuse mid-basilar perforators would be expected to produce a broader, bilateral pontine picture rather than this specific unilateral pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facial weakness plus hearing loss localizes to the pons and AICA territory, not the medulla and PICA, a key vessel-versus-nerve-nucleus distinction tested against lateral medullary syndrome. COMLEX: absence of hoarseness and dysphagia is a useful negative finding that helps exclude nucleus ambiguus, and therefore PICA, involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7 — vertebral artery versus PICA territory distinction applied to a combined medullary/cerebellar vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl16_bloom_q05","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a lateral medullary syndrome from occlusion near the origin of the left PICA also has loss of vibration and position sense in the left limbs. MRI shows that the infarct extends into the posterior medulla caudal to the obex. This extension is best explained because the artery supplying that region usually arises from the ___","choices":{"A":"basilar artery","B":"anterior spinal artery","C":"anterior inferior cerebellar artery","D":"posterior inferior cerebellar artery","E":"superior cerebellar artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Caudal to the obex, the posterior medulla, including the gracile and cuneate nuclei and the spinal trigeminal tract, is served by the posterior spinal artery, which is usually a branch of the PICA and in the remaining minority arises from the vertebral artery. A proximal PICA occlusion can therefore also infarct the posterior column nuclei, causing ipsilateral loss of proprioception and vibration on the body. The basilar artery and its branches, the AICA and superior cerebellar artery, supply the pons, cerebellum, and midbrain rather than the caudal dorsal medulla. The anterior spinal artery supplies the paramedian medulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior spinal artery origin: usually PICA (about 75% per the course), otherwise the vertebral artery. COMLEX: Extra posterior column signs in a Wallenberg-type stroke point to a proximal PICA or vertebral occlusion that has taken the posterior spinal artery with it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7 — vertebral artery versus PICA territory distinction applied to a combined lateral and posterior medullary vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl16_bloom_q06","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict clinical findings from lesions involving nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus/fascicles, area postrema, inferior olive, and medullary reticular formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has a wet, gurgling voice and coughs whenever drinking liquids, without any limb weakness or sensory change. Which structure's involvement best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"nucleus ambiguus","B":"hypoglossal nucleus","C":"area postrema","D":"inferior olive","E":"medullary reticular formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus provides branchial efferent innervation to the pharynx and larynx via cranial nerves IX and X, so its damage causes vocal cord and palatal weakness, producing a wet, gurgling voice and coughing with liquids from impaired airway protection during swallowing. The hypoglossal nucleus would instead cause tongue weakness and dysarthria rather than a wet voice, the area postrema would cause vomiting rather than swallowing dysfunction, the inferior olive would cause ataxia via disrupted cerebellar input, and the medullary reticular formation would cause cardiorespiratory instability rather than an isolated swallowing problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a wet or gurgling voice with liquid-predominant coughing is the classic bedside sign of nucleus ambiguus dysfunction and resulting aspiration risk after a lateral medullary lesion. COMLEX: a formal swallow evaluation is indicated whenever nucleus ambiguus dysfunction is suspected, given the direct aspiration risk this finding implies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — structure-specific medullary lesion findings applied to area postrema and inferior olive vignettes","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms the anterior, transversely oriented bulk of the pons and gives the pons its name, Latin for bridge?","choices":{"A":"The pontine tegmentum, whose longitudinal fibers connect the midbrain to the medulla","B":"The rhomboid fossa, whose ependymal surface forms the floor of the fourth ventricle","C":"The crus cerebri, whose descending fibers arise entirely within the diencephalon","D":"The restiform body, whose fibers sweep posteriorly into the cerebellum caudally","E":"The basilar pons, whose transverse fibers enter the middle cerebellar peduncle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Anteriorly and laterally, the pons consists of a massive bundle of transversely running pontocerebellar fibers that enter the cerebellum as the middle cerebellar peduncle, and this transverse bridging arrangement is the origin of the name pons. The pontine tegmentum instead lies posterior to the basilar pons and carries longitudinal ascending and descending pathways rather than the transverse fibers that define the basilar pons, the rhomboid fossa is the dorsal ventricular floor rather than the anterior bulk, the crus cerebri is a midbrain structure that continues into the basilar pons rather than arising in the diencephalon, and the restiform body belongs to the medulla rather than the pons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The basilar pons and its transverse pontocerebellar fibers feeding the middle cerebellar peduncle explain both the pons's name and why basilar pontine lesions can produce ataxia on the same side as the hemiparesis (ataxic hemiparesis). COMLEX: Recognizing the basilar pons as the anterior, motor and cerebellar relay portion helps localize weakness plus ataxia to a ventral pontine lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — identifying medullary external landmarks such as the pyramids and olives, the same landmark-based approach applied here one level rostrally to the basilar pons and rhomboid fossa","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":1,"stem":"The exit of which cranial nerve marks the anatomic transition from the basilar pons anteriorly to the middle cerebellar peduncle posteriorly?","choices":{"A":"The abducens nerve, whose root exits far more medially at the pontomedullary junction","B":"The trigeminal nerve, whose root lies at this basilar pons to peduncle boundary","C":"The facial nerve, whose root exits lateral to the abducens at the pontomedullary junction","D":"The vestibulocochlear nerve, whose root exits posterolateral to the facial nerve","E":"The optic nerve, whose fibers never actually pass through the pons at any level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The trigeminal nerve exits laterally at midpontine levels, and this exit point marks the transition from the basilar pons, which lies anterior to the trigeminal root, to the middle cerebellar peduncle, which lies posterior to it. The abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nerves instead emerge in medial to lateral sequence along the caudal pons-medulla junction rather than at this midpontine basilar-peduncle transition, and the optic nerve is a diencephalic structure that never traverses the brainstem at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trigeminal root exit at the basilar pons to middle cerebellar peduncle transition is a classic imaging and dissection landmark for midpontine level. COMLEX: This landmark helps localize a mass or lesion to midpontine level based on which structures it displaces on imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — identifying medullary external landmarks such as the pyramids and olives, the same landmark-based approach applied here one level rostrally to the basilar pons and rhomboid fossa","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":1,"stem":"The facial colliculus, a paired elevation on the floor of the fourth ventricle in the caudal pons, is formed by which underlying structures?","choices":{"A":"The abducens nucleus with the looping internal genu of the facial nerve","B":"The vestibular nuclei and the entering root of the vestibulocochlear nerve","C":"The trigeminal motor nucleus and the exiting root of the trigeminal nerve","D":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus and the descending spinal trigeminal tract","E":"The superior olivary nucleus and the crossing fibers of the trapezoid body"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The facial colliculus lies medial to the sulcus limitans on the ventricular floor and is formed by the underlying abducens nucleus together with the internal genu, the looping intrapontine segment, of the facial nerve fibers that wrap around it before exiting the brainstem. The vestibular area lateral to the sulcus limitans instead marks the vestibular nuclei rather than the facial colliculus, the trigeminal motor nucleus and root lie at more rostral, midpontine levels rather than forming this caudal elevation, the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus extend caudally toward the medulla rather than forming the facial colliculus, and the superior olivary nucleus and trapezoid body are auditory relay structures unrelated to this elevation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The facial colliculus, abducens nucleus plus the internal genu of facial nerve fibers, is a frequently tested example of why a single caudal pontine lesion can damage both CN VI and CN VII together. COMLEX: A lesion at the facial colliculus level can produce combined ipsilateral lateral rectus and facial muscle weakness from one localized injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — identifying medullary external landmarks such as the pyramids and olives, the same landmark-based approach applied here one level rostrally to the basilar pons and rhomboid fossa","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pontine cranial nerve nucleus lies most medially, directly beneath the facial colliculus in the caudal pontine tegmentum?","choices":{"A":"The facial motor nucleus, whose cell bodies lie ventrolateral to the abducens nucleus","B":"The abducens nucleus, whose somatic efferent cells form the facial colliculus","C":"The principal sensory trigeminal nucleus, positioned at more rostral midpontine levels","D":"The superior vestibular nucleus, positioned laterally within the rostral rhomboid fossa","E":"The dorsal cochlear nucleus, positioned at the pontomedullary junction laterally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The abducens nucleus sits in the most medial position of the caudal pontine tegmentum, directly beneath the midline facial colliculus, and its somatic efferent axons pass anteriorly through the tegmentum to exit at the pontomedullary junction. The facial motor nucleus lies more ventrolateral in the tegmentum, its axons loop dorsomedially around the abducens nucleus as the internal genu before exiting laterally, the principal sensory trigeminal nucleus is found at more rostral, midpontine levels rather than caudally and medially, and the vestibular and cochlear nuclei both lie laterally within the rhomboid fossa or at the pontomedullary junction rather than medially.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The abducens nucleus's medial position under the facial colliculus, with the facial nerve looping around it, is the anatomic basis for combined CN VI and VII findings in caudal pontine lesions. COMLEX: A lesion producing isolated ipsilateral lateral rectus weakness without facial weakness suggests damage to the exiting abducens fascicle rather than the nucleus itself.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying medullary cranial nerve nuclei as discrete functional columns, extended here to the pontine nuclei of CN V, VI, VII, and VIII","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":1,"stem":"Which trigeminal nuclear component extends most rostrally, continuing into the midbrain to convey proprioceptive input from the muscles of mastication?","choices":{"A":"The principal sensory trigeminal nucleus, positioned at midpontine levels only","B":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus, which instead extends caudally into the medulla","C":"The trigeminal motor nucleus, which lies medial to the principal sensory nucleus","D":"The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus and tract, extending into the midbrain","E":"The trigeminal ganglion, which lies entirely outside the brainstem itself"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus and tract is unique among the trigeminal nuclear components in extending rostrally from the pons into the midbrain, and it uniquely conveys proprioceptive, somatic afferent input from the muscles of mastication and other structures using pseudounipolar cell bodies located within the central nervous system itself. The principal sensory nucleus is confined to midpontine levels, the spinal trigeminal nucleus extends caudally into the medulla rather than rostrally into the midbrain, the trigeminal motor nucleus lies at midpontine levels medial to the principal sensory nucleus without extending into the midbrain, and the trigeminal ganglion is a peripheral structure outside the brainstem entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The mesencephalic nucleus is the classic exception of a primary sensory neuron cell body located within the central nervous system, tested as a proprioceptive relay for jaw muscles. COMLEX: This proprioceptive input from the mesencephalic nucleus underlies the jaw jerk reflex used at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying medullary cranial nerve nuclei as discrete functional columns, extended here to the pontine nuclei of CN V, VI, VII, and VIII","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":1,"stem":"The vestibular and cochlear nuclei that receive input from cranial nerve VIII are located in which general pontine region?","choices":{"A":"Medially, immediately beneath the facial colliculus alongside the abducens nucleus","B":"Anteriorly, embedded in the transverse pontocerebellar fibers of the basilar pons","C":"Laterally, vestibular nuclei rostrally, cochlear nuclei near the medullary junction","D":"Rostrally, at midpontine levels alongside the principal sensory trigeminal nucleus","E":"Centrally, within the medial longitudinal fasciculus adjacent to the midline raphe"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The vestibular nuclei occupy the vestibular area lateral to the sulcus limitans in the rostral rhomboid fossa, and the cochlear nuclei sit laterally near the pontomedullary junction and restiform body, consistent with the vestibulocochlear nerve's posterolateral entry point into the brainstem. These nuclei are not medial beneath the facial colliculus, which instead marks the abducens nucleus, not anterior within the basilar pons, which contains corticospinal and pontocerebellar fibers rather than sensory nuclei, not confined to rostral midpontine trigeminal levels, and not embedded within the medial longitudinal fasciculus or raphe, which are fiber tracts and serotonergic cell groups rather than auditory or vestibular relay nuclei.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lateral position of the vestibular and cochlear nuclei, matching the posterolateral entry of CN VIII, explains why lateral pontine and cerebellopontine angle lesions produce vertigo, nystagmus, and hearing loss. COMLEX: Testing the vestibulo-ocular reflex at the bedside directly probes the integrity of these laterally positioned vestibular nuclei and their brainstem connections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying medullary cranial nerve nuclei as discrete functional columns, extended here to the pontine nuclei of CN V, VI, VII, and VIII","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":1,"stem":"As the medial lemniscus ascends from the caudal to the rostral pons, its orientation and somatotopic arrangement undergo which change?","choices":{"A":"It rotates from a horizontal orientation to a vertical one, with the arm representation shifting medially","B":"It rotates from vertical to horizontal, with the leg representation shifting laterally","C":"It remains vertically oriented throughout the pons with no somatotopic rearrangement","D":"It crosses the midline a second time at midpontine levels before reaching the thalamus","E":"It merges directly with the spinothalamic tract to form a single combined pathway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The medial lemniscus is oriented vertically in the medulla, but as it ascends through the caudal pons it begins rotating, so that at rostral pontine levels it lies horizontally, and during this rotation its lower extremity representation shifts laterally while the cervicothoracic representation shifts more medially. It does not rotate from horizontal to vertical, does not remain fixed in a single vertical orientation throughout the pons, does not undergo a second midline decussation at pontine levels since it already crossed in the medulla, and does not merge with the spinothalamic tract, which remains a separate, more laterally placed pathway throughout the brainstem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medial lemniscus's vertical-to-horizontal rotation with its shifting somatotopy is a classic cross-sectional anatomy detail used to identify pontine level on a labeled brainstem slide. COMLEX: Recognizing this somatotopic shift helps predict which body regions are affected when a lesion involves only part of the rotated medial lemniscus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinothalamic pathway within medullary cross-sections, traced here as they continue rostrally through the pons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":1,"stem":"The corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers descending from the cerebral hemispheres pass through which specific pontine region?","choices":{"A":"The pontine tegmentum, running longitudinally alongside the medial lemniscus","B":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, alongside the crossing pontocerebellar fibers","C":"The rhomboid fossa, immediately beneath the ependymal lining of the ventricle","D":"The basilar pons, interspersed among and broken into bundles by the pontine nuclei","E":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, decussating in the rostral pontine tegmentum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers descend from the forebrain through the basilar pons, where the massive bundle of longitudinally running fibers is broken into discrete fascicles by the interspersed transverse pontocerebellar fibers and pontine nuclei, before these fibers reconstitute more compactly as the pyramids in the medulla. They do not travel within the pontine tegmentum, which instead carries the medial lemniscus, anterolateral system, and reticular formation, nor within the middle cerebellar peduncle itself, which carries only crossing pontocerebellar fibers, nor beneath the rhomboid fossa, and the superior cerebellar peduncle is a purely cerebellar efferent pathway unrelated to corticospinal or corticobulbar fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The corticospinal tract's fragmentation into scattered bundles within the basilar pons, then reconsolidation as the medullary pyramid, is a classic explanation for why pontine lesions can spare some limb function while medullary lesions produce more uniform hemiparesis. COMLEX: This fragmented course through the basilar pons also explains why small paramedian pontine infarcts can produce a pure motor hemiparesis without other deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinothalamic pathway within medullary cross-sections, traced here as they continue rostrally through the pons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":1,"stem":"Pontocerebellar fibers arising from the basilar pontine nuclei reach the cerebellum by which route?","choices":{"A":"They cross the midline and enter the cerebellum via the contralateral middle cerebellar peduncle","B":"They ascend ipsilaterally and enter the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle","C":"They descend ipsilaterally and enter the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle","D":"They cross the midline and enter the cerebellum via the contralateral inferior cerebellar peduncle","E":"They remain ipsilateral throughout and enter via the ipsilateral middle cerebellar peduncle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Axons from the basilar pontine nuclei cross the midline within the basilar pons and enter the cerebellum via the contralateral middle cerebellar peduncle, or brachium pontis, which is why a unilateral cerebral hemisphere lesion produces ataxia on the same side as the body weakness through this crossed corticopontocerebellar relay. They do not ascend to enter via the superior cerebellar peduncle, which instead carries mostly cerebellar efferent fibers, do not descend to enter via the inferior cerebellar peduncle, which carries olivocerebellar and other afferents, do not cross to reach the inferior cerebellar peduncle at all, and do not remain ipsilateral, since the crossing is the defining feature of this pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The crossed corticopontocerebellar pathway, cortex to contralateral pontine nuclei to contralateral cerebellar hemisphere via the middle cerebellar peduncle, explains why a cerebral hemisphere lesion causes ataxia on the same side as the resulting hemiparesis. COMLEX: This double-crossing circuit is a favorite conceptual pearl for explaining why cerebellar signs from a cortical lesion appear ipsilateral to the weakness rather than contralateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and spinothalamic pathway within medullary cross-sections, traced here as they continue rostrally through the pons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of paramedian branches of the basilar artery at caudal pontine levels classically produces which combination of deficits?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral facial palsy, ipsilateral limb ataxia, and contralateral pain loss","B":"Ipsilateral deafness with ipsilateral vertigo and contralateral limb weakness","C":"Ipsilateral abducens palsy with contralateral hemiparesis, the Foville syndrome","D":"Contralateral facial numbness with ipsilateral mastication muscle paralysis","E":"Bilateral horizontal gaze palsy with bilateral limb weakness and numbness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paramedian branches of the basilar artery at caudal pontine levels supply the exiting abducens fibers, the corticospinal fibers, and often part of the medial lemniscus, so their occlusion classically produces an ipsilateral abducens palsy together with a contralateral hemiparesis and variable contralateral sensory loss, the Foville syndrome, sometimes with an added ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy if the lesion extends into the tegmentum. Ipsilateral facial palsy with ataxia and contralateral pain loss instead describes a lateral tegmental syndrome, ipsilateral deafness and vertigo with contralateral weakness mixes structures not shared by one small paramedian territory, contralateral facial numbness with ipsilateral mastication paralysis describes an unrelated crossed trigeminal pattern, and bilateral findings do not match a unilateral paramedian branch occlusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Foville syndrome, ipsilateral CN VI palsy plus contralateral hemiparesis from paramedian basilar artery branches, is a classic named medial pontine syndrome tested alongside Millard-Gubler. COMLEX: The ipsilateral gaze palsy addition, when the lesion extends dorsally into the tegmentum, distinguishes a Foville variant from a pure abducens nerve lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome, the same organizing logic applied here one level rostrally to medial versus lateral pontine syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":1,"stem":"When a caudal paramedian pontine lesion shifts slightly laterally to include the exiting facial nerve root along with corticospinal fibers, which named syndrome results?","choices":{"A":"Foville syndrome, with ipsilateral abducens palsy and contralateral hemiparesis","B":"Weber syndrome, with ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral hemiparesis","C":"Wallenberg syndrome, with ipsilateral ataxia and contralateral pain loss","D":"Benedikt syndrome, with ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral tremor","E":"Millard-Gubler syndrome, with ipsilateral facial paralysis and contralateral hemiparesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When the paramedian lesion extends slightly laterally to involve the exiting root of the facial nerve alongside the corticospinal fibers, the result is Millard-Gubler syndrome, producing ipsilateral paralysis of the facial muscles together with a contralateral hemiparesis. Foville syndrome instead involves the abducens fibers rather than the facial root at a more purely paramedian location, Weber syndrome is a midbrain syndrome involving the oculomotor nerve and crus cerebri rather than the pons, Wallenberg syndrome is the lateral medullary syndrome rather than a pontine syndrome, and Benedikt syndrome is another midbrain syndrome involving the oculomotor nerve and red nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Millard-Gubler syndrome, ipsilateral CN VII palsy plus contralateral hemiparesis from a laterally shifted caudal paramedian lesion, is frequently paired on exams with Foville syndrome to test precise localization of the facial versus abducens fibers. COMLEX: Distinguishing Millard-Gubler from a peripheral Bell palsy requires finding the accompanying contralateral hemiparesis, which localizes the lesion centrally to the pons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome, the same organizing logic applied here one level rostrally to medial versus lateral pontine syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":1,"stem":"A lateral pontine syndrome from occlusion of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery classically spares which function that is prominently affected in medial pontine syndromes?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral limb strength, since the corticospinal fibers lie in the spared basilar pons","B":"Ipsilateral limb coordination, since the middle cerebellar peduncle lies outside the AICA territory","C":"Ipsilateral facial sensation, since the trigeminal nuclei lie outside the AICA territory","D":"Contralateral body pain and temperature sensation, since the anterolateral system is spared","E":"Ipsilateral hearing, since the cochlear nuclei lie outside the AICA territory"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the dorsolateral pontine tegmentum rather than the ventral basilar pons, the corticospinal fibers are spared, so contralateral limb strength remains largely intact in a lateral pontine syndrome, in contrast to the prominent contralateral hemiparesis that defines medial pontine syndromes such as Foville and Millard-Gubler. Ipsilateral limb coordination is not spared, since the middle cerebellar peduncle lies within AICA territory and its damage produces ipsilateral ataxia, ipsilateral facial sensation is not spared, since the trigeminal nuclei lie within the affected tegmentum, contralateral body pain and temperature sensation is not spared, since the anterolateral system also lies within the tegmentum, and ipsilateral hearing is not spared, since the cochlear nuclei and AICA-supplied labyrinthine circulation lie within this same lateral territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preserved limb strength alongside ipsilateral facial, vestibulocochlear, and ataxia findings is the key discriminator that separates a lateral pontine, AICA, syndrome from a medial pontine, paramedian basilar, syndrome. COMLEX: Testing limb strength at the bedside is a fast first step to sort a suspected pontine stroke into a medial versus lateral vascular territory before imaging confirms it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome, the same organizing logic applied here one level rostrally to medial versus lateral pontine syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A neurosurgeon reviewing a sagittal MRI before resecting a tumor in the pontine part of the fourth ventricle needs to know which structures bound that space. The floor is the posterior surface of the pontine tegmentum. Which structures form the lateral walls and roof of the pontine fourth ventricle?","choices":{"A":"Lateral walls of thalamus and hypothalamus, with a roof of tela choroidea from the fornix","B":"Lateral walls of the brachium pontis and conjunctivum, with a roof of medullary velum","C":"Lateral walls of the restiform bodies, with a roof formed entirely by the cerebellar vermis","D":"Lateral walls of the cerebral peduncles, with a roof formed by the tectal plate","E":"Lateral walls of the olives and pyramids, with a roof of the posterior commissure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the pons, the brachium pontis (middle cerebellar peduncle) and the brachium conjunctivum (superior cerebellar peduncle) form the lateral walls of the fourth ventricle, and the roof is formed by the anterior medullary velum, a small part of the cerebellum, and a portion of the tela choroidea; the floor is the rhomboid fossa on the posterior pontine tegmentum. The thalamus and hypothalamus bound the third ventricle. The restiform bodies border the medullary part of the ventricle, and its roof is not the vermis alone. The cerebral peduncles and tectum belong to the midbrain around the aqueduct, and the olives and pyramids are anterior medullary surface features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fourth ventricle in the pons: floor is the tegmentum (facial colliculus, vestibular area), lateral walls are the middle and superior cerebellar peduncles, roof is the anterior medullary velum and cerebellum. COMLEX: Fourth-ventricle tumors in children, such as ependymoma and medulloblastoma, press on these walls and can cause obstructive hydrocephalus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — major components of the ventricular system and their surrounding neuroanatomic relationships","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":2,"stem":"During a posterior fossa dissection with the cerebellum reflected, a student identifies a paired elevation on the ventricular floor at the level of the caudal pons, flanked laterally by an area marking sensory nuclei related to balance. What is this elevation called, and what forms its lateral border?","choices":{"A":"The gracile tubercle, with the cuneate tubercle forming its lateral border instead","B":"The area postrema, with the vagal trigone forming its lateral border instead","C":"The trigeminal impression, with the trigeminal ganglion forming its lateral border","D":"The facial colliculus, its border formed by the sulcus limitans, vestibular area","E":"The hypoglossal trigone, with the vagal trigone forming its lateral border instead"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The paired midline elevation described is the facial colliculus, formed by the underlying abducens nucleus and internal genu of the facial nerve, and its lateral border is marked by the sulcus limitans, beyond which lies the vestibular area overlying the vestibular nuclei. The gracile and cuneate tubercles are medullary, not pontine, dorsal column landmarks, the area postrema and vagal trigone are also medullary structures near the obex, the trigeminal impression and ganglion describe a peripheral, extradural landmark rather than an intraventricular floor elevation, and the hypoglossal trigone is likewise a medullary rather than pontine ventricular floor landmark.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Correctly distinguishing pontine ventricular floor landmarks, facial colliculus and vestibular area, from medullary ones, hypoglossal and vagal trigones, is a common labeled-diagram exam trap. COMLEX: This same floor anatomy explains why a fourth ventricular tumor near the facial colliculus can present with combined CN VI and VII findings before causing hydrocephalus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — identifying the major components of the ventricular system, the same fourth ventricle floor landmarks now localized specifically to the pontine rhomboid fossa","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoes MRI for chronic headache, and the radiologist describes a small pontine cavernoma located in the anterior, transversely fibered portion of the pons anterior to the trigeminal root, sparing the tegmentum. In which pontine region does this lesion lie, and what is its primary fiber composition?","choices":{"A":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, composed exclusively of cerebellar efferent fibers","B":"The pontine tegmentum, composed of the medial lemniscus, anterolateral system, and cranial nerve nuclei","C":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, composed exclusively of crossed afferent cerebellar fibers","D":"The rhomboid fossa, composed of ependymal lining overlying the fourth ventricle","E":"The basilar pons, composed of transverse pontocerebellar fibers and descending corticospinal fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A lesion in the anterior, transversely fibered portion of the pons that lies anterior to the trigeminal root and spares the tegmentum is located in the basilar pons, which is composed of transverse pontocerebellar fibers running toward the middle cerebellar peduncle interspersed with the longitudinally descending corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers. The tegmentum lies posterior to this description and carries sensory pathways and cranial nerve nuclei rather than being anterior to the trigeminal root, the middle cerebellar peduncle lies posterior to the trigeminal root exit rather than anterior to it, the rhomboid fossa is the dorsal ventricular floor rather than an anterior fiber region, and the superior cerebellar peduncle is a separate, more medially placed efferent structure unrelated to this anterior location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Localizing a lesion to the basilar pons based on its anterior position relative to the trigeminal root and its transverse fiber composition is a high-yield radiology-anatomy correlation. COMLEX: A basilar pons lesion sparing the tegmentum would be expected to spare sensory and cranial nerve nuclear function while still risking corticospinal and pontocerebellar involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — explaining structural transitions within the medulla, extended here to distinguishing the anterior basilar pons from the posterior pontine tegmentum on imaging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with acute onset diplopia on rightward gaze and is found to have isolated weakness of the right lateral rectus muscle without any other cranial nerve or long tract findings. Imaging shows a small lesion confined to the exiting fascicle of a single cranial nerve near the pontomedullary junction. Which nerve and nuclear origin best explain this isolated finding?","choices":{"A":"The trochlear nerve, arising from a nucleus located in the caudal midbrain rather than the pons","B":"The facial nerve, arising from a nucleus located ventrolateral to the abducens nucleus","C":"The abducens nerve, arising from the medial abducens nucleus beneath the facial colliculus","D":"The oculomotor nerve, arising from a nucleus located in the rostral midbrain rather than the pons","E":"The trigeminal nerve, arising from nuclei located at more rostral midpontine levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isolated lateral rectus weakness localizes to the abducens nerve, whose somatic efferent cell bodies form the medially placed abducens nucleus beneath the facial colliculus in the caudal pontine tegmentum, and a small lesion confined to its exiting fascicle near the pontomedullary junction can produce this deficit in isolation without other findings. The trochlear nerve controls the superior oblique rather than the lateral rectus and arises from the caudal midbrain, the facial nerve controls facial expression rather than eye abduction and arises more ventrolaterally, the oculomotor nerve controls most other extraocular muscles and arises from the rostral midbrain, and the trigeminal nerve has no role in ocular motility and arises from more rostral pontine nuclei.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated abducens fascicle lesions producing pure lateral rectus weakness without other findings is a classic contrast case to Foville syndrome, in which the lesion extends to also involve corticospinal fibers. COMLEX: Isolated CN VI palsy in an older adult should prompt consideration of microvascular ischemia as a common benign cause before assuming a structural pontine lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — identifying medullary cranial nerve nuclei as discrete functional cell columns, the same nuclear localization principle applied here to isolate an abducens fascicular lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man develops loss of pain and temperature sensation over the ipsilateral face along with weakness of the ipsilateral muscles of mastication, but light touch over the face remains intact. Damage confined to which trigeminal component best explains this dissociated pattern?","choices":{"A":"The principal sensory nucleus alone, sparing the spinal trigeminal tract and motor nucleus","B":"The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus alone, sparing the spinal trigeminal tract and motor nucleus","C":"The entire trigeminal ganglion peripherally, sparing all central trigeminal nuclei","D":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract with the motor nucleus, sparing the principal sensory nucleus","E":"The anterior trigeminothalamic tract alone, sparing all trigeminal nuclei and the ganglion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain and temperature sensation from the face travel via the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus, while light touch travels via the principal sensory nucleus and jaw proprioception via the mesencephalic nucleus, so a lesion damaging the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract together with the adjacent trigeminal motor nucleus while sparing the principal sensory nucleus produces exactly this dissociated pattern of lost pain and temperature and lost mastication strength with preserved touch. Damage to the principal sensory nucleus alone would instead impair touch while sparing pain and temperature and motor function, damage to the mesencephalic nucleus alone would impair jaw proprioception without affecting pain, temperature, touch, or motor strength directly, damage to the peripheral ganglion would produce a combined sensory loss across all modalities rather than this selective pattern, and damage to the anterior trigeminothalamic tract alone would not explain the ipsilateral, rather than crossed, distribution of the deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dissociated sensory loss of facial pain and temperature with preserved touch is the trigeminal analog of a classic anterolateral versus dorsal column dissociation, tested by comparing spinal trigeminal tract with principal sensory nucleus function. COMLEX: Bedside testing of facial pinprick versus light touch can localize a lesion to the spinal trigeminal tract specifically, guiding further pontine or medullary imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — identifying the spinal trigeminal nucleus as a medullary cranial nerve relay, its rostral pontine continuation and dissociated sensory role explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman develops sudden vertigo, nausea, and a sensorineural hearing loss on the right side. Examination reveals right-beating nystagmus and no other cranial nerve or limb findings. Damage to which pontine structures, given the entry point of the responsible nerve, best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The vestibular and cochlear nuclei, which receive the vestibulocochlear nerve entering posterolaterally","B":"The abducens and facial nuclei, which receive nerves exiting medially at the pontomedullary junction","C":"The trigeminal motor and mesencephalic nuclei, which receive input from a nerve entering laterally at midpontine levels","D":"The superior olivary nucleus and trapezoid body alone, without involvement of any cranial nerve nucleus","E":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus, which receives contributions from cranial nerves VII, IX, and X"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vertigo, nausea, and sensorineural hearing loss with nystagmus localize to the vestibular and cochlear nuclei, which receive the vestibulocochlear nerve as it enters the brainstem posterolaterally alongside the facial and intermediate nerves at the internal acoustic meatus. The abducens and facial nuclei instead produce eye abduction and facial expression deficits rather than vertigo and hearing loss, the trigeminal motor and mesencephalic nuclei govern mastication and jaw proprioception rather than hearing and balance, the superior olivary nucleus and trapezoid body alone would not explain the vestibular component of nystagmus and vertigo, and the spinal trigeminal nucleus governs facial pain and temperature sensation rather than vestibular or cochlear function despite receiving minor contributions from cranial nerve VII.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The posterolateral entry of the vestibulocochlear nerve alongside the facial and intermediate nerves at the internal acoustic meatus explains why cerebellopontine angle lesions often combine vestibulocochlear and facial nerve findings. COMLEX: Distinguishing a peripheral, labyrinthine cause of vertigo from a central, brainstem cause often hinges on whether other pontine cranial nerve or long tract findings accompany the vestibular symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — identifying the vestibular nuclei as a medullary and pontine cranial nerve relay, applied here to a purely vestibulocochlear presentation without other brainstem findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a small rostral pontine infarct sparing the basilar pons entirely. He is found to have contralateral loss of pain and temperature over the body along with contralateral loss of proprioception and vibration, but no limb weakness. Damage to which two pathways, both located within the pontine tegmentum, best explains this combined sensory pattern?","choices":{"A":"The corticospinal tract and the medial lemniscus, both located within the spared basilar pons","B":"The anterolateral system and the middle cerebellar peduncle, producing sensory loss and ataxia together","C":"The trigeminothalamic tract and the corticobulbar tract, producing facial rather than body sensory loss","D":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus and the superior cerebellar peduncle, producing gaze and coordination deficits","E":"The anterolateral system and the medial lemniscus, both tegmental sensory pathways sparing the basilar pons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both the anterolateral system, carrying pain and temperature, and the medial lemniscus, carrying proprioception and vibration, travel through the pontine tegmentum rather than the basilar pons, so a tegmental lesion sparing the basilar pons can damage both pathways together and produce combined contralateral pain, temperature, proprioceptive, and vibratory loss without weakness, since the corticospinal fibers in the spared basilar pons remain intact. The corticospinal tract lies within the basilar pons, which this lesion spares by definition, so it cannot be the explanation, the middle cerebellar peduncle produces ataxia rather than a proprioceptive sensory pattern, the trigeminothalamic and corticobulbar tracts would produce facial rather than body sensory findings, and the medial longitudinal fasciculus and superior cerebellar peduncle govern eye movement coordination and cerebellar efferents rather than body sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pure sensory pontine syndrome sparing motor function is explained by isolated tegmental damage to both the anterolateral system and medial lemniscus while the basilar pons and its corticospinal fibers remain intact. COMLEX: Distinguishing a pure sensory stroke from a mixed sensorimotor stroke by exam helps predict whether the lesion is confined to the tegmentum or also involves the basilar pons.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing the medial lemniscus and anterolateral system within medullary cross-sections, the same two pathways traced here through the pontine tegmentum in isolation from motor fibers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":2,"stem":"In a tract-tracing study, a researcher injects tracer into the cerebellar cortex and follows labeled spinocerebellar axons back toward the spinal cord. One group of fibers reaches the cerebellum by passing through the medulla in the restiform body, while the anterior spinocerebellar fibers travel through the pons and enter by a different route. By which route do the anterior spinocerebellar fibers enter the cerebellum?","choices":{"A":"Through the juxtarestiform body with the vestibulocerebellar fibers","B":"Through the contralateral middle cerebellar peduncle with pontocerebellar fibers","C":"Over the surface of the superior cerebellar peduncle in the rostral pons","D":"Through the medial lemniscus as it turns horizontal in the caudal pons","E":"Through the central tegmental tract with fibers bound for the inferior olive"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior spinocerebellar tract ascends through the medulla and into the pons, then migrates posteriorly and enters the cerebellum by coursing over the surface of the superior cerebellar peduncle, unlike the posterior spinocerebellar tract, which enters through the restiform body of the inferior peduncle. The juxtarestiform body carries vestibulocerebellar and cerebellovestibular fibers. The middle cerebellar peduncle carries crossed pontocerebellar fibers. The medial lemniscus ascends to the thalamus, and the central tegmental tract carries rubro-olivary fibers from the red nucleus to the inferior olive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior spinocerebellar enters by the inferior peduncle; anterior spinocerebellar enters over the superior peduncle; pontocerebellar fibers enter by the middle peduncle. COMLEX: Spinocerebellar degeneration, as in Friedreich ataxia, produces limb and gait ataxia from loss of this unconscious proprioceptive input.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — localizing ascending pathways within brainstem cross-sections, here the anterior spinocerebellar tract traced through the pons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has facial pain and temperature loss on the same side as his lesion, together with contralateral loss of pain and temperature over the trunk and limbs. Given that both the trigeminal and spinothalamic pathways ultimately convey pain and temperature information to the thalamus, which anatomic difference between them best explains the different laterality of these two findings?","choices":{"A":"Both pathways are ipsilateral at pontine levels, and the described contralateral body finding must reflect a separate lesion","B":"The trigeminal tract carries ipsilateral facial input, while the anterolateral system already carries crossed body input","C":"Both pathways are already crossed at pontine levels, so facial findings should also appear contralaterally","D":"The trigeminal pathway crosses in the pons while the spinothalamic pathway remains uncrossed until the thalamus","E":"Facial pain and temperature information does not reach the thalamus at any pontine or medullary level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The spinal trigeminal tract carries primary facial pain and temperature afferents ipsilaterally down into the spinal trigeminal nucleus before those second-order fibers cross to ascend as the anterior trigeminothalamic tract, so a pontine or medullary lesion of the tract itself produces ipsilateral facial sensory loss, whereas the anterolateral system carrying body pain and temperature has already crossed in the spinal cord before ascending through the pons, so the same lesion produces contralateral body sensory loss. Both pathways are not simply ipsilateral, since the anterolateral system is already crossed by pontine levels, both are not simply crossed at pontine levels, since the trigeminal tract itself remains ipsilateral until its second-order fibers cross, the trigeminal pathway does not cross before the spinothalamic pathway, since the reverse timing is correct, and facial pain and temperature information does reach the thalamus via the anterior trigeminothalamic tract.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The classic ipsilateral face, contralateral body pattern of a lateral brainstem lesion is explained by the trigeminal tract's late crossing compared to the spinothalamic tract's early spinal cord crossing, a high-yield localizing principle. COMLEX: This crossed pattern is the same logic used to localize lateral medullary and lateral pontine syndromes at the bedside using facial versus body sensory testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 6 — distinguishing medial from lateral medullary syndrome using ipsilateral face and contralateral body sensory patterns, the same crossing logic extended here to pontine level","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has an acute stroke with right arm and leg weakness, loss of pain and temperature sensation over the left face, weakness of the left masseter and temporalis, and left limb ataxia. Eye movements and hearing are normal. MRI shows an infarct in the anterior pons at the midpontine level. Which vascular territory and syndrome best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Caudal paramedian basilar branches, producing the Foville syndrome","B":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery, producing a caudal lateral pontine syndrome","C":"The superior cerebellar artery, producing a rostral lateral pontine syndrome","D":"Penetrating anterior spinal artery branches, producing a medial medullary syndrome","E":"Paramedian and short circumferential branches, producing the midpontine base syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At midpontine levels, occlusion of paramedian and short circumferential basilar branches produces the syndrome of the midpontine base: corticospinal fibers (contralateral hemiparesis), the sensory and motor trigeminal roots (ipsilateral loss of facial pain and temperature and paralysis of the masticatory muscles), and middle cerebellar peduncle fibers (ataxia). The Foville syndrome is caudal and includes an ipsilateral abducens palsy, absent here. AICA occlusion, a caudal lateral pontine syndrome, affects the lateral pons with hearing loss and facial paralysis and spares strength. SCA occlusion affects the rostral tegmentum and superior cerebellum without hemiparesis. A medial medullary syndrome causes ipsilateral tongue weakness rather than trigeminal signs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brainstem vascular hallmark: an ipsilateral cranial nerve sign plus a contralateral long tract sign; the cranial nerve tells the level (VI caudal pons, V midpons). COMLEX: Jaw deviation toward the weak side on opening plus contralateral hemiparesis localizes to the midpontine base.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — predicting deficits from basilar artery branch territories, applied here to the paramedian and short circumferential branches at midpons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man presents with acute vertigo, right-sided facial weakness, right-sided hearing loss, right-sided limb ataxia, and loss of pain and temperature sensation over the left arm and leg, but with normal strength in all four limbs. Which vascular territory and syndrome best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Paramedian branches of the basilar artery producing a medial pontine syndrome","B":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery producing a lateral pontine syndrome","C":"The posterior spinal artery producing a rare caudal medullary syndrome","D":"The anterior spinal artery producing a medial pontine syndrome variant","E":"The long circumferential branches of the basilar artery supplying only the basilar pons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ipsilateral facial weakness, hearing loss, and ataxia combined with contralateral body pain and temperature loss and preserved strength is the classic lateral pontine syndrome produced by occlusion of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, which supplies the dorsolateral tegmentum containing the facial nucleus, cochlear and vestibular nuclei, middle cerebellar peduncle, and anterolateral system while sparing the corticospinal fibers of the basilar pons. Paramedian basilar artery branches instead produce a medial pontine syndrome with contralateral weakness and ipsilateral abducens palsy rather than this constellation, the posterior spinal artery supplies the medulla rather than the pons, the anterior spinal artery also supplies the medulla and would not explain facial, auditory, or ataxic pontine findings, and the long circumferential branches alone would not account for the full territory affected, since they supply only the lateral basilar pons rather than the full dorsolateral tegmentum implicated here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ipsilateral face, ear, and cerebellar findings with contralateral body sensory loss but preserved strength is the hallmark of AICA-territory lateral pontine syndrome, distinguishing it from the weakness-predominant medial pontine syndromes. COMLEX: Preserved limb strength in a patient with acute vertigo and facial weakness should raise suspicion for a lateral, rather than medial, pontine vascular event.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 7 — relating medullary vascular territories to specific artery patterns, the same vascular-clinical correlation approach applied here to AICA territory in the pons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients present with acute brainstem strokes. Patient one has contralateral hemiparesis and ipsilateral abducens weakness with normal facial sensation and no ataxia. Patient two has ipsilateral facial sensory loss, ipsilateral limb ataxia, and contralateral body pain and temperature loss with normal limb strength. Which underlying anatomic principle best explains why these two presentations differ so sharply?","choices":{"A":"Medial paramedian territory holds corticospinal, abducens fibers; lateral tegmentum holds trigeminal, cerebellar fibers","B":"Both patients have lesions in the same vascular territory, and the differing findings simply reflect differing infarct sizes","C":"Patient one has a medullary lesion while patient two has a pontine lesion at a different brainstem level entirely","D":"The corticospinal tract and anterolateral system occupy the same paramedian territory in both patients","E":"The facial and abducens nuclei are supplied by the identical vascular territory in both patients"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The two presentations differ because the pons has two anatomically and vascularly distinct territories, a medial paramedian territory supplied by paramedian basilar artery branches containing the corticospinal fibers and exiting abducens fascicle, whose damage produces patient one's contralateral weakness and ipsilateral gaze palsy, and a lateral tegmental territory supplied by AICA containing the trigeminal nuclei, middle cerebellar peduncle, and anterolateral system, whose damage produces patient two's ipsilateral face and ataxia findings with contralateral body sensory loss but preserved strength. The two patients do not share one territory with differing infarct sizes, since their deficit patterns reflect fundamentally different structures rather than degree, both lesions are pontine rather than one being medullary, the corticospinal tract and anterolateral system do not share the same paramedian territory, since the anterolateral system is a lateral tegmental structure, and the facial and abducens nuclei are not supplied identically, since the abducens nucleus and fascicle lie medially in paramedian territory while the facial nucleus lies more laterally within the AICA-supplied tegmentum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing the pons as two distinct vascular and functional territories, medial paramedian and lateral tegmental, is the single organizing concept that predicts every combination of medial versus lateral pontine syndrome findings. COMLEX: A structured bedside exam covering eye movement, face, body sensation, strength, and coordination allows rapid sorting of a pontine stroke into medial or lateral territory to guide urgent management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 6 — distinguishing medial medullary syndrome from lateral medullary syndrome using the same medial-paramedian versus lateral-tegmental organizing logic now applied one level rostrally to the pons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with a 1-hour history of sudden-onset diplopia and right-sided weakness.\n- BP 168/94, HR 82, afebrile\n- Unable to abduct the left eye past midline; left eye adducts normally\n- Face symmetric, hearing intact, corneal reflexes intact bilaterally\n- Right arm and leg strength 3/5; left side 5/5\n- No limb ataxia, no nystagmus, no dysarthria\nThis patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___","choices":{"A":"circumferential basilar branches","B":"anterior inferior cerebellar","C":"paramedian basilar branches","D":"posterior inferior cerebellar","E":"superior cerebellar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The combination of an ipsilateral isolated abducens palsy, from involvement of the CN VI fascicle or nucleus near the midline, with a contralateral hemiparesis, from involvement of the adjacent corticospinal fibers in the basilar pons, is the classic crossed pattern of a medial pontine syndrome. This territory is supplied by the short paramedian branches of the basilar artery. The long circumferential branches supply the lateral pons rather than the paramedian territory, the anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the lateral caudal pons and would be expected to also affect facial strength, hearing, and produce ipsilateral ataxia, the posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the lateral medulla rather than the pons, and the superior cerebellar artery supplies the rostral pons-midbrain junction and cerebellum rather than the caudal paramedian territory responsible for this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paramedian basilar branch occlusion is the classic cause of a pure crossed motor syndrome, ipsilateral CN VI plus contralateral hemiparesis, without sensory, hearing, or cerebellar findings. COMLEX: Recognizing that sparing of face, hearing, and coordination argues against AICA and localizes instead to the paramedian territory is a frequently tested localization skill.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — crossed motor findings used to localize medial medullary syndrome, applied here one level rostrally to medial pontine syndrome","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q02","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman presents with a 2-hour history of sudden-onset vertigo and imbalance.\n- BP 142/88, HR 90, afebrile\n- Left facial weakness involving the forehead and lower face\n- Decreased hearing and tinnitus in the left ear\n- Left-sided limb and gait ataxia on finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing\n- Decreased pinprick and temperature sensation over the right trunk and limbs\n- Strength full and symmetric in all four limbs\nThis patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___","choices":{"A":"anterior inferior cerebellar","B":"posterior inferior cerebellar","C":"superior cerebellar","D":"paramedian basilar branches","E":"circumferential basilar branches"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, from involvement of the facial and vestibulocochlear nerve roots and nuclei, combined with ipsilateral limb ataxia, from middle cerebellar peduncle involvement, and contralateral body pain and temperature loss, from the spinothalamic tract in the lateral tegmentum, together with preserved strength, is the classic crossed pattern of a lateral pontine syndrome from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery instead supplies the lateral medulla, the superior cerebellar artery supplies the rostral pons-midbrain junction and would not typically involve CN VII or VIII, and the paramedian and circumferential basilar branches supply the medial pons and would produce hemiparesis rather than this facial, auditory, and ataxic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AICA infarction is the classic cause of a lateral pontine syndrome combining ipsilateral facial and vestibulocochlear findings with contralateral body pain and temperature loss and preserved strength. COMLEX: Preserved strength with ipsilateral facial droop plus hearing loss is a high-yield clue pointing away from a medial, motor-predominant syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — nucleus ambiguus and cranial nerve nucleus lesion correlation extended here to the pontine facial and vestibulocochlear nuclei","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes using deficits in eye movement, face, body sensation, motor function, and ataxia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old man presents with a 3-hour history of sudden-onset vertigo, imbalance, and facial droop.\n- Right facial weakness involving the forehead and lower face\n- Decreased hearing and absent corneal reflex on the right\n- Right-sided limb ataxia and dysmetria\n- Decreased pain and temperature sensation over the left trunk and limbs\n- Strength full and symmetric in all four limbs\nThe most likely additional finding in this patient is ___","choices":{"A":"contralateral facial weakness","B":"bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia","C":"ipsilateral hemiparesis","D":"ipsilateral Horner syndrome","E":"contralateral limb ataxia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This presentation localizes to a lateral pontine, anterior inferior cerebellar artery, territory lesion. The descending sympathetic fibers destined for the eye and face travel through the lateral brainstem tegmentum alongside the spinothalamic tract, so a lateral pontine lesion large enough to produce contralateral body pain and temperature loss frequently also interrupts these ipsilateral descending sympathetic fibers, producing an ipsilateral Horner syndrome with miosis and ptosis. Facial weakness from a lateral pontine lesion is ipsilateral, from direct nuclear or fascicular CN VII involvement, not contralateral, which would instead suggest a supranuclear corticobulbar lesion. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from medial longitudinal fasciculus damage, a medial rather than lateral pontine structure. Hemiparesis implicates the corticospinal fibers in the basilar pons, which is spared in a purely lateral, tegmental syndrome. Ataxia from a lateral pontine or cerebellar peduncle lesion is ipsilateral, not contralateral, to the lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ipsilateral Horner syndrome from descending sympathetic fiber involvement is a classic added feature of lateral brainstem syndromes wherever the spinothalamic tract is also affected. COMLEX: Recognizing miosis and ptosis on the same side as facial and auditory deficits, rather than the weak side, reinforces that Horner findings are always ipsilateral to a lateral brainstem lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — ipsilateral Horner syndrome from descending sympathetic fiber involvement in lateral medullary syndrome, the same principle applied here to lateral pontine syndrome","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":3,"stem":"On a ventral view of the brainstem, a student traces the three cranial nerves that emerge along the groove at the pons-medulla junction. Listed from medial to lateral, these nerves are the ___","choices":{"A":"facial, abducens, and vestibulocochlear nerves","B":"abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nerves","C":"vestibulocochlear, facial, and abducens nerves","D":"abducens, vestibulocochlear, and facial nerves","E":"hypoglossal, abducens, and facial nerves"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nerves emerge in medial-to-lateral sequence along the pons-medulla junction; the vestibulocochlear nerve emerges most posterolaterally and, with the facial and intermediate nerves and the labyrinthine artery, enters the internal acoustic meatus. The facial nerve lies between the other two, so orders placing it medial to the abducens or lateral to the vestibulocochlear nerve are wrong, and listing the vestibulocochlear nerve first reverses the sequence. The hypoglossal nerve exits the medulla from the preolivary sulcus, not the pons-medulla junction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pons-medulla junction, medial to lateral: VI, VII, VIII; the trigeminal nerve exits laterally from the midpons. COMLEX: Cerebellopontine angle masses compress the lateral pair, VII and VIII, first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — external medullary landmarks, extended here to the cranial nerve exits along the pons-medulla junction","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":3,"stem":"A neuropathologist finds a darkly pigmented cluster of neurons in the lateral floor of the fourth ventricle at the midpontine level, just anterior to the mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus. These roughly 15,000 cells project very widely to the cortex, diencephalon, cerebellum, brainstem, and spinal cord. This cell group is the ___","choices":{"A":"locus ceruleus","B":"raphe magnus","C":"substantia nigra","D":"facial colliculus","E":"superior olive"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The nucleus, or locus, ceruleus lies in the lateral floor of the fourth ventricle at midpontine levels, anterior to the mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus and tract, and extends into the caudal midbrain. Its pigmented neurons, also called the nucleus pigmentosus pontis, form the largest single group of norepinephrine-containing cells in the central nervous system; about 15,000 cells branch profusely to reach targets throughout the neuraxis. The raphe magnus is a serotonergic midline group at caudal pontine levels involved in pain inhibition. The substantia nigra is a pigmented midbrain nucleus, not in the pontine ventricular floor. The facial colliculus is a surface elevation over the abducens nucleus and facial genu, and the superior olive is an auditory nucleus in the central tegmentum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Locus ceruleus is the main noradrenergic nucleus; its activity is low in sleep and high with sudden threat or arousal. COMLEX: Altered locus ceruleus activity is linked to anxiety and arousal disorders, and it is affected early in some neurodegenerative diseases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — landmark-based identification on cross-section, applied here to a pigmented nucleus in the pontine ventricular floor","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal landmarks of the pons.","batch":3,"stem":"On a myelin-stained section through the caudal pons, a student must draw the line that separates the posterior tegmentum from the anterior basilar pons. At this level, the structure that forms the border between the tegmentum and the basilar pons is the ___","choices":{"A":"medial longitudinal fasciculus","B":"facial colliculus","C":"medial lemniscus","D":"superior cerebellar peduncle","E":"spinal trigeminal tract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the caudal pons the medial lemniscus begins to shift from its vertical medullary orientation to a horizontal band, and it forms the border between the tegmentum and the basilar pons. The medial longitudinal fasciculus lies near the midline in the floor of the fourth ventricle, at the posterior edge of the tegmentum. The facial colliculus is a surface elevation in the ventricular floor. The superior cerebellar peduncle forms the lateral wall of the fourth ventricle at rostral levels, and the spinal trigeminal tract lies in the lateral tegmentum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pontine tegmentum versus basilar pons: the horizontally oriented medial lemniscus marks the boundary; lesions anterior to it are pure motor, and lesions behind it add sensory and cranial nerve signs. COMLEX: Use the medial lemniscus as the dividing landmark when reading pontine MRI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — explaining structural transitions within the medulla, extended here to the tegmentum-basilar border of the pons","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":3,"stem":"Cranial nerve VII has two roots. One carries the motor fibers to the muscles of facial expression; the other carries taste fibers, preganglionic fibers to the lacrimal and salivary glands, and a small set of fibers for skin sensation near the ear. This second root is the ___","choices":{"A":"chorda tympani","B":"greater petrosal nerve","C":"facial genu","D":"intermediate nerve","E":"vestibular root"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cranial nerve VII is composed of two roots: the facial nerve proper, which carries the somatic efferent motor fibers, and the intermediate nerve, which carries visceral afferent taste fibers, visceral efferent fibers from the superior salivatory nucleus, and somatic afferent fibers; its taste and skin fibers run caudally into the solitary and spinal trigeminal tracts. The chorda tympani and greater petrosal nerve are peripheral branches that carry some of these fibers, not the root at the brainstem. The facial genu is the loop of motor axons around the abducens nucleus. The vestibular root belongs to cranial nerve VIII.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intermediate nerve (nervus intermedius): taste, lacrimal and submandibular/sublingual secretomotor, and a little ear skin sensation; facial nerve proper: muscles of facial expression. COMLEX: Lesions in the internal acoustic meatus affect both roots, so loss of taste and tearing accompanies the facial weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — medullary nuclei as relay stations, here the solitary and spinal trigeminal nuclei that receive the intermediate nerve","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has an isolated lesion that spares all facial touch and joint-position input but abolishes facial pinprick and temperature sensation on the same side, with mild ipsilateral jaw-closure weakness.\nWhich trigeminal components are damaged together to produce this pattern?","choices":{"A":"spinal trigeminal and motor nuclei","B":"principal sensory nucleus alone","C":"mesencephalic nucleus alone","D":"trigeminal ganglion alone","E":"anterior trigeminothalamic tract alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Facial pain and temperature sensation travel via the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract, while mastication strength depends on the adjacent trigeminal motor nucleus, so combined damage to these two components produces exactly this pattern of lost pain and temperature sensation with mild jaw weakness while touch and proprioception, carried by the principal sensory and mesencephalic nuclei, remain intact. Damage to the principal sensory nucleus alone would impair touch rather than pain and temperature, damage to the mesencephalic nucleus alone would impair jaw proprioception without affecting pain, temperature, or motor strength, damage to the peripheral ganglion would produce combined loss of all modalities rather than this dissociated pattern, and the anterior trigeminothalamic tract, which carries crossed pain and temperature from the spinal trigeminal nucleus, would produce contralateral rather than ipsilateral facial loss and would not explain the jaw weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A dissociated pattern of facial pain and temperature loss with preserved touch and added jaw weakness localizes to combined spinal trigeminal and trigeminal motor nucleus damage. COMLEX: Bedside testing of facial pinprick against light touch can localize a lesion to the spinal trigeminal tract specifically, distinct from the principal sensory nucleus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — identifying the spinal trigeminal nucleus as a cranial nerve relay, its dissociated sensory role explored further here alongside the trigeminal motor nucleus","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q09","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Localize cranial nerve nuclei and fibers associated with the pons: V, VI, VII, and VIII.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man sustains a small lesion in the caudal pontine tegmentum located immediately beneath the facial colliculus.\n- Complete inability to abduct the ipsilateral eye past midline\n- Complete ipsilateral facial paralysis, from involvement of the looping fibers passing directly over this same nucleus\n- Facial sensation intact, hearing intact\nWhich structure was primarily damaged to produce the eye finding?","choices":{"A":"facial motor nucleus","B":"principal sensory nucleus","C":"superior vestibular nucleus","D":"dorsal cochlear nucleus","E":"abducens nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The abducens nucleus occupies the most medial position in the caudal pontine tegmentum, directly beneath the facial colliculus, and its damage produces the ipsilateral eye abduction failure described; the facial paralysis results not from primary facial motor nucleus damage but from involvement of the internal genu, the looping intrapontine facial fibers that pass directly over the abducens nucleus. The facial motor nucleus itself lies more ventrolaterally and, if primarily damaged, would not by itself explain the eye finding. The principal sensory nucleus governs facial touch, the superior vestibular nucleus governs balance, and the dorsal cochlear nucleus governs hearing, none of which are affected here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The abducens nucleus's medial position beneath the facial colliculus, with the facial nerve looping directly over it, is the anatomic basis for combined CN VI and CN VII findings from one small caudal pontine lesion. COMLEX: This same anatomy explains why isolated CN VI palsy without facial involvement instead suggests a more peripheral fascicular, rather than nuclear, lesion site.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — nuclear versus fascicular cranial nerve lesion distinctions, extended here to the abducens nucleus and its overlying facial genu","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":3,"stem":"In the central part of the caudal pontine tegmentum, the most medial of a row of structures that also includes the superior olivary nucleus, the facial motor nucleus, and the spinal trigeminal tract is a bundle whose major component runs from the red nucleus of the midbrain to the inferior olive of the medulla. This bundle is the ___","choices":{"A":"medial longitudinal fasciculus","B":"central tegmental tract","C":"lateral lemniscus","D":"rubrospinal tract","E":"medial lemniscus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The central portion of the caudal pontine tegmentum contains, from medial to lateral, the central tegmental tract, the superior olivary nucleus, the facial motor nucleus, and the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus. A major part of the central tegmental tract consists of rubro-olivary fibers coursing from the red nucleus to the inferior olive. The medial longitudinal fasciculus lies near the midline in the floor of the fourth ventricle. The lateral lemniscus carries ascending auditory fibers. The rubrospinal tract descends from the red nucleus to the spinal cord in the anterolateral tegmentum, and the medial lemniscus forms the border with the basilar pons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Caudal pontine central tegmentum, medial to lateral: central tegmental tract, superior olive, facial motor nucleus, spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus. COMLEX: The central tegmental tract links the red nucleus with the inferior olive, part of the circuit implicated when palatal tremor follows a brainstem lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing tracts within brainstem cross-sections, extended here to the central tegmental tract of the pons","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q11","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":3,"stem":"A stroke neurologist explains to a resident that a small paramedian basilar pons infarct classically produces contralateral hemiparesis without any accompanying hemianesthesia, because one specific pathway occupies the basilar pons rather than the tegmentum.\nWhich pathway explains this pure motor pattern?","choices":{"A":"medial lemniscus tract","B":"anterolateral system tract","C":"corticospinal tract","D":"spinocerebellar tract","E":"trigeminothalamic tract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The corticospinal tract descends through the basilar pons, broken into fascicles by the interspersed pontine nuclei and pontocerebellar fibers, so an isolated paramedian basilar pons infarct damages this motor pathway alone, sparing the sensory pathways of the tegmentum and producing pure motor hemiparesis. The medial lemniscus and anterolateral system both travel through the tegmentum rather than the basilar pons, so their involvement would instead produce sensory loss, and the spinocerebellar and trigeminothalamic tracts likewise occupy tegmental positions unrelated to this purely motor presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The corticospinal tract's isolated course through the basilar pons, separate from all tegmental sensory pathways, is the anatomic basis for the classic pure motor lacunar pontine stroke. COMLEX: Recognizing a pure motor hemiparesis without sensory loss helps localize a small vessel pontine infarct to the paramedian basilar pons specifically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract within medullary cross-sections as it continues into the pyramid, traced here one level caudal to its fragmented course through the basilar pons","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl17_bloom_q12","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how the corticospinal, corticobulbar, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, trigeminal, and cerebellar pathways pass through the pons.","batch":3,"stem":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus runs near the midline in the floor of the fourth ventricle through the entire pons. Its fiber composition changes along its length: at the pons-medulla junction it contains mainly descending fibers, whereas in the rostral pons it is made up primarily of ___","choices":{"A":"ascending fibers","B":"pontocerebellar fibers","C":"corticospinal fibers","D":"rubro-olivary fibers","E":"trigeminothalamic fibers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus occupies a characteristic position near the midline in the floor of the fourth ventricle; at the pons-medulla junction it contains mainly descending fibers, and in the rostral pons it is made up primarily of ascending fibers, which carry vestibular and abducens-related signals toward the oculomotor nuclei. Pontocerebellar fibers cross in the basilar pons to the middle cerebellar peduncle. Corticospinal fibers run in the basilar pons. Rubro-olivary fibers travel in the central tegmental tract, and trigeminothalamic fibers ascend with the medial lemniscus region.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The MLF links the abducens and vestibular nuclei to the oculomotor nucleus; its lesion causes internuclear ophthalmoplegia. COMLEX: A young adult with bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia should be evaluated for multiple sclerosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — tracing pathways through the medulla, applied here to the changing fiber content of the medial longitudinal fasciculus in the pons","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}]};
